window.FINDINGS={"meta":{"total_sources":105,"total_survey":96,"total_qual":9,"qual_breakdown":"7 interviews, 1 staff focus group, 1 partner focus group","threats_max":96,"report_version":"v6"},"prompts":["On the values","What Resonate does well","Why I'm still here","What I wish was better","How we can improve","10-year vision","Practical next steps","External threats","For the pastor search","Heart of who we are","Other thoughts"],"quotes":[["On the values","We are in the Word every message on Sundays. The Two Yr reading plan is often mentioned and having the plan on Dwell makes it easier to engage and stay up with.\nWed classes dive deep into scripture, even though I haven't be involved in one recently I have enjoyed the time when I was. There are lots of invitations to serve via slack and Sunday morning announcements. LGs doing things together and challenging people to take leadership roles in planning events or loving those outside the church. We support many (in my opinion) missionary units but we also have so many people focused on bring the KoG through their business and professional avenues. You don't have to go somewhere else to serve, people talk about being obedient where you are currently at and being faithful to God's call on your life in the present."],["On the values","Everybody plays- it seems like the same people are serving regularly but not everyone is on a serving team."],["On the values","Resonate has always been so centered in scripture, the sermons are line by line verse by verse. I cherish that so much. It's to the point where when someone preaches not out of scripture my alarm bells go off. In a world where constant opinion and noise are coming out of every mouth- it's so nice to see how Resonate speaks through the word. All initiatives and programs seem to be backed by book and verse.\nI see that so many people play in volunteering, service, and fellowship. One area that I have seen less of this though is life groups. These are built to be places of commitment where everyone contributes- time, money, and resources. This is a mixed bag in my experience.\nEverybody sent certainly is lived out in local outreach and our robust missions partnerships. It's a hard thing to quantify in a incredibly reached place like Atlanta. But I think there is a lot heart here."],["On the values","We have always had a strong Word-centered church at Resonate. The teaching on Sunday continues to be exegetical. Both the children's and adult Bible studies are well attended and dive deep into the Scriptures. Life groups are encouraged to discuss Scripture in community when we gather.\n\nI feel like our community does a good job of volunteering and participating. There is some room for improvement here. I see it particularly in Resonate kids on Sundays, but this is a common struggle for churches and I think we do a pretty good job. I would like to see us have greater participation as a church when it comes to events with prayer.\nEverybody Sent is definitely our weakest value - although I would say we generally agree with the idea that we are all sent to proclaim Jesus in our comings and goings. If the main evidence for this is visitors and baptisms at church, then I think my ranking is accurate. We could have this mindset and be having the conversations in our daily interactions, but not experience that kind of fruit from it."],["On the values","I believe we are definitely word-centered as experienced in every service when whoever is teaching says, \"open your Bible to...\" I feel that not everyone plays. I have been at Resonate for 8 years and my family has been highly-involved, from volunteering consistently to Lifegroups and other things. Yet I feel like I don't know a lot of the folks attending church and I don't see them outside of services. I honestly don't feel like folks, myself included, feel like a missionary in our every day lives. It's a bit of a nuance using that word (missionaries) which has a specific connotation, which can be appropriate, but doesn't seem to resonate. Perhaps there is a more appropriate word-purpose? intention? Respectfully, I think the word \"mission\" can have negative connotations given larger Christian church history."],["On the values","The word is very center at Resonate. So thankful there aren't Sundays or other teaching times that do not include digging deep into the word.\nEverybody Plays- I think the major majority of our people are involved and serving.\nEverybody Sent- I love how seriously our people take this. From Decatur and beyond, our people love their neighbors and are a light."],["On the values","Sermons and lifegroup content pulled directly from scripture.\nI'm struggling to think of many (if any) members/households who don't have at least one person involved.\nI think collectively we could do better living out a missional lifestyle in our everyday."],["On the values","Our sermons and life groups are constantly drawing wisdom from The Word as we go which is amazing. I scored us lower on the Everybody plays portion because it seems like we have a set of regulars doing most of the Sunday volunteering and often times the children’s area can have challenges finding other volunteers if a regular calls out.\n\nWe are becoming an integral part of Belvedere park and it is amazing to see the work that’s already been done."],["On the values","Our sermons and weekly bible studies have been consistently word centered. I see a lot of people, new and old, serving on a weekly basis. But when it come to living missionally, I would says this is the area that could use the most growth. We have some VERY servant hearted partners. People who carry the worlds burdens and care for the lost. But I think we can grow in being more bold about actually sharing the good news of the gospel and speaking the name of Jesus with them."],["On the values","I tend to see a solid rotation of faces at the door, serving communion, helping with kids etc and I don’t think I’ve ever been in the building without hearing some kind of scripture. For everyday missionaries I hear the occasional story at the beginning of a service but in my own life and what I see in others there is mostly a focus on the everyday tasks of life and less on seeking mission opportunities"],["On the values","It can be hard to get plugged in."],["On the values","Resonate has a very strong foundation in teaching scripture/ the word. It is great strong point for the church.\nEverybody plays needs the most work. Being a regular volunteer myself, it feels like majority of the church is not all participating in stepping up in different ministries and the work tends to fall on people that are regular volunteers. I feel that if you are a partner you need to be actively participating.\nResonate does great with emphasis on intentional missional living."],["On the values","As an expository church, being Word-centered is constant and consistent. It is embedded enough that I think many expect and depend upon it as a way of teaching and how Resonate works. I think our church puts a strong emphasis on 'doing' and always has, but I think our congregation being largely of one age group has seen people go through collective burnout with serving and involvement in the church."],["On the values","God's word and principles and character underscore our sermons and interactions, goals, etc. I have seen where it seems fewer people serve than are at least attendees or partners. This church seems to thrive on and live out the gospel mission in their everyday lives, whether here or abroad, and those here support those abroad with joy."],["On the values","Sunday Assembly, Bible Classes and LifeGroups emphasize Biblical teaching as well as any fellowship I’ve ever attended.\n\nMy assessment of everybody Plays & Sent is uncertain due to short duration of Resonate connection, complicated by unfamiliarity with majority of people due to two services and inability to attend multiple LifeGroups."],["On the values","Word Centered- teaching is directly from the Bible and feels like the focus Everybody plays/sent - I can only speak for my family here so I scored in the middle.\nGenerally, it seems we all try to participate and support the church as partners. I believe our community is aiming to live on mission in their everyday lives."],["On the values","Scripture is definitely at the core of the messages. I put a four on the other two because, outside of my life group (and now some connections via the mens retreat), I'm not sure how truly participating and \"mission\" oriented everyone in the church is. That's not a reflection of the church, but more of a reflection of me and my lack of availability to know the church better. I will say, leadership, and the clear \"regulars\" do exhibit those qualities."],["On the values","We are deep in the Word each week. We also are running through the 2 year bible program with our Life Groups.\nI think we mean to do this, but it's very self start. I understand the intention behind that but many people lack confidence or even perhaps the willingness to lean in to start something new.\nI'm not sure about this. But I don't hear about it aside from Brandon, Noah, Missionaries leaving so I'm assuming it's less?"],["On the values","Sermons are scripture based. I always appreciated Chris's analytical view of the scripture."],["On the values","The sermons and conversations in lifegroup always focus on scripture. I think that is done really well by all who lead here.\nI agree that the church as a whole believes “everybody plays”, but there are some who don’t."],["On the values","Several areas/programs are hard pressed to get volunteers"],["On the values","I have never once felt Resonate made a decision without consulting Scripture and praying. There is always a Scriptural reason for why it does what it does and knowing some of the staff personally, I know they take Scripture very seriously. I also appreciate the past teachings, most often verse by verse, all of which has been Scripture-led teaching. On Sundays, I can look around at the different individuals and families and tell\n\nyou what area of church they serve in - everyone seems to be serving in one area of church or another. And lastly, I continue to be amazed at getting to know people at resonate better - I hear so many stories of people serving non-believers and having important relationships with them."],["On the values","I think Resonate does a great job at having Sunday sermons centered around the word and not just feelings or even the political and/or world climate. It centers around teaching scripture and teaching those who want to learn how to read, interpret and study scripture.\nResonate does a great job of providing many opportunities for people to actively engage in loving others and communion with those who attend church, those who seek help from the church and just serving at whatever capacity or level the person can commit.\nResonate does a great job of encouraging us to live as missionaries in our everyday lives. I think there is always room for more intentional conversion on how to share the gospel with those around us, how to love well, and make real connections while staying true to the standards of Christ."],["On the values","I feel our sermons are deeply rooting in the word. It's not always and analytical lecture but it's based on the Bible. I can't speak for everyone as I don't know everyone at church, but I see a lot of people who participate in our church and help spread the word of God."],["On the values","I’ve always felt like Resonate with Bible centered. As for everybody plays and sent, I think this is certainly encouraged by Resonate, but it’s up to the individuals to accept these invitations and I know many regular members who aren’t plugged in this way."],["On the values","I feel that Resonate is a church that has high view of Scripture, and a vast majority of people are willing to serve on a regular basis."],["On the values","I think Resonate does an excellent job of staying centered on the Word. I have seen that clearly especially over the last few months. I think Resonate has a strong focus on trying to understand the context and the language and the nuances which helps interpret Scripture more accurately, but also holding interpretations with humility and being Spirit led.\nFor \"Everybody Plays,\" I think most people participate but I feel like that has become less strong over the past few years. We have a core group that are always serving. But I\n\nfeel like we have a larger group that just shows up on Sundays and for Life Group and does not actively participate.\nFor \"Everybody Sent,\" similar to the answer above, I feel like most people live intentionally and seek to be the hands and feet of Jesus but I feel like we have a larger group that just shows up on Sundays when they feel like it and like that they feel challenged but don't let it impact how they live their day to day lives."],["On the values","Word: Sure seems so! I'd hesitate to give a 5 more due to my experience (LOTS of churches who would give THEMSELVES a 5 I would give a 5 to their inherited religious tradition/denomination being even more of a foundation than actually Scripture.) I can only imagine Resonate has some (much?) of that as well… in short, there's always room for improvement.\nPlays: Seems so, also! 3 is a \"check!\" or \"I've seen that!\" but I have very little knowledge to actually go on--I don't know what __% spectators Resonate might have.\nI'm also probably okay WITH certain sorts of spectating for seasons, certainly more so than in some volunteer-generation machines I've seen.\nSent: I just don't know enough about this. Since I have to mark, I'll give it a 5 to indicate \"no critique.\""],["On the values","I have always felt that Resonate if word centered both on Sundays and outside of Sundays. I think this is something that Resonate has and continues to do well. The other two were rated in the middle because while I think these values are spoken of regularly and encouraged, I do not personally see these things being pushed if people are okay living a more nomial life. It is of course possible that these things happen in life groups in ways that I do not observe. I do think many people at Resonate do these things very well and with consistency."],["On the values","I think our church is solid in biblical preaching and the majority of the people are active participants in serving Christ"],["On the values","I am grateful to see the practical and discussed ways church influences everyday lives.\nScripture as a value during teaching seems diminished after losing a singular teaching pastor. Everyone plays seems average in execution, as people volunteer but not certain there is a greater since if community or connection to purpose in that."],["On the values","I love the way we engage Scripture -- from Sunday sermons, to Wed classes, and the 2 year Bible Reading plan, it's entrenched in all we do.\n\nOur church is generally very connected. There is space for people to feel out the waters before they dive in, but I celebrate that as well. Brent, Noah, and Danielle do an amazing job recruiting and developing leaders.\nI'm not sure the gospel has transformed many in our church significantly enough that they live as \"sent\" -- deeply love and moved by Jesus in such a way that they cannot help but testify about him. Social justice is a high value at Resonate, but we often miss the gospel in it, for fear of offense or insecurity in how it's shared. That said, I would imagine that Resonate still does this much better than most established churches."],["On the values","I love that we prioritize expository preaching on Sunday mornings. This is really important to me and one of the primary reasons we started attending Resonate. I have not done Wednesday bible studies in a few years, but I appreciate that we offer those so consistently.\nI know that everybody plays and everybody is sent are values of the church. I hear it talked about from the stage, and I see specific programming opportunities that support these values (Serve Sunday and Outreach Hour and consistently talking about these values from the stage)."],["On the values","I think there is a core of the church who live this way but I don’t know that we’ve done a great job envisioning these values to all of our newer folks."],["On the values","Word Centered - As staff I am aware of how much Scripture is woven into our meetings as well as our decision making process. We are expository in our teaching which puts Scripture at the forefront.\nEverybody Plays - We have a high number of people in our church who serve and pour into our community in a variety of ways. I gave a 4 because there is still enough of a percentage that do not participate in that way and giving it a 5 would've felt inaccurate.\nEverybody Sent - I'd probably put this at more 3.5. A good portion of our church is outwardly focused and I celebrate that but since people vary on where they are on their journey I felt liked a 3.5 was more accurate to where our body was as a whole. A good way to gauge this core value is based off how many unbelievers each person at Resonate knows and their level of intention towards them."],["On the values","I think we have been consitent about being Word centered without tons of self-help, sensational, or culture centered teaching. Resonate does a great job at encouraging people to participate. I love this about Resonate. I see us less focused on being everyday missionaries but it's there and I see an increasing emphasis on it."],["On the values","The Sunday sermon series, adult education classes, and Life Groups are structured around passages of scripture. The church at large is filled with people who earnestly desire to help wherever needed; it's encouraging how quickly the community responds to needs in the Slack channels. I think the culture of mission is in the DNA of Resonate church with key individuals as the point persons and storytelling during Sunday programming as a way to highlight more hidden examples of everyday moments and connecting it to the \"why\" of their lives."],["On the values","Word centered seemed really straight forward and easy to evaluate because it seems specific to how Sunday morning teaching, Bible studies, and Lifegroups run. The other two with “everybody” feels harder to judge. Some do, some don’t. But maybe some of that is seasonal and how it should be?"],["On the values","I feel like we're doing really well at keeping scripture as the centerpiece of teaching and being expository. I see a diverse group helping in all aspects of church processes. The only reason I would score the 'everybody sent' a 4 instead of a 5 is that I know that I (and probably others) don't live as a missionary in my everyday life, but am desiring to go in that direction."],["On the values","We are a solid expository preaching church. When I look around at ministries, there is a solid core of people serving but we need more."],["On the values","Very limited experience. I’m deeply encouraged by the staff and partners at Resonate.\nNoah is a good friend. I’ve gone to the men’s retreat the past few years. I see a deep level of urgency and intentionality with vulnerability. The challenges Resonate faces is that its congregation makeup (middle class) doesn’t reflect the context in which the church is located (Belvedere Park). I know there is outreach hour but there seemingly could be more done to engage the community in deeper ways."],["On the values","I know there are many areas to study and receive the word. I am less sure on how everyone lives that out."],["On the values","I think the teaching, life groups, and classes are centered around scripture, which is great. I also think there's a great culture of encouraging people to participate and start new initiatives. I think everybody sent is a bit harder to embody day-to-day. I think everyone wants to be good news to the people around them, but it's not always clear how."],["On the values","In my observations and experience at Resonate and life groups, Scripture is the foundation - there may be different ways that teaching may be presented, but whether\n\non Sundays mornings or Wednesdays evenings, Scripture is foundational and at the heart of all teaching. Scripture is also the foundation of all the trellis spiritual formation practices. Of all the various churches I have attended over my life, I feel that spiritual formation and teaching has been the most clear and Scripture-based here at Resonate.\nAlso, from a practical standpoint, I feel like all partners at Resonate really look at how they can participate in serving on Sunday or throughout the week. I do think a larger proportion of people are actively engaged and participating here compared to other churches I have been a member of.\nI also think it's easy to look around and see how everyone lives missionally - in their family, in their community, at school or work. I see this all around me and I think in life groups, we see and encourage it in each other."],["On the values","At least as a church community, Scripture is a central part of almost everything we do, from Sunday morning worship gatherings and kids classes, to midweek Bible studies and youth group, to Life Groups, where discussion of Scripture is a key component.\nAlongside all that, individuals are constantly encouraged to engage in the Trellis and the 2-Year Bible. Even outreach hour has a Bible study. Of all the values, this one seems most evident in practice.\nEverybody Plays is also quite evident. I can't actually THINK of any partners who don't participate in service somehow, and even a lot of regular attendees who aren't partners serve in some way. But I have to imagine there is room for growth. Maybe the growth is more in the amount/quality of service versus a simple binary of yes/no, do they ever server in some capacity.\nEverybody Sent—this one is harder to gauge. I do see many partners/attendees living this out, but I would say that's more like half or so. This is arguably the hardest one to live out, so it's not surprising that some of our people are not there yet. but it's probably where we need the most growth, to be discipling them toward that."],["On the values","My experience at Resonate has been a refreshing one. Coming from a very large church, I see that almost everyone who is in the church contributes and participates in some form. I don’t recall a moment when it felt like anyone was an spectator."],["On the values","I scored 4 on Word Centered because we could do this a little better, but of course it's difficult because it's dependent on people to weave that focus into casual conversations as much as the official teaching/study/LifeGroup times.\n\nI would guess while we have very high service/volunteer rates, there are still regular attenders who don't serve and are more spectators.\nThere's still a gap for many, conceptualizing their non-church work and personal lives as mission fields."],["On the values","Word Centered - self evident in the regular teachings and as the stated basis for spiritual foundation.\nEverybody Plays - unknown to me how everyone plays and there are no 'spectators'.\nEverybody Sent -unknown how it's evident that everyone lives as missionaries in their everyday lives."],["On the values","The teaching is consistently grounded and focused in Scripture.\nResonate has excellent participation. From my understanding of the last two questions, everyone would need to be engaged for these to be 5's and while we have high engagement; 100% probably would never be realistic."],["On the values","Resonate is very word centered. In everything we do, we try and point it to scripture.\nPartners and long-term attenders are consistently involved. Whether it is serving on a Sunday morning or providing meals for a meal train for a family or Outreach Hour to donating for various service projects (Embrace, Serve Sunday), people are engaged and wanting to be a part.\nI ranked Everybody Sent as 3 because while we are a thriving community to each other, I don't always see the outward missionary heart. I include myself in this. It is easy to love our church community, but I think we could do a better job of loving those in our everyday lives more. This includes inviting people to LG and actually making space for them rather than filling groups and cutting it off to new folks."],["On the values","I thought about how often I see these things promoted and happen in practice"],["On the values","I have been attending resonate only for four months so my answer is only based on my short observations. Having said that, I don’t see everyone, actively participating in disciple making events except Sunday services. I don’t know everyone enough, but I could see the actively serving group and actively receiving group."],["On the values","Primarily on the “everyone plays” bullet, I’ve noticed a lot of people will call out from their responsibility to serve which overburdens other people. I know some things come up and life happens, but it seems like this happens at a higher rate than what I would\n\nexpect. In my opinion, if you commit to something, you should stick to that commitment if at all possible."],["On the values","It could be my season in life or that there’s more staff to do the work but I feel less involved in the needs of the church. Also you kinda have to be close to a leader in the church to get a pulse on opportunities, it seems."],["On the values","I see all of these areas being carried out well. There are so many hands that are working towards these goals it feels inspiring."],["On the values","The teaching and preaching, from Wednesday Bible Studies to Resonate Kids/Shout to Sunday morning sermons, are all centered and anchored in the Bible, hence, the 5. I fluctuated between 3 and 4 for \"Everybody Plays,\" because I think there are varying degrees of service and participation. I also recognize that I might not see how many people are actually participating. \"Everybody Sent\" - I hesitated on this one as well, bc, if I'm just looking at the people in my LG and the people I'm closest to at Resonate, I'd say 5! And, even amongst people I don't know as well, I hear stories about them faithfully serving and sharing the gospel in their life contexts. But again, I don't know everyone, so my view might be skewed."],["On the values","Living across the world makes some of these “now” hard to answer but thinking back to teaching, service, and sending, Resonate was living these values out so faithfully a year ago from my perspective, and I imagine this continues maybe especially in a difficult season when roots are being examined. I’ve been personally blessed and challenged by Word centered, serving, sharing people at Resonate."],["On the values","For Word Centered, I feel we are consistently making the gospel our center point in what we do and what we teach.\nFor Everyone plays, I feel resonate has a strong participation in volunteering and service by the body, but could be more.\nFor Sent, its hard to have a good sense of this, but based on what i hear from the body, I would say that about 2/3 to 50% of the church probably live out their faith with mission in mind on a daily/weekly basis."],["On the values","We have have always been a church that prioritizes scripture and community. We are a community who loves to serve and find their place. I think that while we have folks in our community with a very clear calling to be missionaries in their everyday lives, I think people feel more comfortable with the other two through lines. We talk about being missionaries in our everyday lives alot, but do people actually feel equipped? I am not\n\nsure, but I would imagine that people who are self-starters would say they know how to be on mission. Other thought I think may have a harder time because they need help discerning and stepping through that."],["On the values","I believe with the vacuum of no lead pastor, the organization, rhythm, and clarity seems off-balance and unsure. This presents itself as a little weak. We are praying for clarity for the leadership and a boldness to without fear to move forward in God's plan."],["On the values","Word Centered: I feel like both on Sundays and during the week there is an emphasis on studying and dissecting scripture.\nEverybody Plays: I think that we do this extremely well. Pretty much everyone I know is involved in some way in church life and encourages others to do the same.\nEverybody Sent: I answered 3 on this one because I'm not sure what's going on in a lot of people's lives. It's hard to know how well folks are living on mission without having more in-depth conversations."],["On the values","Teachings are consistently scripture based, but some teachings feel scattered/hard to follow.\nI witness what seems to be the majority of the church serving on Sundays, let alone what I don’t see during the week.\nPeople seem engaged with bettering the world and lives around them with the love of Jesus."],["On the values","For value 3, I don't think we spend enough time equipping our church body to live sent."],["On the values","Resonate oozes the Word of God in many ways - via semester-based Bible studies, via Sunday sermons (and kids classes), when designating priorities, and by making decisions. Major decisions are made by seeking the Word and writing position papers to reflect how Scripture guided the decision, which I deeply appreciate. Regarding \"Everybody Sent\" - I have been a member of several churches throughout my life, and none of them embrace the missionary mindset as well as Resonate. By featuring a different missionary speaker at church on Sunday mornings (whether local, global, etc.), we hear firsthand about the many journeys taking place within our church, and it's truly beautiful to witness. Regarding \"Everybody Plays,\" it seems like most people are involved in supporting the church in one way or another, although there are some who seem to be more of a spectator than a participant. Resonate occasionally mentions\n\nareas of need (e.g., kids classroom teachers) but could perhaps emphasize the importance of participation a little more."],["On the values","You guys do a great job deep diving into scripture every Sunday, and you encourage the congregation to engage with others, share the love of Christ, and bring it into your daily life."],["On the values","I think the church does a great job of staying grounded and rooted in the Word. Also, I think the church does a great job of encouraging others to participate in events and in serving the church through their gifts, resources, and talents. However, I'm unclear at times of how the church is equipping people to be sent as missionaries. This portion seems more unclear to me"],["On the values","I feel like the biblical basis is fairly strong, tough is day tradition comes through a bit more in both the partnership packet and services given the string emphasis on creeds and liturgical recitation mother of which directly quote it reference Scripture.\nI'm honestly too new to have a good handle on everyone plays and everyone sent.\nEveryone is definitely invited through many opportunities to serve and support the ministry of the church though."],["On the values","Resonate gives great opportunities to study God’s word and has shown its decision making backed by their interpretation of God’s Word. There is a good community of helpers in our church keeping us going. The spotlights on Sunday are good reminders of how people are trying to live our their faith in the every day."],["On the values","I have threes to all three, because 5 is the best of the best, and it’s a lofty goal to attain.\nI’ve been to John McArthurs church in LA, that would be a 5, he was a great teacher.\nResonate has a lot of young believers that know scripture really well, but with Chris leaving and not having a lead pastor, wouldn’t be able to score the church a 4.\nEveryone plays, I agree with. 52 men were at the retreat, but I was the only one in my LG there. I’ve consistently had a difficult time as a LG leader having everyone participate and show up for LG on a weekly basis. We rarely meet outside of LG during the week. For the kids ministry the same people every week are the ones that teach and recently there has been a few instances where a children’s class was not able to happen due to a lack of volunteers. Everybody sent, I’ve seen obvious examples of people people being sent out of the church. These are extraordinary folks, but I can not score it a 4, because the church is in Belvedere, yet it is difficult to serve the community the\n\nchurch is when a lot of the folks are in Stone Mountain Clarkston or Tucker. To be clear, 3 is not an average score, and it’s not a bad score. 5 is exceptional with no issues."],["On the values","We definitely do have spectators, but there are many faithful people who serve and participate in important roles.\nMany see themselves as sent in their everyday lives, but maybe the same amount don't know what that would look like."],["What Resonate does well","People being willing to open their homes And hearts. And to have honest conversations about what is happening in life."],["What Resonate does well","COMMUNITY. LifeGroups, connection and relationships outside of Sundays and Wednesdays is incredible. Our focus on family discipleship, hyper-local outreach, the way we support global partners, all incredible."],["What Resonate does well","Being a small, welcoming environment, especially for families."],["What Resonate does well","Strong teaching on Sundays. Welcoming of whoever walks in the door. Engaged in the community."],["What Resonate does well","Welcoming people who are different- whether that is a physical difference, socioeconomic difference, etc.\nI also appreciate how the church leaders seem comfortable with questions and doubts."],["What Resonate does well","Pure religion exercised by adoptive/foster parents & congregational support.\nFrequent Assembly presentations by lay persons of Christian living practices.\nOutreach meals at building, Service Day, and LifeGroups Superlative staff"],["What Resonate does well","I love that after service, people linger to talk to one another. It doesn’t feel like just a Sunday thing - people at resonate are genuinely in each other’s lives. I’ve never experienced a church like it before. It’s also incredibly welcoming!"],["What Resonate does well","Inclusivity, Holy Spirit's presence, location, Local community support,"],["What Resonate does well","Resonate is an awesome place for young families. It feels like a place you can come to without judgement or being a mess. The preaching is always Word centered. We LOVE having women in positions of leadership. We love the diversity. We love that it doesn’t feel like a creepy MAGA church."],["What Resonate does well","Diverse church partners, intentional about missions, allowing women to have a voice in the church"],["What Resonate does well","The welcoming spirit I have received and the way the scripture is delivered"],["What Resonate does well","Resonate is great at including everyone and building genuine relationships."],["What Resonate does well","I think we do well with community, intentionality, service, and theology."],["What Resonate does well","The leadership posture of inclusiveness and diversity of multicultural neighbors.\nThe application of illustrating that each message may resonate differently with individuals which is an acknowledgement if how we are all at different stages of our journey(spiritual formation- sanctification)"],["What Resonate does well","We welcome others well and help them feel seen and valued. I think it is difficult for a new person/family to come through our doors without feeling seen. We also do community well as a high percentage of our body is connected into a Life Group and are continuing to step into deeper connection, vulnerability, and trust. We should be proud of the teachers the Lord has placed in our body and how we emphasize Scripture. Our Staff and leaders model what it means to follow Jesus."],["What Resonate does well","Resonate is one of the most genuine churches that I have attended . I appreciate how they approach the gospel in a realistic way and are honest and transparent about struggling and how it’s not going to always play out perfect. They strive to be aware of different demographics/cultures and incorporate them into worship. Resonate reaches out locally and serve the community in practical ways. The leadership team is amazing!"],["What Resonate does well","Outreach to the houseless population right in our area.\nSupporting overseas missions.\nLifegroup structures are fantastic.\nDiverse opportunities for involvement.\nElders/leadership has really stepped up and been a light."],["What Resonate does well","We have ways of connecting with the community around us. We have a great program for kids. We are ok with grey areas and questions but want to proclaim the gospel and walk out scriptural truths. Life groups are great for connecting and being the body and walking through life with people."],["What Resonate does well","One place where Resonate shines is in caring for one another. When a tangible need is shared, others rush to come in and support/fill that need. I think that our church is a very welcoming place. It's easy to come in and find someone who will chat with you and be genuinely curious about why you walked through the church doors."],["What Resonate does well","Resonate is extremely organized and knows how to mobilize people to serve each other and the community. It is a church that cares for the outsiders. I would say Resonate"],["What Resonate does well","We acknowledge the good and the bad, what we don't know, that we don't know. We are becoming more and more diverse in race and age. We are here together for each other."],["What Resonate does well","We live in deep, authentic community and we engage with the lost. We are passionate about protecting vulnerable populations and loving all people uniquely created in the image of God. We seek to know and live out God's Word."],["What Resonate does well","1. Resonate staff and elders live out the Word of God very well - they are genuine role models. Even with Chris Case's recent actions and sudden departure, Resonate staff and elders handled it all by seeking God first, showing grace and understanding to Chris, and ultimately making a hard, yet necessary decision to let Chris go."],["What Resonate does well","Sermons and Bible studies not only teach us the Word of God but give us rich context about the passages' background, setting, characters, etc. They also crossreference related Scriptures, enriching our understanding. In short, Resonate has a more academic-style teaching that makes us unique and equips people with a deep knowledge of God."],["What Resonate does well","Resonate is very outward-focused: on our local community and global community.\nResonate does a good job with equipping missionaries and making a beautiful impact in Belvedere Park, Atlanta and Clarkston, and the greater world."],["What Resonate does well","Welcoming, friendly, genuine community. Offerings for people to get plugged in quick if they want. Serving our neighbors."],["What Resonate does well","I do think we are standing out especially right now for supporting the body together, and showing that the church is more than just Sunday teaching. Figuring out, and sustaining life groups over multiple years also seems like a special thing to me."],["What Resonate does well","There are many characteristics… 1. Resonate focuses on the gospel of Jesus… that justification is by faith alone (and not by any works of the/a Law). 2. Resonate practices being inclusive of people from different faith backgrounds - i.e., it promotes being a “centered” church. 3. Etc."],["What Resonate does well","Discipleship, Community."],["What Resonate does well","Being rooted in the word, spiritual disciplines"],["What Resonate does well","Great at being Gospel and Word centered and less about cultural applications.\nLeadership encourages people to be involved."],["What Resonate does well","We think (intellectual) but we also cry (emotional). This is at front of my since we just had the men's retreat. We have so many smart people in our body that desire to encounter God through study, theology, and asking technical questions. While we could lean more into the mystical side of God we also engage our emotions and allow space for sorrow, lament, and critical questions."],["What Resonate does well","Engaging more with the neighborhood through outreach hour. reevaluating convictions based on scripture"],["What Resonate does well","So proud of our thoughtful teaching, commitment to care, dedication to some repeated practices, and realistic about our approach, emphasizing other ministries or certified tools like therapy rather than absorbing them to the church."],["What Resonate does well","Teaching and Life groups. The sunday sermons and weekly adjacent learning opportunies are solid.\nThough I'm not in a Life Group, I am close to many who are and know that they have great impact."],["What Resonate does well","Everyone is welcome without compromising doctrine or theology."],["What Resonate does well","Life groups, connecting, bible centered sermons."],["What Resonate does well","Community"],["What Resonate does well","- Focus on Scripture. We care about things that Scripture tells us to care about.\n- Diversity and a focus on inclusion - Life Groups and their high participation rates - Our intention to engage with and be a part of the Belvedere Park community."],["What Resonate does well","I think people do really great job of caring for one another. I see a lot of intentionality to be there for one another in the highs and lows. Also, the \"everybody plays\" culture has led to the church being well rounded in the ministries it engages in. I like that we are finding ways to care for those in need locally, serve globally, engage in foster care and adoption, and many other things."],["What Resonate does well","The greatest strength I see is (1) how the church leadership seeks to be Transparent and humble in all ways. People are very genuine and Christ-seeking. The worship is very\n\nbalanced between the scripture and the lead of the holy spirit. The sermon is very biblical."],["What Resonate does well","a community with a heart for God"],["What Resonate does well","Community for those who want it. We do global outreach and mission really well -supporting our missionaries and prioritizing the unreached. We are growing in our outreach -- Serve Sundays are great, but Outreach Hour is growing as well. I believe that our Spiritual Formation and intimacy with Christ is growing among those who desire grown in these areas. Generally, we are a church that is faithful to Christ and open to change and growth. I'm amazed at how adaptable the church has been to changes we've made over the years."],["What Resonate does well","Resonate’s focus on community and being Word-centered is what draws me to the church the most. From the beginning, we have done community really well, and I appreciate that deeply. Certainly, we have the opportunity to grow in that as the congregation becomes increasingly diverse, but it still blows me away when people step up to serve their brother and sister with meals, rides, and other support.\nCommunity is the number one thing I say when I talk to others about Resonate.\nWord-centered is very important to me as I want my faith and life to be grounded in God’s truth. Exegetical teaching, Bible study for all ages, deep consideration of the Word in decision-making, pointing life groups to be in the Word, and the 2-year Bible reading plan all showcase the church’s commitment to being Word-centered, and I find that focus strengthening my faith and a blessing to my heart.\nI am proud of our focus on welcoming people of all abilities, supporting families through their foster care and adoption journeys, and moving towards our neighbors in Belevedre Park. I am so glad Noah is leading the efforts for local outreach and love that relationships are being built with our neighbors. I am very encouraged to see that as it feels like something we would not have been so good at ten years ago.\nI also think we do a fantastic job with family discipleship. What started with a toddler and a newborn has now grown to a very full and thriving ministry. I am thrilled with the elementary Bible study offering, the Jesus-centered curriculum we use on Sundays, Noah's leadership of the youth group. I am thankful for Danielle's focus on safety, foster care, and welcoming all abilities. I am also thankful for the folks before that like Jamie Hutchinson and for all of us pitching in and putting up room dividers and trying to\n\nfigure out what teaching young children in a martial arts studio about God looks like.\nWhat a journey."],["What Resonate does well","The church is willing to discuss the nuances of faith particularly as it intersects culture"],["What Resonate does well","Your mission to serve the community and reach the unreached with the gospel is clear and constant. People staying local seek out ways to be involved with unbelievers in our community, schools, and neighborhoods."],["What Resonate does well","Strong missional statement / living out Strong dedication to spiritual disciplines and scripture / prayer at center of sunday worship Strong ability to serve community around the church / church members Strong desire to have a center set church"],["What Resonate does well","BRENT FREAKIN DAVIDSON !\nA openess and kindness to all, and the fact that I never feel pressured at Resonate to be or do anything, I'm just welcomed there and spoken to. That's huge."],["What Resonate does well","Connecting the church community together"],["What Resonate does well","We welcome people well. One of the biggest reasons we kept coming back to Resonate is that we were seen on our first Sunday visiting- we felt like we were wanted.\nIn the weary state we were in at that time, that was what we needed."],["What Resonate does well","The most unique and memorable thing was the call and response portion of service, which I would love to see again even after Chris. This really set the time that service is a participatory activity rather than a show.\nAnother is the way Resonate always welcome visitors and offers to help them find a Bible teaching church even if it is not here. This so beautifully reminds us all each week that this is just one post of a larger body.\nThe last thing that stands out to me is how accepting the church and congregation are of people having different needs whether that's from circumstances, stage of life, developmental or physical."],["What Resonate does well","So many couples that foster/adopt ‘ wonderful!"],["What Resonate does well","So many things! Keeping Jesus as the focal point across differences in theological or practical ways of \"doing\" church. A community that loves to show up and support one another. A mission mindset for local and global outreach. Intentional discipleship for\n\nchildren of all ages. Increased diversity over the years, across multiple demographic characteristics."],["What Resonate does well","Resonate is gospel preaching and loves well."],["What Resonate does well","I love the Christ-centered teaching at all levels - main service, other yea hong and in Resonate kids. My family is nourished.\nWe also do well leaning into missions and local outreach where the Resonate building is."],["What Resonate does well","I feel like our kids are being folded into the community and are valuable members of the church...not seen as just someone to babysit while the grown ups learn and participate."],["What Resonate does well","Community! Transparency. Discussing the hard things authentically. Diversifying intentionally."],["What Resonate does well","We attract people who are serious about growing in their faith and living it out in their daily lives. That was one of the first things that I noticed about Resonate when I first visited. A lot of people involved in various ministries (local or global), the heart for foster/adoption, the attention to inclusion of people with disabilities.\nOur teaching and preaching are strong. It's nuanced, thoughtful, thought provoking, invites questions, vulnerable."],["What Resonate does well","I think Resonate does a good job with community and being welcoming to new visitors.\nI really like the 3 minutes to say hello to one another during transition time. I also think the worship team is very strong and worship feels very powerful."],["What Resonate does well","LifeGroups, the way it is essential to everyone in the church and it plays a vital role in the uniqueness of Resonate."],["What Resonate does well","-Welcome—Resonate works hard to make people feel welcome from the moment they walk in the door.\n-Inclusion—though the church body is still not as diverse as the surrounding area or Atlanta at large, there is a clear intention to be inclusive across a variety of demographics, whether ethnic, people with disabilities, socio-economic class.\n-Whole family discipleship—it feels like there's something to engage people at almost any stage of life -Fostering community/fellowship—Life Groups are a core part of the Resonate experience, and there are other opportunities for fellowship too.\n\n-Teaching/scriptural engagement—the \"Word centered\" value is constantly on display, whether it is Sunday mornings or the deep dives offered in Wednesday night Bible studies.\n-Quality/excellence—the staff and volunteers at Resonate tend to do things well, whether it's the worship service, the greeters and coffee, or children's ministry. Without giving an impression of opulence or waste, there is a sense that Resonate tries to do a good job in whatever it does with the resources it has available. We tend not to do things that we're actually not equipped/resourced to do, not settling for ramshackle/half-hearted efforts. But we also make the most of what we do have available.\n-Commitment to outreach—while we're still finding our feet and gathering resources/volunteers, the commitment and desire to reach our Belvedere neighborhood is very encouraging."],["What Resonate does well","The community is great. I’ve developed friendships with a few people in my life group that have grown over the last few years."],["What Resonate does well","Strong emphasis on Bible-based teaching and decisions, yet careful and nuanced in interpretation and application. Emphasis on loving and serving neighbors, particularly the vulnerable. Intentionally engaging with and encouraging the practice of spiritual disciplines."],["What Resonate does well","Humility, diversity of thought and background, open and oriented to welcoming all family styles (single, married, family, post-kids) and socioeconomic backgrounds"],["What Resonate does well","The expository preaching. Spending months and years in the same book of the Bible because we take our time to go through Scripture and really chew on it. Digging into the context and background and the original language.\nI think also just the environment and welcoming community. Not everyone has that gift, but there are several people who are intentional about noticing new people sitting around them and greeting them and making them feel welcome."],["What Resonate does well","We've handled crisis admirably and not strayed from glorifying God and growing our church body to want to do the same. Serve Sunday is also awesome!"],["What Resonate does well","Relationships are available Kids are welcome"],["What Resonate does well","The community: being very kind and welcoming to others of whatever diverse background. I never fit unwelcome to the church even when we first started coming here.\nAlso, the life groups are amazing and I love how you all have a mission focused attitude to serve those around Decatur, Clarkston, Stone Mountain, and Dekalb in general."],["What Resonate does well","Resonate has done an incredible job of loving people on the margins, particularly in the last few years. From my perspective, I think much of this is attributed to specific people in leadership. Danielle has done a fantastic job in relation to the disability community and Noah has done a fantastic job with the unhoused community around our church."],["What Resonate does well","I enjoy the worship service, the kids ministry, the Wednesday night Bible studies, communion every week, the slack channels and the women of the church are a strength."],["What Resonate does well","Small intentional community. This stands out because we came from a mega church where we often felt like a number. Reaonate is great at community, foster care, supporting families, Bible study, and loving the people in our community well."],["What Resonate does well","Worship - There's freedom, and also there's tradition. I personally enjoy the Lord's Communion every Sunday. The sermon is always challenge me to be a better follower of Christ."],["What Resonate does well","We're committed. Literally zero partners left in this year so far. And I guess everyone just seems mature."],["What Resonate does well","No additions to what I say in the next box.\nOther than that you should be proud of the movement that is your Men's Retreat ministry and the direction that seems to be moving."],["What Resonate does well","Life groups, family on family discipleship inclusive of kids"],["What Resonate does well","I think Resonate is a church full of mature believers. I consistently meet believers who are pastors' kids, working in full time ministry, passionate about global missions, etc.\nThis is unique in my experience of attending churches. I think it is a strength that the church needs to be aware of and have a strategy for moving these mature believers to the next level in their walk. There is so much biblical knowledge in our church body. It is an awesome asset to have."],["What Resonate does well","The Lord has blessed us with great staff who love the Lord and lead with a generosity of spirit and heart for Him. We are a unique blend of people from different political\n\nviewpoints and we keep Jesus the main thing! We are growing in our diversity of people and that really encourages me about our future."],["What Resonate does well","Resonate to me is welcoming and relational, dedicated to serving the body and the community Many ways to plug in"],["What Resonate does well","Resonate is very welcoming and inviting. For a lot of members, the church feels very much like a family. Resonate is more than just Sundays, so many people in the church are connected in many ways throughout the week. I think the connections make Resonate unique—it’s the first time I’ve been part of a church that corporately communicates outside of Sundays regularly and actually enjoys doing life together."],["What Resonate does well","Recognizing new people, engaging with social justice issues, scripture-focused teaching, serving the local community, supporting each other with meal trains"],["What Resonate does well","Mission minded. We already have so many workers in the field, and we make a profound impact on our neighbors close by. It is fantastic."],["What Resonate does well","We are committed to the whole person. Following Jesus involves every part of you and every area of your life. We are relational and walk alongside people in genuine relationships. There is not a lot of posturing or feigning maturity. We desire to see the lost found in the 10x40 window and in Belvedere Park. Leadership is distributed and anyone can send a message to everyone on slack. Volunteers take ownership over areas to serve."],["What Resonate does well","Community. We have such great community at Resonate. Through Life Groups and touchpoints on Sunday, I have never had such deep relationships with my church community before."],["What Resonate does well","Teaching (Sundays/Wednesdays); lifegroups and retreats (intentionality and vulnerability); engaging and discipling/teaching our children"],["What Resonate does well","Discipleship and Community. This church has changed my life and made me think about our creator in whole new ways. I look back and see TRULY how discipled I have been. Starting at Resonate having grown up in the church I have fallen more deeply in love with Christ. Through teaching and friendships I have dove deeper into the endless waters of a life in communion. I have been taught how to grieve, how to ask questions, how to confess. I'm so proud to say our benediction every Sunday because its TRUE.\nWe are disciple making disciples. There is no fluff. I love how there is freedom to\n\nquestion and to lean in. I love that there is little money spent on huge programs and worship services.\nObviously, I can point to the whole Chris situation too. Very little people left and multiple services were dedicated to mourning. I feel like that is SO unique. Although this situation has been hell to walk through- I'm proud of how we trudged arm in arm."],["What Resonate does well","Small size. Music. Meaningful sermons"],["What Resonate does well","Resonate is very welcoming. Brent and Sarah do an amazing job at making people feel seen."],["What Resonate does well","Strong community and shared sense of purpose for the mission of God. Strong commitment to discipling kids, sending and supporting missions, and teaching the Word. And an increasing focus on loving and supporting our neighbors. Continued growth in diversity and welcoming atmosphere"],["What Resonate does well","We should be proud of our response in this most recent upheaval. While sad that it happened I am so grateful it was found out and dealt with seriously before getting any worse. The elders and leaders did an excellent job communicating to the congregation and for the partners it seems to be oddly unifying. I think it is a testament to our commitment, connection to one another, and a God far larger than anything we can do whether for or against Him."],["What Resonate does well","Loving people where they are. Our family has so many needs, layers, and dimensions and we felt loved, seen, and regularly encouraged by others at Resonate."],["What Resonate does well","There is a real reverence for the Word, love for one another, and care for the community. Every activity and service is intentional."],["What Resonate does well","The care for the other. Resonate is extremely community and globally focused. More so than any other church I’ve attended. It makes it so that we see the scriptures through such a lens and it is refreshing."],["What Resonate does well","Inclusivity, openness to differing viewpoints, and willingness to grow Spiritually."],["What Resonate does well","Being scripture centered."],["What Resonate does well","I have always felt like the church tries to approach scripture with humility and open hearts to hear God's truth vs. our own thoughts. The church body can be very vulnerable and transparent about their walk in the right setting (retreats, etc). The elders and staff have navigated a very challenging situation this past year with such grace,\n\nhonesty and wisdom. We feel so blessed and encouraged by this. The church cares about loving others well."],["What Resonate does well","We love well. We are a strong community. We are passionate about learning. I think we also do an incredible job at embracing a middle ground theologically even when it is hard."],["What Resonate does well","Resonate does community care incredibly well. The way that we rally to help those in our community when they need help is our strongest attribute in my opinion."],["What Resonate does well","You are an extremely young church at a time when a lot of churches have an older church body. I love seeing how many kids and young families attend. You are great at creating a welcoming environment for newcomers."],["Why I'm still here","The closeness of my life group in being a true supporter of my family’s life and how we have community."],["Why I'm still here","Our lifegroups have been a thread of constant support. With our son having a physicial disability, we have been supported prenatally, with prayer, encouragement, and accommodation in childcare space."],["Why I'm still here","Every time we walk in and say hi to at least 30 people we know. It’s amazing that our kids also know these people, and love to see them."],["Why I'm still here","The fact that so many men are willing to be vulnerable and supportive of one another's spiritual journey encourage me."],["Why I'm still here","The story of Joseph"],["Why I'm still here","I am bisexual and only out to some of my family because I am afraid of my family's very conservative bias, but doing life with my life group and sharing about my dating life and whatnot has been reaffirming that God loves me no matter what, even if my nuclear family might not accept me as who I am."],["Why I'm still here","Reality: In my experience with faith fellowships, adult children of my peers universally refuse to attend where their parents worship due to an aging membership amidst rigidity of tradition. And similarly, parents are unwilling to worship where the adult children and grandchildren attend with a younger generation. I choose to practice and promote a multigenerational faith fellowship rather than division based on comfort in traditions."],["Why I'm still here","Really, we’re still at Resonate because of the community we’ve built."],["Why I'm still here","I could list many things here. The first thing that comes to mind is the way our church disciples our children."],["Why I'm still here","My kids are being lived very well."],["Why I'm still here","Relationships. I have been a part of the community since 2014 and the friendships keep me there."],["Why I'm still here","Serve Sunday - the first Sunday we ever visited, it was a Serve Sunday. We were dressed for church, so we didn't participate. But, I loved the idea of a church taking the time to serve the community together on a Sunday morning as a form of worship. It seemed so practical, impactful, and real. We obviously came back the following Sunday :)"],["Why I'm still here","Spiritual direction with Sarah Pascual"],["Why I'm still here","The people here are wonderful and want to serve the Lord."],["Why I'm still here","My first time at LifeGroup in October 2022. The way everyone engaged one another and how welcoming it was."],["Why I'm still here","All my children feel welcome and have found friends here. They are growing in faith my oldest made her commitment to Christ not because of me, but because of the women speaking into her life and encouraging her at Resonate."],["Why I'm still here","Monday outreach lunch"],["Why I'm still here","Sarah Pascual. Being discipled by her, encouraged into leadership roles at Resonate, just feeling seen - all made me so active and excited to care for our church."],["Why I'm still here","People are going deeper in their Life Groups and the depth of relationship here is one of the reasons we are still here. So many examples: during seasons of sickness with friends bringing meals or driving to an apt, alongside our daughters doing life with friends their age. Friends, and leadership, have walked with us as a family through some tough things"],["Why I'm still here","I don't feel like I am part of a show and find a lot of joy in that we are doing our best to truly care for others and disciple them. People have names and stories to us and are not numbers."],["Why I'm still here","The community is why I attend. I am married to a non-Christian, and I know there are people I can sit with every Sunday, and my kids are welcomed with open arms."],["Why I'm still here","Resonate acknowledges what is going on in the world and is not just inward focused."],["Why I'm still here","We have been supported in mountain seasons and valley seasons"],["Why I'm still here","Instead, I'll give you the list of reasons we stayed at Resonate (that I can remember) when thinking it over last year. None of them would have much to do with anything beyond basically Sundays."],["Why I'm still here","Chris. He's simply who I met first. We were of course far less hurt than many at his departure due to the brevity of our relationship, but I'm still going to use his name here\n\nbecause what I mean is his STYLE: questioning, learning, being okay with not knowing, involving the community in sermons, being willing to swim upstream against some of the long-accepted norms of white American evangelicalism, and, basically, an openness to letting Scripture take us new places we maybe didn't think it WOULD take us (versus pouring the wet cement of our own group's dogma and setting our feet to dry in THAT). I'm really wondering what my chances are of the next guy who's chosen filling such shoes."],["Why I'm still here","Liturgy in the service. Wasn't part of my growing up, but the more, the better these days is great with me."],["Why I'm still here","Communion every week. Love that we do this. Also did not grow up with this, and probably grew up subconsciously assuming about those for whom once-a-month wasn't good enough… 'What, it doesn't stick for you?'"],["Why I'm still here","Sarah.\nA) The mere fact she was a woman spoke volumes about the direction, the heart, the willingness of a church to allow Scripture to shape it in spite of the existence of cultural pressures to the contrary. As lifelong, recovering complementarians ourselves, and each recovering, in our own ways, from the abuses inflicted in our marriage [WHILE ALSO in relationship with PLENTY who are not making similar changes and not sitting in judgment OF them]… us finding Sarah at Resonate was a happy match to a path we were already walking.\nB) HER, specifically, once we knew her better. What a blessing and a shepherd of everyone in our family she acts. I really struggled when she was singled out (well, I admit that's my own interpretation--my memory of the quote is 'we have no pastor') during a lament meeting. (I KNOW--better than you might think--those kinds of conversations are complicated and involved, and I'm not stewing about it, honestly. I'm just trying to give what you're asking for in this survey--one opinion.) Well, she's my pastor. (Which is also not an opinion I've ever given to anyone before, and will continue to not give it out except here in this survey to this team.)"],["Why I'm still here","Diversity. This was probably #1 for us, personally. As a mixed family, we visited plenty of great churches that never got a fair shake from us once we'd walked in and felt the whiteness."],["Why I'm still here","To support Sarah Pascual. I’ve been in a season where the sermons have been really important and vital to me. The connections have felt surface level and I’ve needed truth\n\nto keep me grounded. The sermons have changed and the future feels unknown but I’ve wanted to support Sarah and all of her hard work in keeping everything together."],["Why I'm still here","Resonate was a soft place to land when we came from a painful situation at our previous church."],["Why I'm still here","We came to Resonate fairly hurt from prior church experiences and within weeks we were invited to participate in a Scripture reading for Advent. This was a bit surprising as we were so new and not partners. But Song explained he invited us into that ministry as an opportunity to help heal past church hurts."],["Why I'm still here","The women in my life group got together after Chris left and debriefed how we were all doing. It was a super encouraging and connected conversation and just was a picture of how people loved each other and were choosing to follow Jesus in a really hard season."],["Why I'm still here","I think there are too many stories to count, but the thoughtful work of moving through opening the pulpit is one area that I feel like we are willing to hold to conviction and still open up our hands and hearts in ways many communities aren't willing to do."],["Why I'm still here","There are so many stories, it's hard to choose. People like Sarah Pascual and Chris Case and many of the women in the church have touched me deeply in many ways and taught me so much. If I had to choose one story out of my 10 years at Resonate, it would be the one time when I considered leaving Resonate Church, because the reason I stayed reflects one of Resonate's greatest strengths. Nearly 2 years ago, when Resonate first proposed the idea of allowing non-elders (and women) to preach at church, I was strongly opposed to the idea of female preachers based on 1 Timothy and 1 Corinthians. The announcement came right before me and my spouse's 2.5 month trip out of the country for an adoption, and I spent much of the trip praying, studying the Word (and commentary), and reading Resonate's position paper on the topic multiple times. The position paper was helpful, but what really kept me at Resonate was the approach that the staff and elders took when making and implementing the change. They hosted multiple forums, allowing the church to have a voice, they explained that the change is not a foundation of our faith/salvation, they \"talked\" us through their decision making process and reasoning via multiple emails and meetings, and they made the decision via an elder vote. God drew me to the passage about respecting elders' decisions. This transparent and gradual process enabled me to reflect and pray through my hesitations, and it's why I decided to remain a partner at Resonate. That being said, with Rory just now stepping down as elder for the very\n\nreason of not agreeing with women preaching, this is an indication that not all elders fully supported the change, and I hope that Resonate Church re-examines its stance on this issue. Rory did vote for the change, but he says that he did express his skepticism about the change beforehand. This does not seem like the change was unanimously supported. Furthermore, given that Chris' issues were prolonged over a period, he may have not been in the right mindset to vote on a change as big as that one. This indicates that two of Resonate's elders were not fully on board or able to fully support the change towards non-elders preaching, and I hope that this change is re-examined as a result."],["Why I'm still here","It's difficult to define one moment, but here are a few (mostly recent): (1) One woman in my LifeGroup dropping everything to spend a late night with another woman who had just lost her father from COVID. (2) A Wed night in Feb: Elementary schoolers as well as adults gathered around the middle and high schoolers -- laying hands on them and praying for them as they were preparing to be sent out on their first mission trip (3) Testimonies, tears, and glory given to God as we opened the mic for anyone to share at the Living Stone service in the fall, (4) Tony bursting into tears of joy and honor when we gave him a cake and celebrated his birthday with him at Outreach Hour. (5) Walking with the Kelloggs from meeting Jesus to growing in faith to healing and now to leadership."],["Why I'm still here","My first lifegroup is why we joined, and we have stayed for the people. I think that looking towards the future, we would stay for the possibility of our shild getting to be discipled in an ethnically diverse church."],["Why I'm still here","I have several neighbors who go to Resonate, and they are part of several different communities that I'm in."],["Why I'm still here","Life group has been the best and most consistent community I’ve been a part of in a long time. They have become genuine friends and challenge me to live a life more aligned to the gospel each and every day"],["Why I'm still here","We are still at resonate because over the years this church has been faithful to preach biblically. Our family has learned so much from our gifted teachers. Also, our friendships have deep-end in life groups to the point that we consider them like family."],["Why I'm still here","I regularly meet up with so many men in this church. And wow what a breath of fresh air it is. I have been torn down by groups of men all my life. I have been taught subtle ways of toxic masculinity. But, the real vulnerable conversation I am able to have with so\n\nmany men in this church has been healing those wounds. Turning pre-concieved notions on their ugly heads. I have seen kindness in male friendship made only possible through the example of our beloved Christ."],["Why I'm still here","Everyday I am trying to look a little more like Jesus and I feel like at Resonate, those around me are also trying to do the same."],["Why I'm still here","As of late, the way the Spirit inhabits the worship of his people as we gather corporately has blessed my soul and ministered to deep hurts. It's encouraging to hear voices of people singing their testimony or declaring a truth that hits a raw spot in my own life. In an age (or life stage!) where everyday life is individualistic and a bit isolated, the way God shows up in community is a testament to the world."],["Why I'm still here","When we first started and Resonate leadership was trying to decide what do to serve people best during Covid, we were considered. Since our family was high risk, we were told that since Ben still needed a mask then people would wear them until the risk was significantly reduced. This is just a small example of a larger value that Resonate has."],["Why I'm still here","Relationships. Expository preaching. Theological alignment.\nI would not say that we have BEST friends at Resonate. But just being known by so many people makes it feel like home. And we WANT to grow deeper in those relationships."],["Why I'm still here","During the communion, there was a sister under the cross praying and crying. The elders and partners gathered around and prayed with and for her. Even when the communion was finished, she was still crying and praying, and the whole church waited for her. How the whole church and the partners are sensitive how the Spirit is working during the service, how as a whole church deeply care for each member."],["Why I'm still here","Honestly, what drew us to Resonate was the willingness to engage in difficult, real conversations - an honest humility to say \"I don't know\" or \"I'm not sure\" but an honest desire to seek God and scripture in finding out His heart. We started coming during the latter half of the COVID pandemic and after the Atlanta Asian Spa shootings. Chris and others' willingness to name and speak out against the wrong/hurtful/racially-motivated things happening in our city and country at the time were really important to me and my family. Chris's preaching and teaching (and the worship team) was what first really kept us interested in Resonate, but honestly, the life group we are now part of is the greatest part of the healing that we needed and one of the biggest reasons why we are still here today."],["Why I'm still here","Still here bc there are Opportunities to learn from younger generation,,,also family attends here"],["Why I'm still here","So many things. What initially captured us was the welcome we experienced from when we first visited—smiles at the door, people that introduced themselves. A big thing that has kept us around is just the sense that everyone in our family is being discipled and engaged—Wednesday nights are a great example of this. Every one of us wants to be there and wouldn't miss it, whether it's us adults in Bible study, the older kids at Shout, the middle elementary student in her Bible study, and even the five year old in childcare who loves going to \"night church.\""],["Why I'm still here","The need to hug my life group members each Sunday whether we are in the same service or not. The deconstruct to reconstruct series made me feel so happy to be in a church that is ok to wrestle with multiple view points but point back to one good/just/righteous/etc God, his Son, and Holy Spirit."],["Why I'm still here","I don’t know that I can come up with one moment. It’s the overall that keeps us here.\nWe truly feel Resonate is set apart. With such a strong focus on scripture and the role of the church in the community. To win people over to Jesus through love and Christ like principles rather than force. It’s so counter to the majority of churches we’ve been to in the past."],["Why I'm still here","I had a dream this is where we were suppose to be. We were called to the East Lake neighborhood. The church moved closer to us each time it moved.(twice). I’ve received affirmation over the years this is where our home is."],["Why I'm still here","The Sunday we decided to attend Resonate weekly was the week before we heard the news of the lead pastor stepping down. It came to us as a big surprise and we sought God with a question: Is this really a church where you want us to be? As we carefully attended a few more Sundays services, my husband and I were blessed and challenged by how Resonate is responding to the sudden loss of the lead pastor. The leadership was very transparent, vulnerable, but hopeful in Christ."],["Why I'm still here","The relationships we have built here are bigger than the recent trouble. I and my household feel this is where we are meant to be."],["Why I'm still here","Having wonderful conversation with leaders of church and life group and feeling honored in my spirit to be connected"],["Why I'm still here","I had two people over the men's retreat approach me and apologize for something they had done regarding me. Thankfully in both cases I wasn't offended but I really appreciated the humility (one a staff member and the other my senior by at least 15 years) to admit their faults to me. I felt seen and valued"],["Why I'm still here","\"Simon Peter answered him, “Lord, to whom shall we go? You have the words of eternal life\" This is where I'm called to be. I'm not going anywhere."],["Why I'm still here","Men’s Retreat comes to mind this past one especially. I was talking with someone at the fire late in the evening and he pointed out righty so that this is a miracle- Resonate having the biggest mens retreat. The church didn’t implode after Chris was let go. It’s so encouraging to see a church so unified. I was also inspired and encouraged seeing the elders all weekend during the retreat with deep prayers interceding for the men and body."],["Why I'm still here","Willingness for people to invite me over for dinner"],["Why I'm still here","It's the people for me. When I enter the church, it feels different from the worlds outside it's walls. I love this about it."],["Why I'm still here","The fact that our Elders and Leadership Team handled Chris' sin and firing so well with transparency and the skill not to miss a beat. The way the church has responded by leaning into deeper relationship with one another instead of dropping out of faith in Jesus."],["Why I'm still here","Seeing how I've been discipled through the past 8 years and also seeing how I've been able to disciple others through the learning I've done here has been the most amazing gift. This community is my home and we couldn't imagine being anywhere else."],["Why I'm still here","Having lifegroup pray with me and for me consistently while dealing with very challenging physical/emotional issues. Looking back- their support really got me through."],["Why I'm still here","The essence of the messages, they speak to me of Jesus and how i believe he calls us to love our neighbors and pray for our enemies."],["Why I'm still here","The teaching and focus on Scripture was the biggest thing that drew me to Resonate and why I still attend. But then the way that staff have handled the transition over the past months, the intentionality and seeking to support everyone in different ways, the\n\ncare and humility with which they handled the past few months, captures why I'm still here. I think the leadership at Resonate is really strong."],["Why I'm still here","People at Resonate suffer well"],["Why I'm still here","Men's Retreat"],["Why I'm still here","Encouraged by the number of .missionaries we send out that are \"normal\"ish people, getting to be a sender"],["Why I'm still here","My first Sunday visiting over 6 years ago now, getting to meet someone who saw me and loved me and pulled me up a chair in the parking lot!\nAnd that same kind of love, connection, and service has continued."],["Why I'm still here","The way the men rallied around each other to encourage us to not sit in our loneliness but to engage with each other through the recent turmoil. That speaks volumes about the commitment to each other at this church"],["Why I'm still here","As turmoil has deepened in this country, Resonate has not shrunk back from it, but has brought these issues to the light in a way that's affirmative of justice, and the gospel."],["Why I'm still here","Our Lifegroup is what really keeps us here. We love doing life with them. They took us in and cared for us immediately Lex"],["Why I'm still here","Resonate is serious about being a church that both reaches out and serves the Belvedere Plaza community."],["Why I'm still here","There have been some tough years for us finding deep community at the church. I think we may have been left on the outside because it looks like we already have lots of community - and we do, but they are not necessarily hang out when there's nothing to do friends or travel with friends or friends you call when you have a migraine and you can't put the kids to bed. Or, maybe more importantly, friends with whom you can bring your struggles into the light. This was a source of great frustration for us since it seemed like others in the church DID have those kinds of relationships. What was it about us that kept this good thing out of reach? There were many times when, if I had said the word, Phil would have gladly gone to another church.\nBut. This current iteration of life group has been so good for us. The families are engaged and want to be there. We all struggle with time and space in our schedules to hang out, but when something goes sideways like babies born early or having major surgery, we showed up for each other in major ways. It took time (as it always does), but now we can have hard conversations with each other, grow together, and really know\n\nhow to spur one another on. When that growth towards intimacy was threatened, we stuck with each other and fought to keep the relationships moving deeper, seeking God's will and provision for the group. What started as a hurtful experience ended as a beautiful moment to watch God love and care for us. He is moving us to be more like Jesus together."],["Why I'm still here","Being willing to continue to learn and grow as a community."],["Why I'm still here","Going through trauma and having multiple people from church sit with me and listen, not trying to fix my situation or my theology in all my questions, but acknowledging the hard and being present with me through it."],["Why I'm still here","In all honesty, the men's retreat both is one of the best highlights of my time at church, but also is a perfect example of what I love about this place. How we see manhood as a part of supporting each other."],["Why I'm still here","The chaos of housechurch with kids"],["Why I'm still here","There are so many reasons! But, to continue from above, I’ve never been at a church where I could say I know so many people - singles, married, with kids, without kids, with grandkids - who I feel genuinely cared for by and truly enjoy relationship with them. It feels like a glimpse of heaven in this way - of brothers and sisters loving one another well. It’s truly a gem."],["Why I'm still here","In 2016, my wife and I were emotionally struggling with the racial unrest and protest against police brutality that were happening in the country. At the time, there many if any other Black people at Resonate. But our community didn't let the difference in experience get in the way of caring for us in our time of need. We felt cared for and heard even though we were definitely in the minority in terms of how we experienced that time. The community's willingness to sit in that pain with us and not push us away was amazing for us."],["Why I'm still here","Really appreciated the Entrepreneur Bible study"],["Why I'm still here","Hearing my spouse talk about the men's retreat and how they came around each other in prayer, strength and love."],["Why I'm still here","Life groups. Seeing a relatively new life group creating strong connections is why I want to stay."],["Why I'm still here","Resonate is where my people are, so this is where I want to be."],["Why I'm still here","Our LifeGroup becoming one of the most important things in my life over years. They're my friends, my people, my community."],["Why I'm still here","This is a hard question to respond to in a single story or moment, but one story is that each week in the car on the way home, or in the car with my son taking him to bible study on wednesday nights, we get to have great conversations about the Word and faith taught to them by other believers in our church. I believe this is a key part of discipling our kids."],["Why I'm still here","I have a number of stories where men in the church, who weren't pastors or lay leadership, have reached out, supported, been a friend. I struggle with consistency in that sort of activity, but it is meaningful when it happens."],["Why I'm still here","Having experienced some difficult church situations, we found the leaders and partners here to be genuine, compassionate, and patient as we processed past experiences and leaned into becoming a more committed part of Resonate. One sermon several years ago really stood out, as the pastor identified spiritual abuse and recognized it as something God was highlighting and healing. I felt truly heard that Sunday."],["Why I'm still here","We came from a bigger church and we never felt like anyone really knew who we were even though we served and did a lot of things for the community. But when we came to Resonate, we felt like people wanted to get to know us and it was refreshing to see the staff members/elders at the church come introduce themselves to us throughout the first few weeks at Resonate.\nWe also love the Life Groups that we're in. It has honestly been one of the highlights and main reasons why we love the community at Resonate. I feel like I'm actually walking day to day with people in my life that are seeking Jesus and who are consistently showing up to life groups. I feel I can be held accountable and vulnerable with others who will support me."],["Why I'm still here","What comes to mind are times in community, whether it's with our life group or spending time with people outside of church."],["Why I'm still here","I've never been part of a church that does life together so well. I feel known and loved."],["Why I'm still here","When we found Resonate we were specifically praying to land in a place of like-minded community. We didn't know anyone and were so welcomed in and challenged that it was a strong hook for us. It seemed like everyone we met was in our same life stage!\nWe needed friends and community and Resonate provided that and helped us grow\n\nour own community. Another undeniable reason is how our kids are seen, taught and loved at Resonate. Being a part of a church that sees the value in our children and valuing that they learn to love Jesus is very strong."],["Why I'm still here","The first day I attended Resonate, I was greeted by several people and offered a ride home as I didn't have one, and I have always been greeted warmly since."],["Why I'm still here","A common desire in the body to become more like Christ, and further his Kingdom on earth"],["Why I'm still here","The depth of relationships formed through lifegroups along with the intentionality."],["Why I'm still here","Helping to serve"],["Why I'm still here","The way everything has been handled in the wake of Chris’s departure has deepened my belief that resonate is the place for me. I’ve never seen a church walk through something like this with so much transparency and humility. It’s very encouraging to see that leadership and the body have moved toward one another and sought God through it all."],["Why I'm still here","I've never been driven to tears in a religious service as much as I have here. It's usually a confluence of factors - sermons that get me introspective, gratitude for what I have, motivation to do better in my day to day, and the music"],["Why I'm still here","The long term relationships I've built through lifegroup over the years."],["Why I'm still here","Resonate feels like home. I am thankful for all of it."],["Why I'm still here","family"],["What I wish was better","Personal challenge to apply the Word and opportunity to respond, not necessarily an altar call but maybe taking advantage of the prayer rooms"],["What I wish was better","I’m not sure the solution, but more connectedness across life groups, between services, and church wide. Growth towards feeling like a family and having joy in being together!! I know this takes time and we were transitioning back overseas when this started to happen more naturally."],["What I wish was better","Allowing more time for worship."],["What I wish was better","Prophesy and words for people. Space and mentorship for art and Dancing. For the Lord."],["What I wish was better","The only \"negative\" (and I use that adjective loosely) would be the lack of men's discipleship. I was very involved in the D groups and when the transition from those to life groups happened, I was in the minority but was disappointed with the void that it left. I have since filled that through ministering and discipling men through F3. The recent amazing turnout at the men's retreat speaks volumes that the men of Resonate are being impacted, but there could be some space for more intentional discipleship outside of the life groups and retreats.\n(saying this to myself as much as providing it as feedback!)"],["What I wish was better","Somehow after ~5 years at Resonate there are still so many people I don't know, which I suppose is a function of having two services and geographically-based life groups, so I'm not sure how that would even be addressed."],["What I wish was better","I believe we have room to open up to more prayer."],["What I wish was better","The women’s ministry and connection. There are women who have been at the church for years that still feel like they can’t break through the cliques that have formed. I think this reality makes it hard to want to join in the few women’s events that we do have each year."],["What I wish was better","The prayer rooms feel \"too scary\", how can we make prayer for others more normalized during our service in the row or in the corner of the room. Closing the door ... feels too vulnerable and detached from worship."],["What I wish was better","I would have said worship service, but since Sang has joined our Resonate family, it has greatly improved along with organization of the worship."],["What I wish was better","Acknowledged the LGBTQIA+ community, tried to pursue them, welcome them. We don't have to draw a line in the sand, but we can still be intentionally loving. I feel there are a handful of folks in the community or tangential to the community, and we have not done a good job going deeply with them."],["What I wish was better","Inviting community to happen across life groups and including people who are not in life groups. Not just community siloed to one life group."],["What I wish was better","Lifegroups.\nI love the idea of what we have going and I am so cognizant of the difficulty of creating fostering small environments for so many different kinds of people. AND I hear most Sunday's about how most of how to truly get to know the church is through life groups.\nBut if this is the case-- wouldn't massive amounts of time and resources be devoted to this structure? From my vantage point, little investment has happened. I thought we were trending in a right direction with the trellis books last year. That was AMAZING.\nThere were so many great conversations to be had, resources to use. And I understand that took time- but that should be the minimum. In any organization, you invest resources into the things that matter most. I'm so happy to see Resonate invest so much into local outreach. Keep doing that. But shouldn't life groups be next? With how it is advertised on Sundays. I think that it would be. But time and again the discussions are dry, little thought has been put into questions, structure or time. I have been so disappointed with how our church does life groups.\nThe rub though, is that some people LOVE life groups. And that makes sense. The legacy groups that have been around the longest and created clique-like mentalities reap the benefits of a system that only bends to them.\nWhile the rest of the church (I have had conversations with so many people) feel left out forgotten, and frustrated by the half hazard groups that have been put together.\nIt's not fair and it feels like false advertising every Sunday."],["What I wish was better","I wish we would reconsider our stance on “soft complimentarion” and move toward egalitarianism."],["What I wish was better","More service opportunities outside if the church"],["What I wish was better","Nothing"],["What I wish was better","Vision. Plan and roadmap for 3, 5, and 10 years."],["What I wish was better","I wish resonate would update its perspectives on homosexuality being a sin and the hierarchical relationship between men and women. There are more progressive churches. And the time is now. We have a new covenant - love God and love each other. Keep it simple. No need to create unnecessary negativity"],["What I wish was better","I think we could continue to improve our efforts to increase the racial and socioeconomic diversity at our church. Our congregation still doesn't reflect the neighborhood as much as it could in my opinion."],["What I wish was better","I would love to see more opportunities for discipleship and pastoral care weaved into our current rhythms. To be able to have in person conversations with leadership and receive wisdom and guidance.\nAlthough we are aligned with our priority on social justice, conversation around this can feel very political and uncomfortable for us if we have different leanings on certain topics. Not sure how to articulate this but I hope you hear the heart of it.\nWhen it comes to the children's ministry (elementary age), I would love to see more teaching on what we believe and why."],["What I wish was better","Honestly, I don’t think I have anything to contribute. Resonate isn’t perfect. Whenever people are involved, it won’t be perfect. But Resonate has always felt like a Christ centered home. And even during recent difficult situations, it’s been handled with grace by leadership. A testament to how the leadership team has been structured from the start. Again, it’s not perfect, but o don’t think I can come up won’t criticisms to be addressed. I’ll leave that to others."],["What I wish was better","I noticed how the sermons are inconsistent in terms of how they're formatted or preached depending on the speaker for the week. I find this confusing and hard to understand what I should be paying attention to in the sermon at times. How is this specific sermon tied to the previous week?\nInstead I think there's an opportunity to better connect the dots so that every week's sermon is more clear in terms of how it builds up to the sermon theme and/or how it keeps the church accountable week to week. For example, if we're talking about\n\nJoseph's suffering then every week could be more of a reminder of the application from the prior week and how that builds up on the sermon's theme of God's deliverance.\nOverall, I like the sermon themes and I love how we dive straight into the bible."],["What I wish was better","Not really sure. In some ways I feel like it’s a little challenging to build relationships outside of a Lifegroup but I’m glad Resonate isn’t a super events driven church."],["What I wish was better","I want us to be even better neighbors to our local community. I think we are making huge strides, especially in the past few years, but I would love to see our community grow as a force FOR our community even more."],["What I wish was better","Very minor, but would appreciate more asynchronous updates for partners (for those of us whose life group conflicts with the partner meetings)"],["What I wish was better","I wish there was a little bit more intentional personal outreach for new visitors. As someone who hasn't been at Resonate for a super long time, and also as someone who hesitates to take the first step in things without explicit invitation, it can feel tricky to become involved in things like Bible studies or Life Groups."],["What I wish was better","Part of this is something I need to work on personally but I wish we were less rigid in musical worship. There are times when I feel like dancing while singing but I don't feel comfortable doing so. Part of this is fear of man on my part but we also are a very heady church. I feel the emotional side of church in small groups with men but the worship services tend to be more intellectual."],["What I wish was better","I wish we would see more diversity within our community. We have seen many people come through our doors who represent some type of diversity (racial, economic, worldview) and a percentage of them do not stay with us. I don't know the reasoning for this but feel it is an area for us to lean into as a body."],["What I wish was better","This is a tough one but it comes with love. I think over the years we have created a culture that has watered down the gravity of sin and our need for the gospel. I have at times felt that we are more focused on teaching a clever, deep, and smart message, than actually speaking the truth. Jesus says that those who love him will obey him. So my hope for our church is that we would pursue all that God has for us. Grace, repentance, holiness, purity...and freedom from sin!"],["What I wish was better","I would love to see more structure and processes in the Eldership and leadership. This comes from Chris's time. As the only staff elder it felt like it was impossible to move\n\nthings forward because it all depended on him and often those things fell through the cracks."],["What I wish was better","It’s hard to get plugged in. Life groups can be a huge commitment depending on the season of life. There aren’t regular ways to meet and connect with others - families, women. I’d love to see quarterly programming to engage different groups."],["What I wish was better","Defining elder roles and responsibilities Creating more life groups"],["What I wish was better","Building more connections between 1st and 2nd service. Building more connections overall. I interact the most with certain pockets of the church (i.e. my LG, the kids and the families I serve in Resonate Kids), but I'm always surprised (and sad) that there are people who have been at Resonate for years whom I've never met."],["What I wish was better","I would like to see more people help with worship- reading scripture, praying, etc."],["What I wish was better","We should do a better job of supporting the leadership team."],["What I wish was better","Shorter sermons. Sometimes they get long winded and repetitive. I'd love more time to interact with the congregation."],["What I wish was better","My family doesn’t have a life group yet. It is because the life groups in my city is already very full. I wish my family was plugged into a life group quickly. I heard life group takes a break during summer. I can’t wait to join life group this fall and get to know people in great depth."],["What I wish was better","For us the church isn't as diverse (ethnically or age-wise) as we would prefer and we are uncomfortable being somewhere long-term where women are not fully equipped to lead. It has also been hard to make friends or navigate the space as new attendees...\nespecially during the first 5-7 visits!"],["What I wish was better","Better engagement with LBGTQ+ community and clearer stances and structures for how to love that community well and welcome them in to our family while also sharing the truth of the gospel with them."],["What I wish was better","Staff/leadership/elder development is something that seems to be at the forefront at the moment. While my perception of how our staff and elders have navigated the last few months is positive, it feels shortsighted that we only had three elders in December one of whom was Chris. In general, I do not know how much development/discipleship occurs within the staff."],["What I wish was better","I love communion as Tinction but also wish we were more clear before partaking together that it is for believers.\nAlso to be a welcoming space for families who have children who have experienced abuse in various ways. We feel like we are the only ones."],["What I wish was better","I wish we allowed more freedom of expressing differences to our youth. It feels hard to have to fall in line with the elders decisions in all teaching."],["What I wish was better","Resonate needs a better women’s ministry that doesn’t just include non-working mothers for morning Bible studies. We also find the music selection cheesy and out of date sometimes but are happy in general with it. The tv being on in the little kids room is also kind of weird. We don’t send out kids to church for them to watch tv."],["What I wish was better","--When it comes to discipling people of all ages and walks of life, it feels like there is a bit of a gap starting with late teenagers and into younger adults, like college and graduate students, or those just working in their early 20's. There are so many married couples and families with kids that I can imagine the unmarried 18–25 demographic especially feeling like there isn't much for them.\n--Communication outside of Sunday mornings can be a weakness at times. Sometimes things are mentioned in only one forum, whether it's a Sunday morning announcement, a Slack message, or an email. More consistent, frequent, and unified messaging would be helpful.\n--Life Groups are great, but they can also become insular. It's harder to make connections beyond your Life Group. Oftentimes, it seems like we don't always know what people struggles people are going through (or on the positive side, what they're celebrating) until a meal train gets announced! There are partners/attendees who live within a couple blocks of us who we don't know that well because we don't happen to be in an LG together.\n--While it's a strength that Resonate is flexible, adaptable, and not stuck in rigid routines or processes, sometimes it can seem like we're just figuring out things as we go. I don't think this is apparent on the surface (as noted before, we give an impression of excellence), but the longer you're around or see behind-the-scenes, it can raise some concerns.\n--Perhaps relatedly, I think our Centered Set approach is great, but sometimes it does feel like, instead of just saying we don't know or aren't sure about something, we work ourselves into highly nuanced positions that most of our congregation couldn't\n\narticulate and maybe doesn't even understand. (This is weakness I have myself, so I get it.) As an example of both, the leadership structure is very complex, and maybe not necessarily by design but because it developed that way over time.\n--Because the primary leadership position—eldership—is reserved for men only, and thus probably only a quarter to a third of the congregation (ie, excluding women and children), we are not fully leveraging the incredible resources of talent, experience, and skills that God has gifted us with. There are opportunities for women, but they are less visible and less defined, and thus women are less likely to pursue them or even be aware of them. Even if we say that women can now preach or be on LT, when there are only a few women who have ever served in those ways. Eldership provides a defined leadership pathway for men, but there is no comparable structure for women. If we keep eldership as a male-only role, then we need to figure out how to better disciple, develop, and utilize the incredible women in our community."],["What I wish was better","See previous answer"],["What I wish was better","Offering opportunities for folks within the church who don't really know each other, to get to know each other."],["What I wish was better","I mean, I truly have had such a positive experience… I think the discipleship question got me thinking about how nice it would be if there was like a system for that. Like if a group of ladies wanted to sign up to disciple younger ladies - I would join in a heartbeat. (Would love to be discipled by a woman who has been following Jesus longer. And, if I can help someone else, would love to do that too.)"],["What I wish was better","The congregation does not feel rooted. Most church attendees/members don’t know each other outside of life groups and tend to only associate within those groups. It can come across as cliquey. Church Members come and go quickly. You won’t even know that they left or that they were part of the church."],["What I wish was better","More events where I can see people outside my own LG. Sunday potlucks growing up were so special! Also sermons since Chris left have been good but a little more feel-y and a little less head-y."],["What I wish was better","Engaging with women who have are unmarried and have no kids and are a bit older (late 20s and beyond). I think it's a small demographic, but as someone who is in that category it can be isolating; it can feel like life is only about getting married and having kids after a certain age. I love celebrating friends who are getting married and having kids, but I wish there was a little more visibility for women like me."],["What I wish was better","I think Resonate does its best to include all types of people, but as is human nature, we can all tend to find it easy to stick to groups in which we feel most familiar and comfortable. (Not unique to Resonate.) There are Sundays I notice that some people are sitting quietly and no one is greeting them or drawing them into conversation. I try to make a point of doing so, but I am only one person. I also find it challenging to build with people in other age demographics, but find they easily gravitate toward others their own age outside of church functions (based on what we hear people doing, planning, etc.) I am also aware of my need to continue to reach out and care for others regardless of their perceived responses. :)"],["What I wish was better","We could emphasize even more the work of the Holy Spirit in the lives of the believers… both in non-spectacular and in spectacular ways. The latter might look like someone occasionally sharing a “prophecy” (word) - of course, as the Holy Spirit would lead - for the edification of the church. God works powerfully at times through believers!"],["What I wish was better","-Continuing to engage in the difficult, explicit conversations including recent/current events -Encouraging and embracing women leadership; I appreciate that the pulpit has been opened up to qualified non-elders preaching, and I think the language has probably been very intentional but in the past year, I think on Sundays, the only non-elders who have preached have been Sarah and Jonathan Pascual - and Jonathan was a prior elder.\nAfter Chris was put on leave, Sarah and the Eldership team (including Trey who came back on to help) have been incredible - and although the question was raised during the last partnership meeting if an interim lead pastor would be brought in, without the title of \"lead pastor,\" Sarah has faithfully been teaching and helping to effectively lead Resonate through this incredibly difficult time. I don't think she is looking for praise or recognition, but I do think it should be acknowledged and appreciated by everyone that without Sarah, we probably would have struggled a whole lot more to keep Sunday services and our church community together."],["What I wish was better","I don't know that there's much I am afraid to say out loud :) I think our church has grown so much in the few years that it has been around. There is always a strong desire for more 'equipping' - sometimes that means mentoring, teaching how to engage certain communities, giving the tools. So much of this relies on the churchgoer and not just leadership, so I am hesitant to lodge those as things I wish we did better."],["What I wish was better","Nothing comes to mind."],["What I wish was better","My perception is that many of our systems, actions, budgetary choices, etc. lack:"],["What I wish was better","clear and consistent comms about what, why, when"],["What I wish was better","a follow through to completion"],["What I wish was better","follow up in the manner of this document I don't want to be super-programmatic, but to improve follow through, comms, and reflection on things that do happne."],["What I wish was better","We are obviously experiencing a deficit in our senior pastor role. Sermons have suffered and I feel they are lacking. Often times they take too long and aren't delivered in a way that makes the main point obvious."],["What I wish was better","More fellowship opportunities for new members to meet the church."],["What I wish was better","Nothing strategic comes to mind"],["What I wish was better","I think church members largely structure our lives from our own conveniences, indicating that our primary identity is our job or other life commitments. Life Groups still reflect availability of days of week, friend groups, or where we live, which also resemble structural or social determinants. While Resonate church does not want to become an overly programmed church, it does take church-wide functions or special interest group events to bring people together for more in-depth conversations with people outside Life Group that you may not otherwise know. I wish it were more common to know people's stories or testimonies, because that opens the door for authentic or wellmatched mentorship and discipleship opportunities to walk through seasons together. I miss the richness of regularly interacting with people of different life stages and walks of life."],["What I wish was better","More time together as a body! I’ve always wondered why we didn’t do Wednesday night supper which I know has logistical challenges and part of the solution is life groups, but I have always loved going to that at my home church where I grew up."],["What I wish was better","Support for female partners. Since we have all male elders, I feel like male partners are well supported. I think the men's retreat is a really good example. Men are challenged to be honest during and then I have seen elders follow up with them and stay connected with them after to continue to encourage, support and challenge them. But it feels like Sarah is the only female leader in our church and she can't support all the\n\nfemale partners. And while the elders do sometimes seem to try to support female partners, there is a disconnect because they are all male. There has been some support from being involved in a life group but I have not seen intentionality over an extended period of time to continue to check in and follow up. I think some female partners have support because they have deep relationships with people within Resonate but my closest friends are outside of Resonate and it has been hard to establish deep relationships within Resonate because it feels like there are not many people in my stage of life/those that are already have deep relationships and it's hard to join in.\nEmphasis in most of this statement is \"feels like.\""],["What I wish was better","I wish we prayed together more. I wish more new people, non-believers came through the door on Sunday mornings and that we, as a group, had more conversations to that end (I am very guilty of not doing this also).\nLeadership has traditionally not handled conflict and hard conversations very well, leading to burned relationships. This pains my heart that it has happened on at least several occasions.\nUntil last year, I would have said relying on volunteers for some key roles is a big weakness. The hiring of Sang and Noah has helped me feel better about that, but we went way too long without someone leading the worship/production team.\nA lack of clarity and vision and execution for several key processes around church structure and function is a weakness. We have always struggled with having enough elders and the process seems opaque and way way too long. Clarity around the process would go a long way. Clarity and vision for what life groups are supposed to look like and how to better balance deep intimacy with making more room. What is the lifecycle of a life group? Training for life groups and life group leaders has filled an important need in our community. Someone brought up deacons in the partner meeting and I was trying to think of other great ideas and programs that have been tried, but not executed on (maybe because of an over-reliance on volunteers?).\nBetter fiscal responsibility - I know there have been decisions around infrastructure where the cheaper option was taken, only leading to greater expenses in the long run.\nYou buy the cheap microphones and then none of them work. It's not worth the savings when it ends up costing more. To me, this is not fiscally responsible, even though it may look like it at first. Why did it take us so long to buy a safety door for the kids' area? The\n\nlack of leadership over worship/production because we couldn't or didn't have the money hurt the church."],["What I wish was better","I think the debate point that came up most in our trying to decide about staying was, \"I wish they were friendlier.\" Friendliness is tough. Not a characteristic of tons of churches, many of them great, though certainly there are some who do it better than others."],["What I wish was better","More personal connections with visitors to welcome them ( aside from staff)"],["What I wish was better","no major things to mention"],["What I wish was better","Life Groups - These are supposed to be \"location specific\" but end up being people in similar life phases. I was hopeful with the implementation of the trellis study last year, and truly loved it. but this year's study / discussion questions were very repetitive and felt like I was going to a middle school bible study. It seems odd to read scripture without anybody studying it and then everyone just stating their antidotal opinions. I thought we had bible study on Wednesday nights to dive deeper into scripture and have conversations after deeply studying the word to lead to more fruitful conversations. I do not understand the format of this year. On top of that, it feels dishonest to state life groups are location specific, when in reality many people just want to be in a group together and do not actually live in the same area. Its hard to feel like you belong when groups in your area are full with people who lives 20+ minutes away and its more of a friend group than anything else."],["What I wish was better","Care from and relationship with leadership (elders/pastor) I feel like has been slightly lacking in the amount of time that we've been attending/partners. I think the current circumstances change that a bit, but most of the relationships that I've built over the years have been with lifegroups."],["What I wish was better","Having more organization and church-focused events and programs would help build more unity. Danielle B. has been awesome with the kids, and Jamie before her, but the kids program could have more events and more of a focus on learning Bible verses.\nAlso, the adults could use more events, especially co-ed ones given that we already have gender-specific programs (e.g., women's Bible studies, retreats). Resonate does a fantastic job looking outward and could add a little attention looking inward. (Although I know that we have limited resources, and this takes people/time.)"],["What I wish was better","This country would be transformed by the heart change if we were trulky willing and open ... I would appreciate a study about the God's explicit and implicit instruction and guidance through the life and Teaching of Jesus."],["What I wish was better","Some more intentionality to embrace diversity and multiculturalism."],["What I wish was better","I believe Resonate dismissed and overlooked Chris' character issues for too many years.\nThe elders were too busy and too disconnected (understandably) to see the gravity of his behavior and pointed to the fruit of his teaching and general functionality of his leadership to allow inappropriate behavior to go unchecked."],["What I wish was better","Raising leaders That our church would better reflect our community (race/ethnicity/ability)"],["What I wish was better","Keeping the sanctuary quiet during worship and service. For example, loud doors constantly slamming, people talking loudly outside in the lobby or in the hallway, etc."],["What I wish was better","While I love the model of a center-set church, we don't talk enough about sin, repentance, and our deep need for the gospel."],["What I wish was better","I wish the elders recognized that Sarah has been leading the church with excellence, humility and wisdom. Even before Chris was fired, it felt like Sarah was our pastor and Chris was the preacher. Sarah shepherds us and is responsible for all the deep teaching/learning. I think it’s time to really dig in theologically and reassess the stance that n women in leadership. At this point, it will feel forced to bring in someone from outside of resonate to take over. Sarah should lead and back fill her current role."],["What I wish was better","I think that it is relatively easy to attend Resonate, be in a Life Group, serve, go to a bible study - and be basically coasting in your faith. I know this probably true about any church, but I think that there is a lack of real disciple making and disciple maturing programs. Life Groups are fine. But they are not great. Especially in 25-26. Reading one passage of scripture out of context when 80%+ of the people in the room are not caught up on the bible reading plan and then asking \"what stands out to you?\" (this is basically what's happening in the groups that are trying to do what Brent planned for us this year) feels not only basic, but wrong. If we are going to prioritize studying scripture in life groups, it should be far more structured than this. The Trellis was GREAT. But we also don't NEED to develop ALL our own curriculum. Why reinvent the wheel when there is so much content already out there?"],["What I wish was better","The biggest thing was in the past, not feeling like anyone stepped up to Chris to his face. He held back the church in so many ways and should have been forced to change or move on."],["What I wish was better","I think there’s opportunities when it comes to some of the song choices and also the starting point of the teaching. I think too often these are focused more on what we as believers receive/our standing because of the Gospel as opposed to the Gospel itself;\nthat is the life, death, and resurrection of Christ. I can only sing so much about the blessings we receive without also singing about WHY we receive those blessings.\nGranted, this is just my opinion and I’m one individual."],["What I wish was better","Sometimes the small group/family cliques can be challenging to feel as I am part of the body."],["What I wish was better","Life Group structure and purpose. At Resonate, from my perception, many people get into a LG and the goal is to stay with the same people for 5-, 10- years. I would challenge that framing. I love that we can be in long-term relationship with people. But I also think that unintentionally that excludes others from being able to join and it can come across as clique-ish. I would love the goal of LG to multiply, where groups go deep together and grow and then at a point they split into two to make room for new families and individuals. This is how we invite people into community and keep the deep relationships with some of those you have been doing life with. I have heard multiple stories of people not being able to join LGs because they are full and I just wish we had more of a heart of making space for others."],["What I wish was better","I have loved my time attending Resonate, however I feel like I have one foot in and one foot out because I am unsure what stance the church has on certain issues. When I first started attending, I was amazed at how young the congregation was. I loved that political and current issues were discussed and I respect the middle ground approach where we can recognize the issues but still support everyone. When I have lightly discussed topics of sexuality and women’s roles in the church, I have been given a broad, we invite everyone in, no matter what response, which makes me think the underlying belief of the church is that men have the head of household, decisionmaking role, and a marriage is only between a man and a woman. I have previously worked at a Christian school that took a similar approach, and by addressing nothing head-on, they said very loudly that they don’t support these people. I was very\n\nsurprised to hear women were not allowed to preach at Resonate (and I would argue that at that time, Sarah was already preaching almost every week through her announcements, prayers, and encouragement. Although she wasn’t delivering the sermon, she was in a preaching role). I almost stopped attending at that time, but stuck it out when I heard it was a topic up for a vote. I am thrilled the church voted to allow women to preach, but until there is a female elder, women still don’t have a vote at the table, and that is troubling to me. I was ministered through confirmation by a female elder at my church. That had a deep impact on me as a young Christian woman, and I would not have had the same experience if I had been walked through that process by a man. I was raised in the Christian church, it is a very large part of my life. However, I also chose to marry a non-Christian and surround myself with believers and nonbelievers. I have seen friends enter same sex relationships and transition genders to come out so much happier and well-adjusted on the other side. I don’t see the devil in that, I see God in their journey. There have been times at Resonate where we were asked to interpret scripture through a modern lens, I ask that you consider doing that when it comes to women’s roles and sexuality as well. If Resonate does in fact support women’s rights and gay marriage, I think you should do it more loudly, because I have been attending for a few years and am still unsure of the stance. If you don’t support these issues, I would love to discuss why I do and why I think this forward-thinking approach can help grow the congregation further and better reflect the community we live in."],["What I wish was better","I wish we had more ways for new people to get connected before they are in a life group. It’s a big jump to go from being new to joining a life group."],["What I wish was better","Community events that give time and opportunity to get to know people more outside of the 5 minute Sunday morning hi. Retreats have helped this and community dinners after church, but finding ways to do men/women’s events that encourage building friendships outside of just life groups I think would be helpful."],["What I wish was better","I don’t believe women should preach from the pulpit"],["What I wish was better","I think I've said everything out loud."],["What I wish was better","I worry some people prefer to be a fuzzy church than a centered-set one. People are hesitant to correct or challenge one another, but chalk it up to different beliefs or convictions."],["What I wish was better","More elders but this is already being addressed and I understand it takes time to foster the relationship."],["What I wish was better","More operational skills and less elder attrition. We need more people to step up within leadership in different capacities.\nTaking care of the the actual building. Yikes."],["What I wish was better","Somehow I'm not really changing (growing). I show up, but deep down, real change doesn't happen in a place I've felt convicted should be.\nI'll put it this way. I've been wanting to grow in generosity for three years, and still feel like I haven't made any progress. And somehow, after being in the church for so long, I still feel this way. Seems easy to believe that says something about me, and an opportunity for church culture somehow."],["What I wish was better","I wish there were more frequent opportunities to do community service. It could be smaller volunteer groups during the week or weekend, but just more ways for us to use our time and effort to help others."],["What I wish was better","Resonate would do well to imitate its superlative staff in acknowledging everyone, particularly new attendees. Emphasis on the Trellis practice of Generosity in relational engagement outside one’s LifeGroup or close friend group would help."],["How we can improve","I think \"mentorship\" and \"discipleship\" can be vague or fuzzy concepts because people hold different operational definitions of what that looks like. If mentorship/discipleship were to occur primarily through informal relationships, how are we fostering intentionality and consistency? Is sharing and following up on prayer requests sufficient? If something more holistic than this is needed, how are we actively growing and training these skills within the body? What are scenarios for peer-level vs spiritual mothering/fathering needed? If those relationships don't naturally exist, then what?"],["How we can improve","Resonate is a church of former fundamentalists, who are glad not to be in such a legalistic environment, but we are not reaching many new converts. We have a difficult opportunity to reach both progressives in Decatur and Black nominal Christians with the gospel."],["How we can improve","I’d love to get more emails. Slack is not an intuitive way to stay connected."],["How we can improve","We love you all and are very grateful for Resonate. You have helped us grow in our faith and made us see Jesus in a way we hadn’t before. For this, we are forever grateful to all of the leadership over that last 8+ years."],["How we can improve","I can’t think of anything at the moment. I love so much about Resonate - honestly, don’t want it to change too much."],["How we can improve","Find a way to engage middle schoolers in their transition into the service. Especially the 6th grade year is a tough transition and finding ways to help them study the Bible and engage in community on Sunday morning would be helpful."],["How we can improve","Sharing the long term vision of the church. 10 years plus."],["How we can improve","There is a wonderful community. For someone like me there is still a bit of stigma to go into the prayer rooms. But I’d be totally willing sit and pray w/ anyone who’s asked me.\nI can’t pinpoint why I feel that way."],["How we can improve","We need bigger kid classrooms! We have so many kids coming on Sunday, which is amazing!, and it appears we may be outgrowing that space.\n\nDiversity! Would love to worship like heaven with people from every nation and tribe!"],["How we can improve","We often preach about our struggles and hardships. Its been a theme for many years and quite relevant since we all have them. But I think we can also begin to focus on the freedom that God has for us. How does the gospel transform us, really? This is personal for me because for many years I sat in LG circles where we all shared our struggles.\nWhile this was incredibly comforting, to be seen by my brothers and sisters, years would go by and we all stayed in those same places of pain with no breakthrough. I think its good to share our struggles with one another, but I think Resonate could benefit from hearing some powerful testimonies of freedom as well!"],["How we can improve","More parental participation (by rotation) in teaching Bible Classes and Shout.\nInteraction among LifeGroups to promote familiarity with broader group of attendees."],["How we can improve","We love Resonate and appreciate how the staff and leadership pours into the body.\nThe mens and womens retreat have been so wonderful. We'd love to see more opportunities to get together as a body (women, men) to build relationships in fun ways during the rest of the year. This has felt hard to do with people outside of our life group."],["How we can improve","Really review if we need a lead pastor."],["How we can improve","One of Resonate's strengths has been not shying away from hard questions. I appreciate that Chris Case would name the hard things (i.e. Christian nationalism, systemic racial injustice). I do think that there should be more time, space, energy given to having difficult theological discussions (i.e. our theological stance on women leadership, why do we use the CSB instead of the ESV, gender/sexuality). I recognize that this transition season in the life of Resonate isn't the time, but I think these discussions are necessary for shaping and equipping our church."],["How we can improve","More participation in community service and support by our body"],["How we can improve","I think encourage the congreation to seek out new people, and people they don't know. Even in passing. Just a nod \"hello\" goes a long way. Sometimes the congregation doesn't seem to notice new folks. I was blessed that I just so happened to meet Brent Davidsons and Mayos out the gate."],["How we can improve","I could certainly share more on why I think we should move towards an egalitarian belief but maybe I’ll send in an email"],["How we can improve","Keep the Holy Spirit coming baby!!!"],["How we can improve","Has anyone ever complained about slides on the TVs disappearing too quickly? I have no idea, could be my own attention issue or processing-time needs, but I feel like I've never been somewhere where so often by the time I look up to read (or re-read) something on the screen, it's already gone and back on the dang placeholder screen.\nJeez, what a small issue, eh? So in case a second person never chimes in with this same thought, well, then we've got just one guy who could do with more time marinating with an outline or quote slide than the same title screen all the time."],["How we can improve","I love the attempts to do more whole church activities like the all church lunches. It's a great way to connect with people from both services who I wouldn't often see or be able to connect with. However, doing them after second service is a really hard time frame for families with small kiddos as that is nap time. We have not been able to attend these for a while because of that. I know that some Life Groups meet on Sunday afternoons/evenings so there isn't a great time to do them but having them be later in the day on Sunday would make them more accessible for families with small children!\nOne last thing is Life Groups. I think to match with our model of disciples making disciples we should have a more regular group splitting/multiplying model. This would make some Life Groups feel less \"cliquey\" and grow relationships across the church. I know some people have found their tribe in their Life Group. But that can make it really difficult for new people joining because it feels hard to connect and integrate into the group when some people have such established relationships. If Life Groups regularly split when they reached 12 adults (for example), that would create opportunities for people to be involved with leading and facilitating new groups (everybody plays) and provide more opportunities for people to get connected in Life Groups and establish relationships across a broader group in the church."],["How we can improve","Provide more opportunities for global mission experience, for those who are curious or who would benefit from a short-term trip."],["How we can improve","Retaining and designating elders seems to be a struggle, although I know that Resonate does what it can to support the elders. ...Not sure how relevant this is, but multiple outsiders that I've brought to church over the past 10 years have commented on how young our elders are. It has crossed my mind too, especially recently knowing that Ryan Kellogg was only baptized a few years ago, and our elder policy says that they should not be new believers (although I'm not sure how to define \"new,\" and I\n\nmay be wrong about Ryan). I know that youth is not an indication of ability, and I've always had confidence in our elders. ...I've heard Resonate say before that anyone wishing to be an elder should express interest, but I'm curious whether other Resonate elders also approach potential new elders to suggest the idea? I perceive that there are multiple men in the congregation who would be willing, capable, and good elders, but they may be too humble or may feel more comfortable being approached about it rather than pursuing it... just an idea. Resonate may already approach men directly, so if so, then disregard."],["How we can improve","Put that open lot to some kind of use for the community. Maybe a kitchen for meals?"],["How we can improve","Not at this time."],["How we can improve","Nursing room needs a cleaning and could feel more welcoming"],["How we can improve","So many positives! Great staff and elders, leadership and teaching… so I’m not aware of needed improvement"],["How we can improve","I love seeing more college-aged/young adults consistently coming into service. How great would it be to have them connect with our youth as part of a mentorship/discipleship program. Praying about how this can come to fruition organically."],["How we can improve","Perhaps publish the song list for the next week's service, so we can practice during the week and come together as a chorus. How might resonate help its people get more comfortable discussing Christianity with Christian Nationalists."],["How we can improve","A little more emphasis on working out our salvation through personal holiness/sanctification"],["How we can improve","I do believe we can still grow in the area of “being a relational church.” (Of course, participation in a Life Group seems very important.). The church can always foster a culture where the participants take an interest in both outsiders and in those to whom they are not naturally drawn. Regular participants/attendees should not “feel” invisible…"],["How we can improve","Sometimes people gravitate toward their life groups and it can be hard to fit in if you aren’t sure you’re welcome. Feel bad for new and timid people."],["How we can improve","I do think that communication can always be better. Whether it's social media or another outlet, a lot of times things get missed because of how they are announced or communicated.\nI would like to see us be a church that helps one another evolve and transition through seasons and stages, with transparency, honesty and grace."],["How we can improve","The live stream is not reliable. We'd love to be able to watch when we are not able to join in person, but there are consistently technical issues that make it more of a turn off than a blessing. Not a big thing in the grand scheme, but if we are going to offer it, I'd love for it to be reliable. :)"],["How we can improve","Clearly state the gospel and the benefits of the gospel each week. It doesn't have to be the same boring statements, but part of the Word brought during sermons, especially.\nThere are probably many new people coming in who could not articulate the whole gospel of Christ."],["How we can improve","I wish we had more concrete ways to study Scripture together in Life Group. The questions that go with the reading plan don’t always get to the heart of what we are studying and don’t always lend themselves to diving into Scripture as much as talking about how we all feel about the passage. I miss talking about sermons in Life Group."],["How we can improve","I appreciate how much thought has gone into previous position/white papers, but I think it would be helpful for partners to also hear all the perspectives and reasons behind how these position/white papers have been concluded - some of this might be intellectual curiosity on my part, but I think it would be beneficial to us all to have a deeper understanding for how our core convictions have been described/outlined."],["How we can improve","Mostly shared above. We love Resonate and pray for us as a body moving forward. I’m still on the fence with the idea of women preaching even though we love and respect Sarah so much."],["How we can improve","Nothing else comes to mind"],["How we can improve","I am just not sure that our elder structure is effective in doing what they are appointed to do. The lay elders are spread too thin, have an incredibly high attrition rate, and the ones who finish their terms are burnt out. The lay elders lack perspective on the day-today life of the church. The lay and paid elders also often lack perspective of those who are not like them (i.e. women, people of color, those of lower SES, Resonate Kids\n\nvolunteers, etc.). Their time is spent putting out fires, writing papers, voting, and showing up for meetings -- not prayer and ministry of the word, which Acts 6 instructs.\nI am not sure what the alternative solution is, but I have doubts that this form of governance will ever be effective or successful at Resonate. I think it has been overanalyzed and over-disected and we miss out on celebrating and learning from the example of the more mature in the faith because of it. I would love these men to have time and space to give their lives to growing up in Christ, devoting themselves to prayer and Scripture, and letting that flow out of their lives in a way that all of the church can look to as an example."],["How we can improve","I would love to see a place to recommend songs for worship on Sundays. I'm not sure if this is something that already exists. But there is a multitude of great worship songs out there that would be so fun to sing as a group on Sundays."],["How we can improve","I feel like the life group structure is still a bit of a struggle for me. It is a lot of work to make it happen, and hard to build community that is genuinely transparent with such a large group."],["How we can improve","We need an actual discipleship program. As I said, we have a lot of biblical knowledge in our congregation, but we do not have the apparatus to support individuals to really mature in their faith and grow in intimate and vulnerable relationships. Life groups could be this for some people. But I would say that most life groups do not function this way. It would be much easier to support spiritual growth in a group of 3-4 versus life groups of 12-20+ people. Ultimately, I think Sunday Morning, bible studies, life groups, and small discipleship groups are what we need in terms of spaces/programming. And we need to decide what we want out of each of these spaces to design the best approach/curriculum."],["How we can improve","Nothing I can think of at the moment."],["How we can improve","Other than the few points I’ve brought up, it really is a fantastic church."],["How we can improve","It would be great to be more transparent and honest with the naming of the life groups.\nmaybe instead of area names it was something more realistic to what was occurring.\ne.g. \"Young family group meeting in tucker\" or \"Young professionals in decatur\" The vision of having people across all different life stages in a LG is beautiful and I would love to see that come to fruition. I don't love people being grouped together in\n\nlife stages, but in reality, that is what our LG currently look like. And to state that they are location specific feels dishonest and hurtful.\nIf our vision is really to meet with people in our geographical areas, then I think a whole life group reconstruction would have to occur across every life group. I am highly doubtful any group would be amendable to this, which I understand. If i had a bunch of best friends I had been in life group with for 10 years, i wouldn't want that either...But what do we do about everybody else who would benefit from being in a community centered group ? I don't have the answer. I think we need to spend time figuring out what the actual goal of the groups are and then figure out how to fix the problems. we have been at resonate for a long time and have never been able to see LG done the way resonate proclaims it is on Sunday mornings through promotional videos and testimonies."],["How we can improve","Try to nail the sermons down a bit more in the interim. (Shorter, more concise)"],["10-year vision","In ten years, I would like to see:"],["10-year vision","a broader team of volunteers, so some of our most faithful get a break in rotation"],["10-year vision","discipleship / mentoring for older children"],["10-year vision","an expectation of regular attenders to give back to staff in terms of times and talents on their initiatives."],["10-year vision","I would envision it being a mid-size church that has a lot of community partners who want Resonate to join them in their mission because the people at Resonate are impactful, considerate, and well off enough to donate their time/resources to it.\nLikewise, Resonate would be considered the gold-standard of how to maintain strong relationships with the congegration while not making church feel too big for those who are new."],["10-year vision","Kids who came to faith and stick with the church!\nOur church would reflect our neighborhood We would plant another location We would be known as a place people can come for physical and spiritual help. For resources and for prayer"],["10-year vision","Church planting outside of the city/country in places that are in need of hearing and experiencing the love of Jesus."],["10-year vision","Belvedere Plaza is demolited and afforable housing and business’s move in. We serve the community in the direct area. Also we buy the property behind us(the motel) and us it to serve the homeless or as a halfway house for sobriety. The church plants a church in Clarkston, one in Stone Mountain, one in Tucker. We baptize more adults than kids.\nWe become multigenerational, multicultural and multi economically diverse church."],["10-year vision","Having a huge community impact with hopefully some established facilities or outreaches set in place to serve the underserved in our community, much like we have seen with some of the organizations with which we partner. Impactful prayer, healing\n\n(emotional, mental, spiritual), freedom from worldly influence, and the darker spiritual influences around us. A maturing body of believers who walk in unity with joy, so others can see our love for each other is genuine becaue of Jesus."],["10-year vision","A place where my kids want to continue to be a part of the congregation and start their own life groups."],["10-year vision","A community that is strong in the Word and growing in love for both its people and the surrounding neighborhoods. A church that doesn’t only work as its own church but partners with other believers and churches in Atlanta, expanding the vision of the GLOBAL church and God’s kingdom on earth."],["10-year vision","I would dream to see greater diversity in the church as far people who didn’t know Him previously now part of our congregation because they came to know Him through Resonate. And, would love to see more global partners too."],["10-year vision","Sprouting church plants from our body. Larger and more demonstrative community outreach program. Perhaps a non profit brand off with that specific intention. Building out the space more in that open lot to support outreach. Showers? Food/kitchen?\nHousing?"],["10-year vision","For resonate to be part of the local community and serve it well. We do great with local outreach hour. It would be great to see us branch out to serve the nearby community and serve their needs."],["10-year vision","Supporting maybe 20 more families who want to foster, having a fair or some sort of monthly gathering where the homeless can come and get a hair cut, get new clothes for a job interview, receive financial education of some sort and maybe have other organizations that will come together to help meet some of the needs. (Rehab for substance abuse? Counselors? Prayer and delivery?)"],["10-year vision","In 10 years, I envision Resonate continuing to operate in it's current building (expanded) with a more vibrant community ministry operation. I envision more diversity within the Resonate body. I envision a TON of middle and high school youth, and a lot more college students, most of whom are actively involved in the life and mission of the church. I envision a church plant from Resonate operating in Clarkston to be able to focus more directly on the refugee population that we care so deeply about. I envision that church plant also being very diverse."],["10-year vision","In 10 years, I hope Resonate can plant another church. I hope we can also grow in diversity (e.g., income level, race, disability). I hope we have triple the number of LGs."],["10-year vision","Resonate is multiplying LifeGroups across the city with more groups every year. Some of those LifeGroups organize to plant new churches in target neighborhoods. Resonate is developing elders and leaders to send out to these church plants and the mission field. Our church better reflects our neighborhood with more Black elders, leaders, staff, and partners. New believers are baptized almost every week, and deeply discipled through relationships. Partnerships with a day shelter, career training, transitional housing, and permanent supportive housing allow us to guide neighbors in homelessness to stability. Our church is known for its generosity to neighbors in need of utility and rental assistance. Kids and teens from the neighborhood spend time in our building after school and feel they belong here. After school, ESL class, Alpha, and other outreaches give partners opportunities to meet neighbors and make disciples from the lost."],["10-year vision","Not knowing what Resonates boldest aspirations area…. But I would dream of a thriving church active in reaching and serving the Decatur area…. Our neighbors"],["10-year vision","We would like to see a more robust and exciting youth group for our Littles to grow into."],["10-year vision","I think it would be great to have a more developed play area or space to congregate for hosting dinners or community gatherings.\nEven more community members in the direct neighborhood."],["10-year vision","Maybe expanding into other communities and raising up pastors to lead those churches"],["10-year vision","Everyone at Resonate knows at least one way they are blessed to serve the kingdom and community and they do so regularly with joy and for God's glory. In this way the kingdom thrive around the resonate community as the lost and Church experience the gospel in word and deed, in love and power, leading to repentance and growth through grace."],["10-year vision","Resonate would stay relatively small and homey. There would be defined ministry groups apart from life groups. Ministry groups would actually be out on mission together weekly or bi-weekly. We would be pushed to engage with and invite non believes to join us for meals and events."],["10-year vision","increase in disciple making by measurement of friends and neighbors being brought into our fellowship. Deepened focus on being missional (local and global)."],["10-year vision","1 church 10 x the size, 1,400+ life groups."],["10-year vision","Even more kids :) Actually some form of school or even home school co-op support would be an incredible asset to the congregation.\nLogistically I see us in a new building or expanding our current one in 10 yrs time."],["10-year vision","I've never been much this kind of thinker, and used to feel a great deal of pressure to be more of one.\nThese days I'm at peace with what my gifts aren't.\nAnd I've probably downright soured on words like 'impact' and would personally spend my prayers and sweat and tears replicating (for a recent example, again) Men's Retreatlevel depth and connection and let the Spirit drive the bus from there.\nWhich isn't to say I don't appreciate my more visionary friends and leaders. I do. Up until and not including the point that we start hiring narcissists, who have the VERY best-sounding, boldest aspirations for impact and reach."],["10-year vision","I pray that Resonate establishes itself as a pillar in the Belvedere/South Decatur neighborhood for those living on the margins in the area. Especially as the area gentrifies, I think we could be a place that those who might be pushed out or away can go for resources and community."],["10-year vision","This is a good question. It's tempting to think about things that are \"bigger and better\" (like a bigger building, more members, or a big online presence) but I'm not sure that's necessarily the best thing to aim for.\nInstead, what comes to mind is multiplying impact through individuals, in ways that extend beyond our community. Maybe that looks like planting 1-2 new churches or helping launch some faith driven businesses and nonprofits. Maybe it could look like members being community builders in their neighborhoods or workplaces. It would be amazing to see more people coming to know Christ, and to see that happen through lay people.\nI also think about the intangibles—the things that are hard to measure. Is there a way for us to grow in contentedness, joyfulness, generosity, etc? Is there a way for us to grow more comfortable being a community that welcomes people from all backgrounds, races, and classes?\n\nI'd probably need more time to think about what I think the next 10 years would could look like, but hopefully those thoughts help."],["10-year vision","I would love to see the theological understanding and scriptural knowledge grown in our people. I think we have done that for a while but would love to see more leaders raised up and their giftings sharpened. Those lead to teach, taught to teach.\nEvangelists sharpened in sharing and meeting neighbors. Musicians sharpened and used."],["10-year vision","I see our church being a cornerstone of the community with a growing congregation that includes diversity and people from all walks of life. The primary attendees are young, white, traditional (male/female) families. It doesn't reflect the diversity of the greater community Resonate is in. If we can reflect the community we live in, I think we can grow even more."],["10-year vision","I think there is a huge cultural gap for churches that are welcoming and a place where the churched, the unchurched, and the de-churched can come, ask questions, and be safe. In our community we've seen a high percentage of people leave the church due to church hurt and hypocrisy, and many are still seeking a community that they can't find.\nWe've heard so many people express they want a church community that is friendly, real, welcomes everyone, challenges them spiritually & scripturally, and actually lives their values. We were attracted to Resonate because their values & scriptural teaching didn't avoid the challenges of our time. Going forward, I hope that Resonate continues to meet the gaps of the faith community in this cultural moment and is able to continuously plot a unique and people-loving way forward."],["10-year vision","Even more outreach, really living close to our neighbors and having them attend service regularly. More partners sent to reach the nations, and providing a debrief and welcome home space for them coming back."],["10-year vision","-Increased youth group size and presence -Increased ministry to college and professional students -Increased opportunity for church led missions trips -Church wide memorization of scripture -Cross generational mentoring and discipleship -Likely needing a bigger space or two services"],["10-year vision","I think Resonate would have a larger number of community attendees as well as more age diversity among members."],["10-year vision","A church where both the pastoral staff and the other believers have an active (vibrant) faith that is very relational and is focused on reaching the non-believers in the community."],["10-year vision","I haven’t thought about this much so I’m kind of shooting from the hip but I think it would be cool to see Resonate continue growing and developing in their being a place for the broken and weary. We definitely do this practically through Monday lunch outreach and other ways we try to engage Belvedere park but what if this grew?"],["10-year vision","I've learned over the years that I'm more of an \"Executer\" than a \"Visionary Leader\" (a class I took with Sarah!), but my vision would be for a thriving and unified church with a highly organized children's program, adult programs that draw us deeper into God's Word and create meaningful relationships (with each other and with God), and outreach programs that transform the hearts and lives of the people around us."],["10-year vision","A place where people don't feel the need to join groups like Alcoholics Anonymous anymore. Life groups simply are that safe, and yet the Spirit's conviction is strong, so lives change."],["10-year vision","I think the love and unity between people who are very different in every sense would be drawing people who do not know Christ to come and see. The testimonies of how people experienced God in their lives--healing, freedom, and redemption--would be a foretaste of heaven."],["10-year vision","To be a church that worships in SPIRIT and TRUTH! Solid bible teaching pared with the leading of the holy spirit in all things. I have no idea where that can take us in 10 years, but I think if we come into submission to Christ and His leading of our church, we will land exactly where we are supposed to be."],["10-year vision","Small. Same location. Continuing to grow its impact locally and globally, rather than trying to grow into a mega church."],["10-year vision","sorry, I don't have feedback for this"],["10-year vision","A support to our Belvedere community. having regular gatherings at church and in partners homes with those in our area. Severing them. loving them, building relationships with them. I see us on our way, and it's so beautiful."],["10-year vision","A thriving engagement with our local community participating daily in the life of our church with our building being used every day for missional work. weekly connections with our mission partners around the globe. full wednesday bible studies on a variety of topics. seeing the spirit work wildly through prayer on a daily basis in the lives of our congregation. weekly baptisms and attendance by a signifcant group of seekers who long for hope in this lost world. A strong team of elders and LT members with a deep pipeline for leader development and discipleship"],["10-year vision","All of the current programs bolstered."],["10-year vision","In 10 years we have a very healthy and robust leadership/elder team that matches the size of the church, to where any partner feels very pastorally cared for and heard. In 10 years we've hopefully planted 1-2 other churches, and would practically have to do that because we've steadily grown so much."],["10-year vision","Following after Jesus, with a diverse group of people of all ages and backgrounds, (including older generations).\nHonestly, I don’t know what the church’s vision or aspiration for impact and reach are now. I don’t know that we’ve quantified what that kind of impact would look like."],["10-year vision","2 to 3 new church plants. Belvedere location to be more reflecting of the Belvedere Park area."],["10-year vision","Connectedness as a family and growth in Biblical literacy deeply impacting individual lives, families, workplaces, and the neighbors of global partners as well!"],["10-year vision","I would love to hear that Resonate is a positive resource for the community. Not just in spiritual aspects, but practical as well. That we stand for justice, truth, etc."],["10-year vision","I see Resonate becoming a Church that is even more deeply living out its mission. I see the community of 40 somethings now making the conscious efforts to stepping into intentional discipling relationships of those in their 20s. I see us possibly planting a church, not as a satellite, but one that is it's own healthy community with a unique vision and calling. I see a church that is more deeply invested in the the local community."],["10-year vision","I don't see us changing as far as our heart and the culture we have seen the Lord continue to create. Our authenticity and love for others is something that I hope continues to grow deeper year by year and continues to make us unique. I would love for our church to look like the community we live in and for us to be willing to step into opportunities for the sake of those in our community. I want to be a church who sees\n\nthe lost come to Jesus. A lot of our church growth seems to come from church transfers and a smaller percentage from someone who has no faith at all or have never stepped inside a church."],["10-year vision","Resonate has a tremendous amount of children at the moment and one thing that I would personally love to see is the growth of the children’s ministry to young adult ministry that can impact generations to come."],["10-year vision","I think it would probably be located somewhere else. Being located in the shopping center seems to limit reach and might turn off some potential partners. Also, if we are going to be there, we need to find a way to utilize the outside space. It feels like a prison yard."],["10-year vision","As church and members grow, church planting in a different location and keep multiply as a separate but same church."],["10-year vision","Unknown at the moment"],["10-year vision","Healthy life groups. Integrating neighbors of Belvedere park. Communities that reflect a number of ages, backgrounds grounds, cultures. Double the missionaries we support.\nTales of miracles that only God could do that we asked for faithfully.\nYouth a part of the church. Taking leadership roles."],["10-year vision","The body is even more multi racial and multi socio economic levels. We have no need and the community around us knows we will serve them."],["10-year vision","I could see more locations, more global partners, a thriving youth group, even more healthy life groups, and loving our community(ies) well. The impact would be exponential and vast."],["10-year vision","I am very new to this church so it is hard for me to answer this question. But I hope and pray that Resonate will become a missional church where both rich and poor, educated and not educated, us citizens and undocumented, able and disabled can co-exist and flourish together in Christ."],["10-year vision","I would want to see Resonate become a community that embraces the unique racial and socioeconomic context and ultimately bring glory to Christ in creating a space where people from every background and race can worship the living Christ."],["10-year vision","This is a difficult question for me, because I'm so new. I have a hard time imagining this."],["10-year vision","Healed from this season. More life groups with margin for new people. Increased diversity. Tangible discipleship at our core. Clear track to help houseless community"],["10-year vision","I'd love to see more church plants due to healthy growth that includes a large number of people from the surrounding area, ethnically and socioeconomically reflecting the area around us joyfully."],["10-year vision","My hope is that Resonate will be a light in the city. That people from all walks of life feel welcomed and know that they are created in God’s image. I hope for continued and increasing support for missions in the city and globally."],["10-year vision","I would love to see Resonate grow to be a church that is full of people who don't feel like they \"fit\" in other churches. A place where all people are welcomed and feel like they have a place where they belong."],["10-year vision","When Resonate Church prays, God moves mightily. Our commitment to deep, diverse community stands out to those who see us. Our kids are taking ownership of their faith and leading us in opportunities to serve our neighbors and schools."],["10-year vision","We join a denomination or network that we feel closely tied to and supported by. We continue to be grounded in Scripture - equipping people to go out and live missionary lives in their Mon-Sat contexts. We develop more leaders and teachers within the church - actively investing in discipling and developing them. We expand and develop the courtyard space so that it's more functional. We are an important presence in the Belvedere Park neighborhood. I'd like to see us be more collaborative with other churches in the city - shared teaching/preaching, programming, volunteer/service opportunities, activism around social justice issues."],["10-year vision","--I think we would still be in Belvedere Park, but with an outreach ministry that extends far beyond Mondays. Daily meals and maybe even a residential facility?? That might take more than ten years, but that is the dream.\n--We would still have the pastor we hired this time, providing longterm stability and continuity of leadership.\n--We would have a larger and more diverse team of leaders (not thinking of the current LT structure specifically, but just the overall group of leaders). Probably additional staff leadership roles, like an executive pastor and full time roles for outreach and youth discipleship.\n--Many of the same LG's would still exist and with the same core groups, allowing true \"life on life\" friendships that have grown deep, intermingled roots.\n--One or more LG's develops into a new church plant (in Clarkston?)\n\n--Shout has regular attendees from the surrounding neighborhood --We would have raised up and sent out additional global partners -- And just for fun, the courtyard turned into a sustainable garden and prayer/contemplation space."],["10-year vision","Still the same size but deeper impact. A safe place in the community, a staple for certain needs, and internally where relationships thrive - doing lots of life together."],["10-year vision","Maybe in a bigger space but in the same area. Not a mega church but still very much identified as we are now. With more families and individuals from the Belvedere Park area who have come to know and love Jesus. Also with a more diverse feel. We are already heading in that direction. 😁"],["10-year vision","To enhance our willingness to show up intentionally for people with disabilities and foster care support"],["10-year vision","More connected to the community."],["10-year vision","Pastoral care and discipleship would be prominent in our body. We would all have someone ahead of us and behind us that we are connected to and spending time in the word with. Families would be strengthened spiritually as a whole.\nWe would be boldly proclaiming the gospel in our neighborhoods, schools, etc. and be a bridge for people that don't know the Lord."],["10-year vision","-Deeper local community engagement around church and in Clarkston -Increased multigenerational church partners -More elders (and rotation) -More expository teaching of Scripture -Deeper trellis practices among partners"],["10-year vision","My vision for Resonate Church would be to grow in size, specifically attendees that live in the immediate area, and that our kids' ministry would be strong and solid. I want Resonate to be a place where people can come together after living as a follower of Christ through uncomfortable days in our world and find solace, companionship and accountability."],["10-year vision","To have a church plant in that time."],["10-year vision","I would love for our church to... (1) look more like our neighborhood -- SES and racial makeup, (2) Be more expressive/charismatic in our worship and prayer, (3) allow women to become pastors (i.e. become egalitarian), (4) have planted at least one church"],["10-year vision","Same culture but scaled. 100% of attendees and partners in a life group"],["10-year vision","I would like to see resonate continuing to send partners to missions overseas but also to plant a church in our city. Atlanta is largely secular and unreached and is full of great needs- the greatest being the gospel."],["10-year vision","Making a more significant impact on the surrounding neighborhoods and community. I haven't given this much thought to really specify, but I'm a believer in quality over quantity; so my hope is that we continue to be faithful stewards in the areas God has given us."],["10-year vision","I imagine spin offs from the church, other plants. I imagine a very large youth program since we have so many kids. And due to our location, I expect our population to become more diverse, which is a good thing. I think that Resonate should focus on it's people, and as long as it does, should continue strong for the next decade."],["10-year vision","Honestly I’m not sure. To be honest, I don’t think I will be at Resonate in 10 years due to one reason or another (job moves, different seasons, etc) so I’m not sure if I can answer this question with the right posture"],["10-year vision","I would love for it to continue being the \"smaller\" church that it is, with a core group of folks but with people who have come and gone and built strong relationships here and can remember they can come back to (whether they be missionaries or college students). I want it to be known as \"home\" for hundreds, thousands, who remember a sweet time here. I want it to be renowned for being a safe space for vulnerable communities, and a place that launches people (from those communities) for the kingdom and for the better."],["10-year vision","Church plant sent from Resonate. More missionary partners sent. More and different lay-led ministries supported by Resonate but largely driven by non-staff. Another durable program like 2 Year Bible."],["10-year vision","I envision a modest sized church continuing the hard work of daily discipleship. I see a church devoted to the direct community around them- supplying resources, aid, and encouragement to the underprivileged and people experiencing homelessness in the Belvedere neighborhood. I see a church that lives countercultural lives in every respect.\n\nI see a church that protests against oppression and speaks up against violence. I see a church that is the hands of feet of Jesus- looking to the downcast and forgotten and lifting them up onto the highest seats in heaven."],["10-year vision","Honestly not sure. I hope we are continuing to raise up godly men and women who can disciple others. I hope the Word of God continues to guide and shape us and that our positions are rooted deeply in Scripture. I'd love to see stability among our leadership and to see our church staff grow."],["10-year vision","“Dreaming Big”, Resonate Church would be supporting the same number of Local Missionaries to the “unchurched” population in ATL as it does in foreign countries, having the identity of an impact church meeting needs of the “the least of these”."],["10-year vision","A bigger area for the kids and youth group to meet and have shared experiences."],["10-year vision","I’m curious to see what will come of our youth program"],["10-year vision","I'd love to see a group of people who are even more rooted in the Word and marked by compassion for our neighbors. I want the kids in our body to grow up as lovers of Christ. I hope we welcome many more partners but are still around the same size, due to missionaries being sent and a church plant."],["10-year vision","I would love to see more diversity at Resonate. We do great outreach with our neighborhood, but we don’t have many neighbors in service on Sunday. We’re still largely young, middle class and white families. It would be great if we better represented our neighborhood.\nIn 10 years, I would love for our body expand our outreach programs. I am not concerned with growing in numbers. Only in depth and in inclusion."],["10-year vision","I think we should have a capital campaign in the next 5 years so we can expand the building. A gymnasium would be great, but perhaps a larger room for worship as the church grows. Ultimately, I think that we are going to grow out of the space we are in relatively soon - especially for the kids. I also think the building should be used for additional programming (internal or partnership) during the week. Homeschool co-ops, afterschool programs, etc."],["10-year vision","Welcoming space in the current parking lot whether it's building out and/or a courtyard.\nPlant another church it we're called to do so. Or maybe it's being more connected to already existing local churches more than planting."],["10-year vision","In 10 years, hopefully Christ will have returned. If not, the urban network of Atlanta and the State of Georgia will have come to Christ through the God's movement through the souls of Resonate and those they have impacted through Him."],["10-year vision","Workplace ministry for leaders/executives Youth Philanthropy training (youth get to give grants, review applications, site visits, prayer, serve, fund)"],["10-year vision","I love the idea of providing for the community in practical ways. I would love to be able to use our space in the community to do much more for our neighbors or build a new space that serves the community well."],["Practical next steps","We need better women’s/mens/youth ministries that are interesting for us to bring our friends to."],["Practical next steps","Partnerships with missionaries like the Maxwell’s.\nContinuing VBS and possibly other community events."],["Practical next steps","Deacons of certain areas could be a really intentional way allow certain people to champion new areas."],["Practical next steps","-Hire a lead pastor with a missional vision for shaping Resonate as a community that loves God and serves others.\n-All of this will take money, so there is a need for growth there, whether through increased giving or external partnerships.\n-Pursue/increase partnerships with other likeminded churches in the area -Many of the initiatives are already in place, so it's more about growing and improving what has already been started."],["Practical next steps","sorry, I don't have feedback for this"],["Practical next steps","Not sure. I think we need to hear that vision first."],["Practical next steps","Draw deep in Him, getting wide will come from that."],["Practical next steps","Yes! Partnership with schools, neighboring churches! Offering a full spectrum of serving the hurting/lost"],["Practical next steps","Greater use of facilities for:\n- Meetings of faith-based Substance Disorder Recovery Programs (Celebrate Recovery/Most Excellent Way) LifeGroup monthly assignments for:\n- Food Bank & Homeless Shelter partnerships - Adult education & jobcorp roles"],["Practical next steps","As our middle and high school youth group continues to grow (exponentially), there is going to be less and less space for them to be involved in serving on Sunday mornings\n\nso needing to think creatively about how to continue to involve them in the life of the church and disciple them effectively.\nI think with that, we may need to think about a building initiative to add additional classrooms. I think this would be helpful for more dedicated space for adult classes but also to have larger and more separate spaces for the middle and high school youth.\nExpanding from there, as our youth continue to move through middle and high school and into college, I think we are going to need to be a lot more intentional with our college students. It's one thing when we are their church while they're away at school.\nIt's another thing when we are their home church where they return to. I think having both gives us a unique opportunity to have a consistent and vibrant college ministry that can operate all year long. Creating a dedicated space and separate programming for college students would be really impactful, while also continuing to invite them into the regular life and rhythms of the church, such as Life Groups.\nFor the church planting, I think considering offering some classes and training on church planting to the church during Wednesday evenings, Sunday mornings, or through a remote class might be really helpful to get people thinking and praying in that direction."],["Practical next steps","With a church full of young children, find community engagement or outreach activities that all family members can participate in together, and that has a regular cadence, of at least once a month."],["Practical next steps","getting Life groups more actively missional. preparing them to plant, share, and live life with others to spread the gospel intentionally."],["Practical next steps","I would like to see the church more connections with local ministries for the unhoused. I would also like to see a more robust prayer ministry grounded in intercessory prayer. I would love to also see the church become even more invested in adoption and foster care. I feel like that has fallen off a bit. As for programs, I don't see many new programs, but I do think our pastoral needs will increase as the community gets older"],["Practical next steps","Have a class/interest meeting for those interested in church planting. Encourage a group to meet and plan to start a church plant.\nBuild upon current outreach efforts to reach and invite our neighbors. Encourage partners and regular attenders to invite their neighbors to LG and church.\nTrain LifeGroups to multiply."],["Practical next steps","Plug into our local neighborhoods more.\nContinue to expand kids and youth programs.\nContinue learning and growing as a community."],["Practical next steps","I'm not engaged enough with what Resonate is doing to really speak into this, but my guess would be more connection points with other local groups to serve needs."],["Practical next steps","Raise up more leaders for life groups. Teach and encourage healthy “good byes” (a natural requirement to split and multiply groups)"],["Practical next steps","Living in community with our community, which many of our partners already do. Be the church that people want to visit because they've heard from other visitors that it feels like home. This could look like every day invites to colleagues, strangers, unbelieving neighbors. Partnering closely with already established orgs (like Clarkston Cares) who walk people through life (from not having a home to stability and flourishing). Then sharing those stories and hearing and spreading their testimonies."],["Practical next steps","I love the Monday outreach program, I definitely think that’s an awesome step in the right direction. And loved hearing about youth being able to come play basketball also awesome. Maybe more things like that, or faithfully doing those things. Maybe more or continuing partnerships with organizations around Resonate to the community.\nAs far as global partners, love that we do M week with the kids - maybe continuing that and doing a little with the adults? Or hosting a Perspectives?"],["Practical next steps","Continuing to develop our outreach structure is important. There are so many needs in our community and I celebrate that we can open our doors, have a meal, and build friendship with others but I would love for there to be a day when we didn't have to point others to some other organization for basic needs like clothing, food, and practical needs like jobs/housing/etc.\nContinuing to expand the reach of our Life Groups is important so that others can step into deep community with us a church. Continuing to pour into our leaders of our groups and raising others up must continue to be an intentional process that we are continuing to make progress in. I would love for there to never be a wait for people to step into a group and that can only be accomplished by discipling and empower others to lead and shepherd others well."],["Practical next steps","Learn from partner programs in the community. Encourage greater involvement with these partners with increased opportunities to serve. Continue hosting representatives\n\nfrom foster care, those serving the underserved, missionaries, etc. to envision our church body in practical ways to serve, learn, and grow. Prayer, prayer, prayer. We can do nothing with God moving and directing and empowering."],["Practical next steps","Having more community involved events, especially in/around the church building to familiarize community members with the physical location of the church. It would be good if these events had church member involvement too, so we could all really interact with one another. Additionally, asking members of different age groups to give ideas for how to best support their demographic would be useful. I also think that having a more defined mentorship 'program' for young adults to be mentored by older believers would be beneficial."],["Practical next steps","More Serve Sundays along with a team with process and programs to help manage, plan and implement."],["Practical next steps","local outreach! the work Noah and the team has done on monday has just been amazing and I'd love to see the church live into that more and really support that ministry. i beleive it would be such a powerful story and opportunity to draw in other nonbelivers with a more progressive mindset who are jaded by a church that doesn't live out what it says. See also my prior comment about LBGTQ engagement and care."],["Practical next steps","We'll need a stable group of elders leading over the years, continue with a solid teaching team, most likely have a strong lead pastor who owns and carries vision for the church, healthy staff covering all ministry areas, partners who are all tithing regularly."],["Practical next steps","Keep up our volunteer opportunities in the local community."],["Practical next steps","Continuing community outreach. Emphasizing an open, loving, safe, and accepting environment for all to attract a greater diversity of community members."],["Practical next steps","First, by studying the Bible and see how the Gospel overcome socioeconomic conditions and learning how uncomfortable yet rewarding it is to break such socioeconomic walls.\nSecond, by intentionally building relationships with people from different cultures and values.\nThird, … I can’t think anymore ;)"],["Practical next steps","Encourage life groups to consider what it would look like to join a few other groups in church planting."],["Practical next steps","When I’ve seen churches grow and expand their reach, it’s because they stand firm on the Word and aren’t afraid to say the things that are offensive but true. The Gospel by nature should be offensive to the world, and speaking truth with grace is key. So many people are searching for truth in a world that says truth is subjective."],["Practical next steps","Possible mission trips in the USA and internationally"],["Practical next steps","Partnering with some organizations, maybe funding from companies?"],["Practical next steps","Time for fun together! Camping, hikes, events beyond the men and women’s retreat.\nContinued opportunities for people to dig deep into the Scriptures outside of Sunday and LG."],["Practical next steps","See above I think multiplying into other areas is better than making the one church bigger and then having to move"],["Practical next steps","Partner with Will Francis and the Gathering to meet tangible needs through special events Develop a Benevolence policy that uses our resources to build relationships with those in need outside of our church Identify and disciple new elders and leaders, especially people of color Develop a multiplying plan for LifeGroups to train new leaders and multiply regularly Create partnerships with Frontline, Dekalb County's new day shelter, and other organizations Learn from Peace Baptist Church's after school program and consider applying for Georgia's 21st Century Grant to launch our own Start an ESL class and Financial Literacy class to benefit neighbors Start Alpha groups to invite searching and skeptics to consider Jesus Identify target neighborhoods to plant new churches someday"],["Practical next steps","We need older men to disciple the 40 year olds."],["Practical next steps","RAM medical trailers, they provide healthcare to folks for free no insurance necessary.\nWe could provide Medical and dental or vision care through a partnership with them."],["Practical next steps","Again, this is just a really tough one for me because I haven't been here enough time."],["Practical next steps","-Continue to engage community with \"The Gathering\" -Continue to build community partners in Clarkston\n\n-Offer more guided silent retreats -Continue with mens/womens retreats"],["Practical next steps","Relationships and resources from other churches and organizations doing similar work."],["Practical next steps","Either find a new building in a more residential type area in an old church building, or raise money to improve our outdoor space."],["Practical next steps","More outreach opportunities in the Belvedere Park area, Afterschool programs, hosting social justice and racial reconciliation events."],["Practical next steps","Continuing summer kids camp with an emphasis on inviting local kids. I love the Monday lunch ministry and wish I could be part of it. More outreach in the area and ways of patterning with ministries that reach the population here, like The Gathering."],["Practical next steps","AA/other celebrate recovery groups, grief groups, hosting classes/conferences that tend to people in their own brokenness, as well as train people who don’t experience that specific kind of brokenness. Could we be an outpost for any organizations in the city/surrounding area that work with homelessness, abuse, job less, etc?"],["Practical next steps","- An annual Resontate volunteer day across the city (or within Dekalb) - Music outreach program - Advertising for more people to join LifeGroups even if you're not a member at Resonate - Helping other churches set up a Life Group structure that feels intimate and connected with others"],["Practical next steps","I think we have the bible teaching/studying in a solid place. So i think a prayer ministry would create a good balance. Also a place to start equipping the saints in gifts of the spirit."],["Practical next steps","Groups/programming for moms. Partnership with local businesses."],["Practical next steps","What a great question - I need to think about this more but here are some initial thoughts.\nI love how much we are focused on reaching the community around church and think this is really important. I also don't want us to miss the community already within the church. I see a need for more direct pastoral care for men, women and families as a whole. This strengthens the body and hopefully strengthens our outreach outside of the body too. Maybe we think about ways to make this a reality... families connected with\n\nan elder or a staff member(s). Our life group supports this but it's important to have this from leadership too, as it's a different dynamic."],["Practical next steps","A difficult question 😊… The church can seek out and learn from others who do this well. Resonate, under a new leadership team, will have to seek God’s favor and direction for how to have the most fruitful impact."],["Practical next steps","Find ways to worship and serve with other believers in Atlanta.\nCommunity garden- bring beautiful to the concrete jungle- maybe give opportunities for struggling neighbors to take part?"],["Practical next steps","Starting small, I would say at the very least asking people in lifegroups each week how they shared the gospel that week. That challenge alone each week would be greatly impactful.\nGrowing the outreach department of the church to include emphasis on more than just the people in Belvedere park but also the unreached.\nHave classes and workshops on sharing the gospel. A lot of people don’t share their faith because they don’t know how to. Being taught how to share and practicing it is important for feeling comfortable.\nDevelop a discipleship program. Disciples make disciples and multiplication is the fastest way to the way to reach more people. But people aren’t truly being discipled."],["Practical next steps","1. Join a network or denomination. Really utilize the resources available for leadership development of the elders, staff, XLT. Sometimes we don't know what we don't know, and we need resources outside of Resonate to help us."],["Practical next steps","Hire the right Lead Pastor for us who is a visionary leader, who cares about discipleship and leadership development, who is collaborative."],["Practical next steps","Grow our partnerships/relationships with neighboring churches or organizations."],["Practical next steps","Look for grants/funding for developing the courtyard space."],["Practical next steps","Ordain more elders, invite and develop people to do more to serve the church."],["Practical next steps","City council or neighborhood engagements. More LG service events in neighborhoods."],["Practical next steps","partner with other local churches, continue hosting volunteer events, medical outreach clinics, creating blessing bags."],["Practical next steps","Being a hub for the community. Parenting classes, resources for navigating government assistance and immigration. Ministry for seniors. An inclusive playground and outdoor space."],["Practical next steps","To be part of the community with the community drives where we open the church to our neighbors"],["Practical next steps","Not sure"],["Practical next steps","I think it starts with discerning together with God (this survey is helpful for that), and once the direction feels clear, looking for the people God has prepared to help get us there."],["Practical next steps","I think initiatives need to be created to be better citizens of heaven in the place we find ourselves. The more aware you are of corruption the more equipped you are to fix it.\nCreate recycling initiatives and ethical resourcing classes (The clothes swap is a great example of this.) To better equip us of being stewards of this earth.\nBring back Be the Bridge or something like that. Let's start informing and mobilizing members to speak against unfair oppression and backwards justice systems.\nCreate spaces regularly to talk about hard things and question conventional teachings of the church."],["Practical next steps","Apologies if you already do this. Invite missionaries to speak at a conference aka missions conference. Continue to raise up leaders. Elders. Have an internship/church planting program. Continue to collaborate preaching and teaching as has been done.\nNot have a dominant teaching pastor. Continue to invest in youth for them to serve and share the gospel. Partner with other churches in Atlanta to reach Atlanta and stand unified in that pursuit."],["Practical next steps","I think more curricula like the Trellis would be helpful for our spiritual formation. I'm also interested in how the potential partnership with the church network we are currently exploring could grow the reach of Resonate.\nThe external programs we run that seem to be the most successful are the ones where we are filling a visible and urgent need, so I think a flexibility to respond to those needs when they arise and the knowledge of how to do so is often more important than trying to plan so far ahead."],["Practical next steps","Grow biblical teachings to our children and create a strong foundation as they grow and star experiencing the real world as young adult adults."],["Practical next steps","(1) Our next pastor needs to be focused on the lost in our area -- guiding our church to stretch outside of our comfort zones in who we worship with and how we worship. (2) Continue to make space for the men and women who are doing this, who have vibrant\n\nprayer lives, to be highlighted and set examples -- retreats and worship services are an important component of this! (3) Elder vote (4) Pursue and intentionally (not passively) develop a church planter. If we don't have one within the church, recruit one as an intern from outside the church"],["Practical next steps","I honestly have no idea but I happy to help those with the ideas when the time comes."],["Practical next steps","-1:1 discipleship and mentorship -Quarterly (or some other frequency) missions and prayer dinner for focusing on specific people groups and missions opportunities -Evening dinners with unhoused community -In 10 years there will be a much larger youth group and need for more youth space, programs and leaders/volunteers -‘Dinner with 7 strangers’ to bring people together who may not cross paths in the church otherwise"],["Practical next steps","Move forward with the spirit of God rather than ego-driven self-serving growth"],["Practical next steps","I think the Church at large will grow through simultaneously incorporating more of the strengths of the ancient/liturgical and new/charismatic traditions. Our culture needs more saturation with meaning and reminders and tangible symbols of God. And we also need the reminder of the power of the Holy Spirit at work in our lives: our Counselor, Healer, Ever present help in trouble, etc. Understanding the cultural roots of our faith and the traditions of worship could be an aspect of adult education or teaching from the pulpit. I think equipping the body with a deeper knowledge or experience of intercessory prayer and inner healing might also be a practical next step."],["Practical next steps","I don't have a quick answer for this question other than our kids get older and more of us have more bandwidth."],["Practical next steps","I'll have to think on this!"],["Practical next steps","I don't really know for sure, of course. I have been working on myself so much it feels weird to start suggesting what the church would do at large.\nMaybe add more explicit accountability in leadership roles. This includes volunteer roles like youth group volunteers even. Or life group coaches can undergo this extra accountability and then be trained on doing that for life group point leads\n\nWhen I say more accountability, I mean the appointed leader will meet occasionally with the leaders and ask more personal questions that prepare people's heart for real change, and then expose where true change needs to happen.\nThe idea would _not_ be to systematically make sure that all people in the church are forced to do something like this. Rather, some points of leadership are strategically chosen to undergo and lead something like this. That would have the kind of impact on overall culture that's real and long-lasting I hope."],["Practical next steps","1. Hire a full-time children's director (expensive, I know, but children seem to make up half of our church and need more investment; Noah has been a FANTASTIC investment but his focus is more on middle and high schoolers)."],["Practical next steps","Transform the outdoor parking lot to be a more inviting space for outreach events more greenery, permanent fixtures (providing some shade), a small playground. I know that Resonate tried to obtain a grant to do this a few years ago and didn't get it, but I'd be willing to help, and if you throw the idea out to the church, I bet others would help apply for grants."],["Practical next steps","Host more children events. The Christmas program every other year is nice, and the summer Bible camp is great, but more events like these would help give opportunities for the MANY kids in the church to participate in at least one or two events each year.\nThis helps create unity and opportunities for the kids to be exposed to God in new, unique ways other than on Sunday morning. I suspect that parents would be willing to bring kids to events on Saturdays. The Bible Olympics was a good idea (from the recent past), and we could do something like Bible-focused outdoor games or a talent show where we encourage them to perform in groups of 2-3 with a theme such as \"using our talents for God\" or something like that. Just throwing out unpolished ideas."],["Practical next steps","Host more co-ed social events for adults that could have a faith-driven theme or something. Maybe even tie service events to social events by combining a local service project with dinner or a Braves game afterwards. Escape rooms are also fun. Resonate has events like this during summertime about once a month, but continuing to do it monthly throughout the year would be amazing, especially if we could combine it with a Word-centered session, service project, or training. I know that's a lot of work to plan, though."],["Practical next steps","Keep doing surveys like this one and seeking opportunities for feedback from the church."],["Practical next steps","Capital campaign. Strategic partnerships. New programming to use the building more frequently."],["Practical next steps","I had wondered about partnering with a consultant or something like that about how to maximize our space for impacting our community and came across this while listening to a podcast: https://www.proximityprojectinc.com/"],["Practical next steps","Would highly recommend more volunteers greeting & getting to know new people (Brent is amazing!), plus more regular sharing of meals or food onsite. A church of this size has a wonderful opportunity to creatively connect and build community outside of life groups. Would also suggest planning more events that aren't a deep spiritual dive...so many people with church hurt need to build trust again! I would love to see more congregationally-led or planned events by volunteers. I think this is a great way to offer a range of social opportunities without staff having to lead everything.\nFinally, diversity in staffing (and elders!) doesn't just attract more diversity, it helps diverse groups know they are safe to attend AND plug-in. I would recommend continuing to make this a priority since it is especially important to the unchurched and de-churched communities."],["Practical next steps","I’m not sure. I couldn’t even answer that question for myself much less the church."],["Practical next steps","N/e/A (even my N/A's are wordy, I think 'exactly' snuck in the middle, there, all on its own)"],["Practical next steps","Whatever matters should be talked about from the stage every week. Right now that includes local community ministry and the liturgical recitation, and maybe either the two year Bible or Wednesday night classes. To be fair those recitations cover a lot of ground. I didn't recall much being said about discipleship outside of it being a goal.\nWhat are priorities for discipleship at Resonate? How are these organized, encouraged, promoted?\nHow do we invite and challenge the congregation to bless others in ways specific to them - their gifts, skills, calling, season of life - other than helping the Sunday service?\nHow do we normalize and foster life on mission and ministry without glorifying activity, setting up false idols, building a culture of guilt or obligation or hero worship? I think Resonate does fairly well at this, actually.\nTalking about these things from the stage every week in announcements, sermons, reflections, and/or worship is an important prerequisite.\n\nA church wide, all ages and stages discipleship program might be with considering. I've seen very good kids programs and youth, even collegiate ones. I've yet to see a church with any formal discipleship program for post college adults. Grace fellowship Snellville had the best youth program I've ever heard of with college students and young adults discipling high school students, high school students discipling middle school students and middle school structure discipling elementary students with adult supervision at Middle and elementary levels. I've never even had much success asking people to disciple me as an adult except when I was at Grace Midtown where discipleship was at the heart of the church.\nConsider classes/trainings around a wide variety of ways to serve across different types of gifts: evangelism, care, prayer, prophecy, mercy, teaching, leadership, vocation. One series that touches on many might be a great inspiration."],["Practical next steps","I think we are on track to do this but under a healthy leader, who is bought in, it will go much smoother."],["Practical next steps","I think the way our church has been, it only makes sense that church with grow with more members. When it grows, instead of focusing on how to maintain its numbers or to grow even more, but separate and plant a new church and keep multiply in that fashion."],["Practical next steps","Monthly or quarterly multi church \"all city\" nights of worship and prayer 😎"],["Practical next steps","Continuing outreach and growing the scope of partners that are involved in the weekly program. Instead of relying on a few, having many come to serve. I think that we need to continue our instructional programs (Bible studies) OR partner with/lead others to other solid studies in the area. The 'hows' of practical conversations with other nonbelievers in our community are ripe for teaching."],["Practical next steps","Continuing outreach hour's voluntary bible studies. We want popele joining who genuinely want to grow like Jesus"],["Practical next steps","Not sure, I Love the serve Sunday. Embrace baby shower, Sunday updates of what people are doing."],["Practical next steps","We could establish a food pantry to able to consistently provide decent and healthy food to the community. Partnering with local nonprofits to serve as a hub to deliver their services could be really helpful. Serving as a polling place could also bring people of influence in the neighborhood that otherwise we would never meet."],["Practical next steps","I’m not sure"],["Practical next steps","- More outreach days in the community.\n- Partnering with homeless shelter in dekalb and having member sign ups, etc - Continued classes and education to equip the members with how to step into the community."],["Practical next steps","Continued daily pursuit of the community directly around the church, training for the congregation on interacting with people who they may feel uncomfortable interacting with, partnerships with neighboring disability and/or homeless organizations."],["Practical next steps","Youth programming. Shorter Bible study or a re vamp of our system."],["Practical next steps","Raise specific funds to allow the outreach community to come to the men and women's retreats."],["Practical next steps","Having regular events in the courtyard with community resources"],["Practical next steps","Invest in our kids and our youth in particular with resources, relationships, and mentors Form relationships with our community Work towards planting Pursue generosity and gifts of the spirit to benefit our neighbors"],["Practical next steps","practical steps - community events for surrounding ares and their corresponding demographics.\nprograms - a community resources coordinator for poverty stricken families/individuals new initiatives - Third Space Physical Utility of site during the week, online platforms for local community storytelling, Or dinner party model for newish visitors to up the relational depth."],["Practical next steps","Meeting place for other groups like AA, ESL, food pantries, etc. during the week.\nRegular events at church. I guess the connect lunch is kind of the potluck idea - I guess I prefer it to be at church to more easily convince others and myself to stay.\nTurn the outdoor area into a playground and picnic area. Encourage people to spend time there instead of just in and out on a Sunday."],["Practical next steps","In the current climate, I think resonate can look to be more active in the community l.\nContinuing to build on what we already do, look to interact with the marginalized, the\n\noutcasts, etc. Possibly look to interact with partners or organization that can provide a greater impact on such initiatives."],["Practical next steps","unsure"],["External threats","Community reputation, given the loss of our lead pastor, is one thing that could be an external risk. I am unsure how that departure is being communicated outside of our church body."],["External threats","Funding/economic factors and the current political climate. People are already feeling financial pressure from growing inflation, and it seems like it'll get worse before it gets better. This could influence how people give financially to the church."],["External threats","Transient nature of life in Atlanta can hinder long term relationships"],["External threats","Economic instability, shifts in community needs"],["External threats","I think the biggest external risk to Resonate's future success is the same as the risk to growing Christianity in general. Right now, I am not proud to be a Christian.\nUnfortunately, I often hear stories of people using Christianity as a reason to not be accepting, open-minded, and non-judgmental. I love sharing my religious views when I am asked about it, but at this time, I always have to start with a big BUT. I am a practicing Christian, BUT I also support x, y, and z. I see detrimental effects on other people when Christianity is practiced in a narrow scope where everyone has to fit in a set mold. We are losing people from the religion, churches are shrinking, and I believe in order to grow, we need to create a safe space for all."],["External threats","None that I can think of."],["External threats","Christianity are large is at a crossroads. In the one hand, more progressive holistic and wholesome churches are thriving with more young families showing up. In the other hand, Christianity is being weaponized, leading to many losing faith in this religion.\nHow can Resonate be part of the force that helps to maintain Jesus's main teaching, without needing to force the human made elements."],["External threats","Lead pastor search - contention on process/ choosing poorly.\nThe tension between social economics in the church body as we become more diversified.\n\nElimination of non-profit tax exemptions.\nLoosing additional key leaders."],["External threats","Funds and resources. The resonate team is relatively small and they can only do so much."],["External threats","The challenges lie in Atlanta being a transient city. How to continue raising up leaders.\nPouring into college students (I.e. Emory) even if they end up moving away after graduating. There could be economic collapse. The risk of new pastor bringing in a new culture that doesn’t align with the current culture. Political difficulties too. If we increasingly grow politically suffused, the impetus on Resonate and other churches to take political stands will only increase but the risk is alienating one side while being completely complicit with one side."],["External threats","People leaving the church in the next 9 months due to decisions Resonate makes about the next lead pastor and new vision or theological stances."],["External threats","Gentrification and the needs moving further from our building."],["External threats","There could be large economic hardship due to AI. This could pose a challenge but also an opportunity to speak truth about what it means to be human. Sometimes the challenges bring about growth (see the persecuted church). When the challenges come, how can we remain hopeful and bold?"],["External threats","An increase in public apathy and skepticism of the church. I think it’s very important that church people aren’t in a church bubble, and that we don’t treat our relationships with non-Christians as merely evangelical opportunities. Most people I know who came to Christ did so because they knew a Christian who was real and loved Jesus."],["External threats","Many growing churches struggle with the pressure to keep up with larger churches, especially when people begin comparing worship styles, programs, buildings, or online presence. That comparison can slowly push a church to focus more on production quality and creating an impressive experience than on helping people grow spiritually.\nAt the same time, churches are dealing with deep divisions over politics, elections, and social issues, which can create tension within the congregation and distract from their shared mission. There is also a broader cultural skepticism toward Christianity, especially among younger generations, many of whom are attending church less often or questioning organized religion altogether."],["External threats","sorry, I don't have feedback for this"],["External threats","The church is in the middle of diverse communities. Predominantly African American community to the south, an affluent white and liberal community to the north and a diverse immigrant community to the east. It’s important we preach the gospel and are careful how we approach the political divide, accepting people’s views especially the ones we don’t agree with. I would expect the area to continue gentrify and change. The poor and marginalized will be pushed further south and further east. Decatur seems to be well off, Atlanta City is trying to keep up and Dekalb doesn’t have resources(so it seems). AI will be used to preach sermons and it will need to be carefully used as a tool. Scripture reading and prayer need to be foundational in how we share the gospel, not AI."],["External threats","I'm not sure this fits a strict definition of a threat, but finding a new leader feels like it has the potential to blow this up. Hiring someone who is a bad fit culturally or theologically, or whose preaching style isn't quite right, could send many families away.\nIt feels like a lot hinges on this decision."],["External threats","Obviously the pastor search is a big one. If that does not go well with our first pastor hire, and we need to transition to someone else/different structure, then it will be hard on the leadership to carry that forward again.\nOur nuanced position to complimentarianism may also become a hurdle to address as we continue to restructure our leadership.\nLack of engagement with older generations. We are weak in this area. We do not have older people in our leadership structure and would greatly benefit from their wisdom and experience."],["External threats","Loss in money due to congregational shifts."],["External threats","Current political climate"],["External threats","The current Christian nationalist movement comes to mind. Resonate seems to stand in contrast to much (if not most) of this mindset. Depending on how our county continues to go, resonate could face funding issues, persecution, etc from these groups. Sadly, my concern is that Resonate’s Jesus centric message may be labeled as “woke” by these groups."],["External threats","If the current neighborhood becomes unsafe for families to attend church in the current building If the staff attention is so outwardly focused to the detriment of focusing inward"],["External threats","Belvedere Plaza's decline with the empty Kroger. If the plaza is an unsafe environment it will become harder to use our building at night.\nPartners losing jobs or becoming more economically strapped that they are not able to keep giving at the level we have had.\nBlack neighbors not trusting the predominantly white leadership of Resonate and choosing not to be a part."],["External threats","Power (as spiritual force arrayed against us).\nMammon.\nOthering.\nPossibly American politics, which is not a statement about Resonate people (I know nothing), just a statement about the ripping/tearing I've witnessed in recent years.\nIn my personal opinion based on non-Resonate observation, the ways that Theological Certainty is handled will affect, not IF people come/stay/go but WHO comes/stays/goes. And while that might VERY WELL affect our level of 'success' in God's eyes, WE 1) aren't going to actually be able to confirm that this side of the new earth, and 2) will probably mostly continue to judge 'success' in terms of attendance, paying all the bills, ongoing programming, and other business-world measurables. Not all bad. But care must be taken to never conflate the second with the first.\n(and this wasn't an attempt to make an exhaustive list)"],["External threats","I wonder if external factors revolving around how much our partners' family lives, sports, activities, work, and school obligations affect commitment to church outreaches, participation in life groups, etc."],["External threats","I’m truthfully not sure"],["External threats","Not sure."],["External threats","Emptiness of Belvedere Plaza? Outgrowing space or making sure that we make the best use of the space."],["External threats","I worry about how the church will respond to new leadership coming in. It can be a very polarizing, especially dependent upon posture. Economic and cultural threats continue to surround us and I don't see that going away."],["External threats","Maybe the direction of a partner org"],["External threats","Not sure"],["External threats","This is just a technicality, but the safety of the building in this people because of the surroundings. However, that is what’s beautiful about resident that it can impact the surrounding communities in spite of this."],["External threats","Economics--more folks being out of jobs."],["External threats","A general culture shift as the church goes through different members."],["External threats","I am not sure…maybe law-making? I’m not sure. Hard for me to answer, especially being outside the US. I know the economy is not great right now, but I also think Resonate members will continue to be generous where they can be."],["External threats","The aggresive gentrification efforts going on in the BP area. Also the on-going tension between the different racial and socioeconomic groups. Subtle aversion towards multiculturalism and etc."],["External threats","- Funding/financing - Becoming too big in terms of membership - Having too much of a heavily progressive and liberal mindset (that it deters others from wanting to come to the church)"],["External threats","Whatever new anchor business that may come into Belvedere Plaza - that would just introduce new dynamics and challenges. There will be an eventual change in presidential administration, and that would trickle down policy changes, particularly with refugee resettlement."],["External threats","I'm not sure."],["External threats","I think our generosity is outstanding, but the real truth is we need more. I think this will come with time as our community gets older. I think that development is a real concern if Belevadear Park becomes more gentrified. What happens when the shopping center is bought out or redeveloped? I also think that with new vision, there will be new growth. I am not sure how much our building can hold without adding? I don't know what we can add either?"],["External threats","Funding & policies"],["External threats","Currently, Resonate has a need for a lead elder/pastor with the time and insight (sensitivity) to direct the church to achieve 10-year goals. Policy changes may have to be considered… with much prayer… however, I personally can not be too specific at this time."],["External threats","I am not sure about this."],["External threats","Shift towards more liturgical/sacremental seekers, will we stay relevant with a more nondenominational/evangelical leaning?"],["External threats","Since we are placed in a unique area, I think often the needs of the community could cause people to be hesitant to step into that aspect of our vision and heart. If people aren't willing to step into \"messy ministry\" to love and serve our direct neighborhood and to invite others into their lives then it could be hard for them to consider Resonate home."],["External threats","Gentrification could fundamentally change both the needs of the community and who we see on the day-to-day basis. Also, now that Kroger is closed, whatever replaces it could potentially affect how the community operates."],["External threats","Not finding a pastor or finding a pastor that is so different that lots of people don't align and look for another church to worship with. Decreased financial giving with economic changes. The building being in one location but members moving further away due to economic/housing factors. inability to get people well connected in lifegroups."],["External threats","Historically, I feel like we had an exodus of people after every big initiative, like moving spaces, buying a building, etc. I'm fairly certain part of that was expecting too much, or not having organization structured to put boundaries on how much people put in.\nI'm not saying that all needs to be professionalized, but certainly more thought and care and appreciation for the work of the people in the church."],["External threats","Hidden sin Gossip Identity in world/politics instead of body of Christ"],["External threats","Belvedere Plaza is constantly changing and shifting as is our congregation, which can be very transient. Our local demographic may change over the next 5-10 years, as may our congregants as they move."],["External threats","Just how hard it is for life groups to meet up and form deeper relationships. So, in all seriousness, Atlanta traffic and sprawl distance from each other.\nPolitics has become very polarizing. I think that's a real demonic stronghold wherein the only way many of us know to navigate this is pure conflict avoidance. While better than attacking and hating each other, it breeds a type of chilling effect."],["External threats","No threats come to mind. I believe that the ones you reference in the question would all be provided for by God as we continue to faithfully serve and obey. I really only see any potential for failure to be internal collapse which would only happen through disobedience and moving away from a Christ and scripture focused mission (which I don't see happening!)"],["External threats","Underdevelopment of the surrounding community."],["External threats","Continued political strife and cultural stress driving highly anxious people, especially the next generation"],["External threats","Maybe who we bring in as lead pastor"],["External threats","The church is very small so I imagine not having enough partners and people tithing would cause issues."],["External threats","Obviously if our lovely economy goes more sour in the coming years I can see finances straining. I can also see the tempestuous political landscape potentially dividing us and/or painting a target on Resonate as a whole."],["External threats","Transience in people present is a threat to long term connections"],["External threats","The wall behind the church that’s failed in several places. That’s a REAL ISSUE and external threat that could crash into the building causing financial and location problems and damages."],["External threats","Funding, lack of member involvement, the need is greater than what the church can supply."],["External threats","thought about it for a while and could not think of anything"],["External threats","I think the church has a responsibility now more than ever to support marginalized and disproportionately affected community members and partners. The threat of losing these members is real, and would be a great detriment to the church as a diversity of backgrounds and voices is a disservice."],["External threats","Spiritual warfare."],["External threats","1. Reaching people in our church neighborhood who have transportation issues unable to come to church and/or outreach events."],["External threats","Economic and political changes that impact the most vulnerable people - rising gas prices, immigration visa bans, etc."],["External threats","Obtaining funding from grants and contributions from partners."],["External threats","The transient nature of Atlanta always feels like a threat to the relationships that I've formed at Resonate, key people leaving for one reason or another. Also, a new pastor that doesn't fit seems like the largest looming threat to me."],["External threats","Socioeconomic gap between the church members and neighbors of our physical location. The trap of \"elitism\""],["External threats","Unsafe incidents that cause people to leave"],["External threats","Not certain I can think of them."],["External threats","Affordability - housing, inflation, gas, food."],["External threats","Political policies causing economic crisis resulting in salary decreases, lost jobs, relocation of residence, and unmet community needs."],["External threats","The location of the building (will there be future construction at belvedere plaza? safety at night)"],["External threats","An overall threat is Christian Nationalism and a view that American Conservatism is somehow a one to one with the gospe, which at this moment, I detect no sense of at the church, but I do think we have to weary of that ideology. On the flip side, I think we should also be mindful of bowing to the whims of the world that at times can be pushed in more liberal ideology. It's a tough line to walk, but I do think the church needs to everything it can to identify itself by scripture and not man made ideologies about said scripture."],["External threats","Being primarily white in a more black neighborhood. (Becoming to inwardly focused.\nNot finding balance between discipleship and growing as a body and expanding and out reach and pouring out could create a challenge.) Dream: outreach so active that we buy another building in the strip to minister to the community."],["External threats","The threat of additional political strain in the world and allowing that to become either too present, or fearing it such that folks of varied political opinions don’t feel welcome"],["External threats","Probably money- just the number of people who can give financially might be a challenge"],["External threats","Churches tend to grow to the capacity of their building space. Also, how might changes in commerce and nearby development affect the population of Beldevere and who is our neighbor?"],["External threats","As the world becomes less religious and more polarized will we be willing to ask hard questions, wrestle with what everyone is wrestling with and be in tuned with the outside world? Ex: there are serious shifts in the world and we just blindly keep following our x, y, z sermon series? Or will we engage with what’s going on in the world around us and wrestle together with what we believe is the most god honoring path."],["External threats","Still somewhat on the outside, I sense there may be some values misalignment of the staff and/or congregation re: the role of women in the church now and going forward.\nThat may be something to be mindful of or even reevaluate.\nMorale-wise, the trauma related to Pastor Case is significant and the church needs a truly healthy leader at the helm during this sensitive period of recovery. Sarah is doing a wonderful job in this challenging moment."],["External threats","I’m not sure about this one. Resonate has such a unique and amazing culture. A potential threat might be a major shift in DNA and culture in a negative way if someone not like minded steps into leadership."],["External threats","The biggest threats that occurs to me are still internal but not necessarily weaknesses.\nOvercorrecting in response to the situation with Chris and overemphasizing in particular sin or sin pattern.\nFocusing on growing attendance and/or physical expansion rather than kingdom impact and lives changed.\nNeither of these seem to be prominent at this time but they are risks."],["External threats","I worry about our concern on matters like \"should women teach\". Really just boxes and rules that have been created around religion that distract from the point which is studying the Word, following Jesus, and loving each other and our community."],["External threats","-Though I don't have specifics in mind, economic factors and local development could have adverse effects on Resonate's ability to thrive where we are planted and to reach the surrounding community. E.g., the Kroger closed. What if the rest of the stores in Belvedere Plaza eventually closed too and it ended up like some of the abandoned shopping centers we see all around Dekalb county? How would that change things for the surrounding community, and for us as a church?\n-Continued political/economic factors could well affect giving down the road. Even increased costs of transportation due to fuel prices could affect how far people are willing to travel to be at Resonate."],["External threats","- Smaller church - Mainly one generation (not many older members) - Do others prioritize these things? Busy-ness is such a problem in our American culture at large and we are at risk of falling into lack of volunteer opportunities due to people having too much on their plate.\n- high emphasis on families with children (national church wide problem, but we are not immune to this idolization at resonate either)."],["External threats","Swaying to every political whim.\nBecoming self centered- not having eyes open to what is going on in community around us and addressing it."],["External threats","Chris’ failure as a leader is definitely a threat. I think the church is in danger of not healing-just trying to move on from what happened. And I think that is going to come out sideways if we aren’t careful during the pastor search.\nI’m also worried that we will slide into having a woman as an actual pastor. I’m not fully convinced on the position about women preaching (still to this day) and it makes me wonder how much of that shift originally came because of Chris. I wonder if the elders were making the decision now, if it would be any different without Chris’s input and thoughts on scripture. I don’t trust his preaching from the past year and it feels like he was starting to deconstruct his faith while he was preaching. Or maybe trying to make allowances in his mind for the sin he was entangled in already."],["External threats","economic challenges, health care/funding challenges, access to care; lack of awareness of impact of politics/policies"],["External threats","Apathy toward the local church due to lack of Sr. Pastor. What is happening around is is only noise. True Believers will be strong under the care of a local shepherd rightly dividing the Word."],["External threats","Repeating what just happened with Chris. Such a need for transparency and vulnerable humility from the “top” down throughout our body."],["External threats","I can see the demographics of the Belvedere Park area changing over the next several years, which could alter the kind of church we are trying to be. In 10 years will we be a wealthy church in a predominantly white area?"],["External threats","A probable increase in local poverty in the next 3 - 5 years."],["External threats","Definitely economic factors, especially now with helping programs /funding slashed"],["External threats","I can see the size of the building impacting growth down the road."],["External threats","I cannot think of anything specifically at this time."],["For the pastor search","I would love assurance that the pastor search committee is only looking for a male pastor."],["For the pastor search","We are an eclectic bunch from many different denominational backgrounds grounds.\nFinding someone comfortable with grey areas, but confidently holding essentials. That can ask good questions and listen to different opinions (with gentleness and humble patience) will be important."],["For the pastor search","I trust the leadership team to find good connections and do their best to vet a new leader"],["For the pastor search","One thing that bothered me with the last lead pastor is he would frequently say, “some people believe I don’t have a high view of Scripture.” I think it’s a red flag if the lead pastor has to say that from the pulpit. It should be unquestionable based on the pastor’s words and deeds that he views scripture highly."],["For the pastor search","I think it's very important that our next hire is a good fit relationally and culturally. We need to not hire a Chris 2.0, but also not swing the pendulum to Chris' opposite. We should specifically consider how he will relate to the Belvedere Park Community and his theological convictions on gender, as these are both important conversations at Resonate right now."],["For the pastor search","I would encourage them to think outside the box. If a traditional head pastor role won't work well in our community, don't be afraid to propose a job description that works well for Resonate, even if it's unconventional"],["For the pastor search","My personal hope is for a seasoned (though not of retirement age!) pastor who understands current cultural issues from a biblical perspective, motivated by compassion and commitment to see people mature in Christ, no matter where they are in their journey. I hope for a person who loves Jesus, believes that the spiritual gifts are for today, for the building up of the body to effectively proclaim the gospel and show others the rule and reign of Jesus as one who can free us to love others fully."],["For the pastor search","-I hope they will have a pastoral heart where they actually talk to church members and attendees (not just a select few) and want to see, know and care for the congregation, not just intellectually share knowledge from the stage.\n-I hope they want to actively train others in critically reading and teaching scripture.\n-I hope they see beauty and worth in diversity including life stages, cultures, marriage status, etc.\n-I hope they receive and desire accountability and operate in spirit filled humility.\n-I hope they are not caught up in the politics that many churches are currently caught up in."],["For the pastor search","I want us to be known as the church that welcomes everyone. That strives for loving, teaching, and growing together. For not judging, as we are not God."],["For the pastor search","Many times when I have faced difficult choices between good options, God has made it clear that he has blessed me with good choices and that he will be with me either way.\nMay God do so for you in this search as well. Blessing in the search."],["For the pastor search","Find someone who is collaborative and will work well with others who are different from them. Someone who is a visionary leader and who is gifted in teaching/preaching/discipling."],["For the pastor search","I think our pastor should be male. I know others may rankle at this but that is what I believe is supported by scripture."],["For the pastor search","See my application for said committee"],["For the pastor search","I’m not sure why we are going outside of our current church members. We’ve always had a long and detailed process for our Elder team. Why is this any different?"],["For the pastor search","I think there are some people who are going to think that Sarah should be the next Lead Pastor. I think that there is another group of people that may not 100% agree with a woman as a lead pastor, but would prefer Sarah over an unknown outsider. I hope that Resonate would only hire Sarah if they felt true conviction that this is a a. Biblically sound b. holy spirit led - choice. Please don't hire Sarah just because she is better than an unknown.\nAlso, Sarah is GREAT in the role that she is in. We NEED her in a prominent leadership role. And I believe that there is a way to design an org chart and roles and\n\nresponsibilities where Sarah can thrive as we develop and build more robust bible study, life group, and discipleship programming - ALONGSIDE a new lead pastor."],["For the pastor search","We need someone who is extremely humble but who will have a strong vision for the church, grounded in a genuine love for the body and openness to the Spirit."],["For the pastor search","See other notes"],["For the pastor search","My hope for the search would be that the committee puts a significant emphasis on talking with candidates that care deeply about nurturing community trust and working in a diverse environment. I think for our church right now we need trust, transparency, and the ability to adapt to a diverse and dynamic community."],["For the pastor search","I would like to find a candidate with the ability to follow through, and articulate what they believe follow through looks like, and what the outcome should be after the work.\nI'd like a candidate that focuses less on position papers for the eldership and more on basic structural truths for people in congregation.\nI would like a candidate who wants to be known, and actively engages in doing so. I would like a candidate who is not withdrawn from bringing their struggles to account.\nI would like a candidate who is fine with confronting, and enters into it with grace.\nI would like a candidate who is open to counseling, and has a history of engaging in it.\nI would like a candidate who can read the room and meet the people in it.\nI would like a candidate that acknowledges that the Bible is ambiguous at best on the idea of a head pastor, and doesn't see the role as the defining one of the church.\nI would like a candidate who would be fine not being an administrator, or at least acknowledges their weaknesses in administration. Teachers and administrators are rarely contained in the same body, and there's a reason that executive and teaching roles, or two founders in a startups, are common patterns."],["For the pastor search","I hope our next hire is forward-thinking, open-minded, able to hear concerns from both sides of the table, help with compromise, but also have a firm footing in their beliefs and plan for the future. I hope we can continue to dive into the scripture and learn from what is written, but also view that through a modern lens. We have learned a lot and developed as humans over the last 1500 years, let's apply that to scripture. Finding someone who lives in or moves to the community would be amazing. I hope to continue attending Resonate for years to come. Finding a pastor who will lead the congregation for the long-term would be ideal."],["For the pastor search","While I'm not completely opposed to having a woman pastor, I do feel like it would be a bit awkward and rub many in the congregation in the wrong way."],["For the pastor search","You cannot \"see\" the heart of a man. And many are swayed by how the world measures. Only God, through prayer and fasting can reveal to you the Man of God He has for us."],["For the pastor search","Personally, I’d love to see a pastor who teaches well, sure, but is truly a shepherd. And, a person of integrity. Someone who won’t come in and try to change a lot, but see the good God is doing in Resonate and be excited to join it."],["For the pastor search","See previous responses"],["For the pastor search","I hope the next pastor has deep understanding of multicultural and international discipleship."],["For the pastor search","Take the time to find the right fit who will lead our church where we want to go. A new pastor will shift the culture--I'd like to see us shift to be more missional in reaching the lost, forming disciples and leaders, and planting new groups and churches."],["For the pastor search","That they can teach in a way that connects with both the academic and the average layperson.\nObvious heart for people and easier to interact with and will answer messages within reasonable time.\nOpen about needs and seeks help if there is a need."],["For the pastor search","Someone who wants to commit for the long haul."],["For the pastor search","I have more misgivings than high hopes, to be honest. But that has NOTHING to do with y'all (I don't even know who you are). I support you and wish you the very best in the process.\nTo me, Resonate was a church I could feel a measure of 'at home' at even as I began to find less and less of a home in my inherited religious tradition. I want to be a good Body member. I want to support my leadership. But (and I feel like this is an obvious point, not a high-discernment point) the next person brought in could be a person who wants to take Resonate in directions that don't feel like home anymore. I am in flux with many things these days--\"how have we come to center 'sermon' so much?\" \"do we DO Sr. pastor right? or even close to right?\" and just many things.\n\nBut I know other people are in other places. And there are tons of things that I am flat out wrong about. I choose unity, first. Let the learning stay fun. And I want to always be able to learn from those who think differently than me.\nI am committed to being a body member and fellowshipper. But I know that bringing in one guy with a vision and handing him power over others… affects dynamics explicitly, and I am a little leery, that's all. But you all do what you have been entrusted with, and God be with you!"],["For the pastor search","I hope they have a gift is engaging the congregation in real conversation and a way to delight people in the story of God revealing scripture to be interesting and rich."],["For the pastor search","I believe Sarah should be the lead pastor"],["For the pastor search","-Let's focus not on replacing what we lost but on finding a leader to lead the way to where we want to go.\n-Let's find someone who is an effective, humble, and relational leader of people— someone who can guide and equip the staff in fulfilling their roles.\n-Don't rush it!"],["For the pastor search","We hope for a lead pastor that is a great teacher, grounded in the word of God, skilled at pastoral care, motivated to speak the truth in love, and uncomplicated by politics."],["For the pastor search","Be weary of smooth talkers, those who seem to \"know\" everything, \"seen\" it before, etc. I think that our church is unfortunately ripe for a bad actor to show up and attempt to take advantage. I think the next pastor will need to be sweating with humility, and I tend to think the next pastor should be a younger Gen X or older Milennial due to our demographics at Resonate"],["For the pastor search","I would by lying if I said this didn't worry me. I loved Chris' teachings. His analytical brain complimented the church's spiritual side so well. Truthfully, I would love someone who teaches like Chris. Who loves questioning everything and making Christianity \"weird again\". Who knows the context and reasoning behind scripture. Who teaches verse by verse. Who cares deeply for the church and for it's members. Chris, didn't do that. He was always an arm's length away. I pray that we find someone who teaches and gets his hands dirty. I hope you all know that I am praying for this committee and that the spirit moves in the process of this hire!"],["For the pastor search","Good luck"],["For the pastor search","Someone with a long track record of proven character."],["For the pastor search","My hopes for our next lead pastor is for the individual to have a deep relationship with Jesus, a deep understanding of the word, and a heart of teaching. One thing that I admired was how the word was taught at resonate and I valued it."],["For the pastor search","Nothing comes to mind."],["For the pastor search","Hoping for a strong pastoral leader, emphasis on shepherding and compassion, relatable"],["For the pastor search","I trust the Lord will provide the individual we need for this next season and the future of the church. Thinking of bringing someone completely new from the outside is hard for me to wrap my head around but I do believe Jesus is head of our church and can provide that person to step into our community. I would encourage the committee to be open to setting a new job description for this lead pastor role and to be patient to see how the job description comes together. Ask themselves, \"What do we really need this person to do?\" I don't think it needs to be exactly the same role Chris was fulfilling/not fulfilling."],["For the pastor search","How the individual interacts with the team and works alongside the vision already established"],["For the pastor search","We trust you."],["For the pastor search","Nothing specific… other than an individual who has a strong and growing faith, as well as, an individual gifted with leadership qualities."],["For the pastor search","We would be devastated if someone overtly politically conservative was hired.\nSomebody who didn’t care about people with disabilities, refugees, and women."],["For the pastor search","I feel we should hire within. I feel Noah Noami would be an excellent lead pastor.\nPlease consider this as a serious recommendation."],["For the pastor search","Some ideal characteristics of a lead pastor might include a spiritual father figure and apostolic gifting that equips and releases the body to live out its God-given potential."],["For the pastor search","Organizational leadership is as important as teaching, maybe more"],["For the pastor search","I'm hoping he is a big believer in Holy Spirit as our helper. A charismatic would be wonderful but not so much so that we aren't still staying in the Word. Ideally he'd strike a balance across these things."],["For the pastor search","I would love for our lead pastor to be able to communicate/teach clearly, work effectively with all staff, be humble and willing to receive feedback/accountability, and have a heart for our local communities and the many unique things that so many of our\n\npartners care deeply about (fostering, refugee population in Clarkston, social justice issues/speaking out against racism, disability, etc). I also would love for the lead pastor to be pastoral/caring/shepherding - not just an expository teacher of Scripture."],["For the pastor search","Nothing specifically! Praying that God brings someone that will continue to lead our church well and aligns with our core and secondary beliefs and values."],["For the pastor search","Hoping for a vineyard-like balance in word and action/prayer"],["For the pastor search","Thank you for including the congregation in the search process. I'm hopeful that the next hire will be someone who embodies Resonate's academic, context-driven style of teaching from the Bible and someone who has extensive experience preaching and leading a church."],["For the pastor search","I think it is very important that the new pastor comes in alongside our current leadership and does not come with a new agenda or vision for Resonate. We have a vision already.\nWe need someone to join us in what we are already doing. I think it is crucial that the new lead pastor can and will work with Sarah as a co-laborer and not a superior."],["For the pastor search","We have the utmost confidence in our leadership and are praying for the team.\nI am hopeful we can find someone who preaches gospel centered, working through line by line in scripture. But who is also gifted in the area of teaching to pull out the exeJesus emphasis, and balancing emotional/spiritual teaching with didactic/academic."],["For the pastor search","True change takes wisdom. I guess I hope the next lead pastor is not so focused on instituting whatever he wants to change that he misses the people in the process."],["For the pastor search","Would love an individual who wants to commit to the neighborhood and build disciples"],["For the pastor search","We are praying for you!!"],["For the pastor search","Dispute all that happened, Chris was a phenomenal teacher and truly gifted by the spirit to lead our church. His commitment to tackle the hard/controversial issues and deep study into the original and historical context of scripture had a profound impact on my personal relationship with biblical study and how I view the gospel. I fear loosing this in the next lead pastor. I pray that God would guide the team to prioritize finding a like minded individual who will seek to challenge and strengthen the church, much like Chris had."],["For the pastor search","One thing that I appreciated about Chris was the curiosity and humility in his approach to studying and teaching the Word. I don't want a pastor who thinks he knows everything about Scripture and is inflexible when it comes to interpretation. I am\n\nconcerned about the arrogance that comes with that and have experienced it myself in churches I've previously attended."],["For the pastor search","I think that many folks at Resonate are hopeful that Sarah will lead. Though the elders shared their stance when we left Acts 29 and allowed women to teach, we think it’s important to address this again. Are the elders open to change? I feel strongly that we need someone who is already deeply invested in our church body and who understands our strengths and weaknesses. I am worried about Sarah’s role being diminished depending on who is hired. She is the most steady, wise, and humble leader I have ever had in a church (and I’ve spent my entire life in church). I have learned more from her teaching (classes, curriculum, preaching) than anyone else. Please ensure that her work will be amplified and not tamped down."],["For the pastor search","A strong lead pastor will be a Mentor and Builder. Inclusive of a devoted private prayer life, humility, servant leadership, and a compassionate heart for people."],["For the pastor search","1) What will be the safety net for Pastor accountability in view of recent history?\n2) Hoping the Elders & Committee acknowledge the faithfulness, spiritual example and congregational support of current Staff so that they also have a significant role in the search process."],["For the pastor search","I would like for the new pastor/ ( + if married spouse)to be more involved with the congregation and less hands off. It felt like Chris and Leah were not as involved with church family as they could have been. It would be nice to see the next pastor/ pastor wife to be more engaged in general. ( with of course a healthy work/life expectation, I understand they have lives outside of the church.)"],["For the pastor search","One thing I have thought of is that we have someone who comes in with an attitude of \"I know how it all is and I am right and no one should disagree,\" that would really change our culture.\nI hope they are someone who walks in love for people like Jesus did."],["For the pastor search","praying for you all! thanks for leading faithfully"],["For the pastor search","He needs to be known, held accountable, and invested relationally with the staff and leadership"],["For the pastor search","Not at this time."],["For the pastor search","Not at this time"],["For the pastor search","I am praying for y’all in this journey. I’m praying for peace, discernment, faith, hope, joy, and unity in this search."],["For the pastor search","Get plenty of partnership input on how to present candidates with an accurate picture of who resonate is"],["For the pastor search","Whoever we get, let’s make sure they aren’t open to regular check-ins and particularly that they are willing to grow close as friends with our body and be vulnerable early"],["For the pastor search","Not sure, but I have been enjoying hearing the voices of multiple different people preaching as we have been doing recently, and would enjoy seeing that continue on a semi-regular basis even if/when a new lead Pastor is hired."],["For the pastor search","The new lead pastor will be my family's first lead pastor since we joined Resonate.\nPraying for God's intervention on every step of the way!"],["For the pastor search","Thank you for your hard work. I pray for us to find a lead pastor with compassion for the marginalized, broad and concrete vision for a developing a multicultural church context, heart and passion for planting new churches in our region rather than focusing on church growth."],["For the pastor search","Consider whether we need a lead pastor or a different position hire"],["For the pastor search","I would love women to be considered for the role"],["For the pastor search","I hope we interview people of all backgrounds to find someone who understands our body and community well."],["For the pastor search","We are praying for you! Excited to see how the Father leads!!"],["For the pastor search","I would love to see our lead pastor be someone who has evidently been transformed by the gospel. Someone who has a deep and close relationship with Jesus. Someone who is bold to preach the truth, even when it's hard."],["For the pastor search","I’m hesitant to install someone who hasn’t been part of the church for a while. There is a chance it could be a seamless and wonderful transition, but it also has the potential to feel clunky and awkward with an outsider coming in and “taking over”. What if we don’t like this person? Can we take it back?\nAt the same time, hiring from within seems tricky because the obvious choice would be Sarah Pascual. Apart from being unsure how I feel about being part of an “overtly liberal” church with a woman lead pastor, I think it’s worth thinking through what we\n\nwould miss out by making Sarah lead pastor. She is amazing and does so much good work in her current role. Would she have the capacity to continue to do what she is doing? Would we be worse off by losing all that she currently does and giving her new responsibilities? Would it be more limiting than expanding of her gifts? I don’t know, but it’s worth thinking through."],["For the pastor search","It may be an unlikely person. Like David who was the youngest, but the most qualified because God knew his heart. No concerns just a thought that came to mind when praying."],["For the pastor search","Thank you for your effort."],["For the pastor search","I’m sure it would be helpful to many at resonate to explain what accountability looks like for this new lead pastor. I’m hopeful for someone who deeply knows the word and can teach well - not just stir emotions. We seem to be a church that’s hungry to learn."],["For the pastor search","I love and trust you all and am praying for you as you take this on."],["For the pastor search","Want them to be focused on the Word and how to grow the church in our local area.\nWe need someone who is able to help us grow in our understanding of how scripture applies to our changing lives regarding sexuality, AI, sharing the gospel, etc."],["For the pastor search","I think a posture of humility and collaboration will be vital. We have a strong elder board, staff, and leadership team. Someone with a strong frontier mindset might not be a good fit. I think it will be important that it is someone who loves and wants to grow what has already been planted in Resonate Church."],["For the pastor search","Just this prayer over them: Eph. 1 - 17 \"that the God of our Lord Jesus Christ, the Father of glory, may give you the Spirit of wisdom and of revelation in the knowledge of him, 18 having the eyes of your hearts enlightened, that you may know what is the hope to which he has called you\". I am praying for you brothers and sisters!"],["For the pastor search","Have reasonable expectations for what the lead pastor alone should do. It could be a good time to reevaluate the roles and responsibilities of the full leadership team."],["For the pastor search","Lets go!!!!!!!!!!!"],["For the pastor search","I’m hopeful that we can hire a man of color or a man from a different cultural/racial background and lean into his gifts and vision for the church. It feels like putting the cart before the horse to set the vision before we have our next pastor."],["For the pastor search","I am optimistic about how our leadership team is going about the lead pastor search. I would encourage some sort of guidance outside of the search team - someone(s) that can help mentor and encourage those who are leading the charge."],["For the pastor search","I'm desiring someone mature in faith who knows God's Word, is able to shepherd, and whose life gives evidence to the Holy Spirit at work in their lives."],["Heart of who we are","People/ families who want to follow Jesus with love and dedication"],["Heart of who we are","Welcoming climate"],["Heart of who we are","Christ centered teaching and worship"],["Heart of who we are","For me it means that the truths of the Word resonate deeply in the church and its partners and overflows into how we move around the world and interact with others"],["Heart of who we are","This survey is too long. I feel like I have already answered this question."],["Heart of who we are","All I can think is our mission statement: \"Resonate Church exists to equip disciplemaking disciples for the mission of God.\""],["Heart of who we are","Imperfect people who love and welcome other imperfect people."],["Heart of who we are","Resonate is a bible believing and preaching church."],["Heart of who we are","We are a place where everyone is welcome to come as they are and to pursue wholeness in Christ together."],["Heart of who we are","Community is more important than any individual"],["Heart of who we are","A bonded community of Christ followers living out his mission and calling for our lives on a daily basis"],["Heart of who we are","The people! Resonate has the best people"],["Heart of who we are","I think community and love."],["Heart of who we are","We are a community of believers and followers of Jesus - unified in His heart for others and love of all God's image-bearing neighbors."],["Heart of who we are","disciple making disciples equipped by His Word and empowered by His Spirit to freely share our gifts with our church family and proclaim the gospel in word and deed in all we do"],["Heart of who we are","Sincere faith in normal living community"],["Heart of who we are","My first experience of Resonate still represents Resonate. It was during the sermon series years ago from Chris where he talked about more controversial questions around faith. It included things like evolution and gender identity and other stuff.\n\nThe fact that he was so relatable and disarming and deescalating was really warming.\nWhile at the same time, there was a sermon where he made clear Jesus is the only way.\nThere's still conviction on what really matters.\nAlso, I think our ties with Hope Heals really impacts the church"],["Heart of who we are","We take accountability for our sins and wholeheartedly ask God and ask brothers and sisters for forgiveness."],["Heart of who we are","Community and caring for each other. We do a great of showing up for one another in real way...I sincerely believe that where we stand out."],["Heart of who we are","Resonate appears to be a place of belonging without grumbling or dissonance, hopefully driven by Christ-like thinking and action in all things."],["Heart of who we are","We're imperfect but we care to improve."],["Heart of who we are","We love and welcome others well and make space for them to feel seen and valued.\nWe are present with others and do our best to not get caught up in a show or performance as a church. We make it hard for people to slip through the cracks. We love Jesus and want others to see and experience the reality of who He is."],["Heart of who we are","We are community church that truly loves God and wants to know him better."],["Heart of who we are","FAMILIES! We are a church of families, who all want to see their kids walk with Jesus all the days. Strong families = strong church."],["Heart of who we are","Community based church making an impact at home and globally."],["Heart of who we are","Resonate is about people. We love people big, and we do it well. Resonate wants to see all people grow to their fullest potential in Christ."],["Heart of who we are","Relational disciple-making"],["Heart of who we are","The gospel above all else. To reflect Jesus beyond our church building walls."],["Heart of who we are","A welcoming community that loves Jesus."],["Heart of who we are","BRENT FREAKIN DAVIDSON lol Humility and Care are the heart at Resonate. It creates joy :) Thank you all for what you do :)"],["Heart of who we are","The community and how people genuinely do life together."],["Heart of who we are","E pluribus unum"],["Heart of who we are","I'm out of juice for this survey. Love you all."],["Heart of who we are","Resonate is a unique place where we learn and grow in this messy world and we learn how to go out and spread the love of the Lord in all we do. It’s a ripple effect and Resonate starts the ripple."],["Heart of who we are","We foster and care for everyone we interact with, loving them (to the best of our ability) like Jesus has taught us."],["Heart of who we are","a community of believers that love Jesus and seek to know him more and be more like him through the power of sanctification."],["Heart of who we are","We are a people who love serving God and serving each other + our community."],["Heart of who we are","We are people who care for and about each other."],["Heart of who we are","People who come as they are and are pointed to Jesus over and over. We pray more will come and grow in their knowledge of Him to the praise of His glory in Decatur and beyond."],["Heart of who we are","We love people well with God’s love and make each person feel seen and needed and appreciated."],["Heart of who we are","A church that deeply cares for its community and he looks too deep in its relationship with the community and one another."],["Heart of who we are","Strong Community"],["Heart of who we are","Relationships. I could tell on the first day that this was a strong community that offers many outlets for building relationships and supporting each other. I grew up in a larger church that felt much less connected than Resonate. I appreciate the warmth I feel every time I attend."],["Heart of who we are","Resonate has always been a Scripture loving, community focused, politically neutral church. Everyone is welcome and seen. You know when you walk through the doors you’re going to get Truth."],["Heart of who we are","Transparency, humility, intentionality, and genuine hearts."],["Heart of who we are","Simplicity. Learn and follow the ways of Jesus."],["Heart of who we are","The community. The love, vulnerability, and kindness shown throughout the body of this church. A community that loves unconditionally, a glimpse of heaven."],["Heart of who we are","Scripture centered, relationship/community focused, equipping, balanced, and welcoming."],["Heart of who we are","Real, genuine relationships to be deeply formed by Jesus\n\nLove for the gospel and seeing it lived out in our city Intellectual and activist, thinking deeply and serving together Distributed, flat leadership"],["Heart of who we are","we are loving."],["Heart of who we are","People love Jesus and want to follow Him."],["Heart of who we are","Community - which is why I’ve stayed for nearly a decade. We go deep with others in this hard walk of life and don’t try to skirt around the challenges. Foster care, adoption, mental health, homelessness, kids with differing abilities - it’s so much more authentic than any other church I’ve experienced."],["Heart of who we are","It’s very community oriented"],["Heart of who we are","Willingness to be Christ centered, and Christ like. Empathetic and inclusive."],["Heart of who we are","A people committed to practicing the way of Jesus through loving God and loving our neighbor. We are resilient, trusting, and deeply committed to our faith and God in a world that is shifting and changing all around us."],["Heart of who we are","Community--our people."],["Heart of who we are","We are a genuine Christian church, made up of genuine believers, who seriously wish to follow and love Jesus - taking seriously the command to reach our world, etc."],["Heart of who we are","A real church who cares deeply about people, people to know Jesus and be changed by His grace. Love His word. Love His mission. Not a surface level.an abiding church.\nAs said on Sunday. Christ has died and Christ is risen and Christ will come again."],["Heart of who we are","Community. Building community with others in faith, and using our faith to love others well."],["Heart of who we are","I think it's the community and how there's a spirit of everyone contributing and caring for one another. The people are filled with great ideas and great care for one another.\nIt's not a passive community, but an active one. I think that's really special."],["Heart of who we are","Resonate TRULY cares about each person-- partner or otherwise. I feel seen and loved."],["Heart of who we are","This season has shown the heart of the church to be in people's commitment to the body, and their local relationships within the body, even when leadership is ambiguous, flawed, or absent.\nAnd, I think behind the scenes, the rest of the staff must be working hard to support that. So diligent staff, too."],["Heart of who we are","Strong Theology, All are welcome!"],["Heart of who we are","It's people. Resonate is full of God-seeking individuals who live out their faith on a daily basis and are exemplary role models for others."],["Heart of who we are","Community - life on life ministry and discipleship, where the lines between sacred and secular are blurred in every area of life."],["Heart of who we are","I believe that what makes Resonate is the group of people who strive to live like Christ in Atlanta with their whole being. Our church sees others as true image bearers, with a desire to spur one another one. That is who we are."],["Heart of who we are","Christ-followers who are passionately identified by, and pursuing Jesus faithfully."],["Heart of who we are","Modeling how to be a loving congregation."],["Heart of who we are","Resonate is a body of real, grounded people who love Jesus and love each other.\nNothing is a show- people genuinely care for one another."],["Heart of who we are","A desire to know God more deeply and join Him in His work in the world and in the lives of the people around us."],["Heart of who we are","Diversity in community, being real but with a present hope in the gospel of grace"],["Heart of who we are","We are a down-to-earth set of scrappy humans who seek to love others the way Jesus loves."],["Heart of who we are","Warm, welcoming, friendly, fresh, accepting, caring."],["Heart of who we are","I think it's the people that make Resonate special. We have the most empathetic, warm, come as you are, kind of people."],["Heart of who we are","diversity"],["Heart of who we are","I believe our church has been built on our community building and how we love each other."],["Heart of who we are","We are gentle, tightly bound, and thoughtful in our communities, and bold for the Gospel."],["Heart of who we are","Loving people where they are and pointing the towards Jesus ❤"],["Heart of who we are","Our impact on those around us"],["Heart of who we are","The life groups and the passion to serve those in the surrounding community first that demonstrates the love that God showed to us."],["Heart of who we are","The Word. Jesus. Being Gospel centered in all we do."],["Heart of who we are","I see every Sunday that everyone is there to become a better follower of Christ. Not because of the programs, or sermon, or worship, or music, but genuine desire to become a better follower of Christ. And I think Resonate is doing an amazing job by creating a creative and safe place, but Christ centered way to do it."],["Heart of who we are","A mix of diverse people who love the lord, each other, and our community (with good coffee)."],["Heart of who we are","Resonate is a group of people that care. If I need something I know someone will set aside time to be there. If there is a need in the community, we mobilize quickly to be the hands of Christ, regardless of who is in trouble."],["Heart of who we are","We are a people who fear the LORD, love Jesus, and are empowered by the indwelling of the Holy Spirit. We live out gospel lives for the flourishing of our church family and in our everyday life in whatever context God has placed us."],["Heart of who we are","Christ and community centered. Relationships"],["Heart of who we are","Caring for one another, and centered on Jesus"],["Heart of who we are","See other notes"],["Heart of who we are","A people who see those around us and want to be more like Jesus on the way that we interact"],["Heart of who we are","We love people well, we care about what God says and we try to live it out every day."],["Heart of who we are","Community and caring for one another"],["Heart of who we are","Resonate seeks to love people, see others know Jesus and walk with Jesus, and desires to grow and change as God continues to call us to serve each other, our community, and beyond."],["Heart of who we are","A church rooted in the word and serving others."],["Heart of who we are","down to earth people that don't worship a pastor or culture, but focus on the Word and it's application to their life"],["Heart of who we are","I haven't yet put my finger on that. It's a good question. Not that it has to be answered with something \"unique\" cause then that infers \"branding question\" more than being an ecclesial question."],["Other thoughts","No, just that we love you and hold you all in our hearts and prayers!!"],["Other thoughts","I love resonate!"],["Other thoughts","I love this church :)"],["Other thoughts","Nothing else comes to mind."],["Other thoughts","I am thankful for this church. Praise the Lord in his faithfulness leading us through a difficult time. I pray that the changes to come with grow us in wisdom and knowledge and continue expanding God’s kingdom on earth."],["Other thoughts","My prayer is that we can put away pride and humbly serve as we exalt Jesus, reflecting Him and in so doing be attractive to the community around us"],["Other thoughts","Praying for our staff as you navigate this next challenge. Trusting God will provide the right person for what Resonate needs in this next chapter."],["Other thoughts","Thanks for the opportunity to share!"],["Other thoughts","I appreciate the clear communication and your willingness to listen to my feedback and concerns. I am praying for the leadership team."],["Other thoughts","Thank you for all that you’re doing!!!"],["Other thoughts","Love yall!"],["Other thoughts","I hope Resonate can be a part of future growth in the Christian religion by showing that the faith can adapt to modern-day beliefs and rights."],["Other thoughts","Allergies are lame."],["Other thoughts","I love this church!"],["Other thoughts","I am very thankful of how things happen handled, and I pray that the Lord will continue blessing and guiding elders, executive team, and staff as they navigate this next season."],["Other thoughts","I do want to add how I love our welcoming heart that opens the doors wide to all who want to know Jesus and be transformed by him."],["Other thoughts","I love you!!!! I love this church!!!!"],["Other thoughts","Thanks for putting this together! I think the feedback will be so valuable."],["Other thoughts","Love y’all!"],["Other thoughts","Not at this time."],["Other thoughts","limitless, sometimes"],["Other thoughts","Resonate seems to be led by a lot of type B people. It’s be great to get a few more type A people involved. This could help with communication and programming."],["Other thoughts","Not that would be appropriate for this forum/survey but would more be appropriate to discuss in person with a life group leader or staff member"],["Other thoughts","26. I pray that Resonate remains a place of belonging for those Christ liked, not just those we like."],["Other thoughts","We love you all and thank you for taking the time to read this! Praying over our church as we navigate this together."],["Other thoughts","I’m happy to be a part of resonate and am thankful for the way that our leaders are steering us."],["Other thoughts","Love our church, staff and leaders. Feel incredibly blessed to be apart of such a great group."],["Other thoughts","Thanks for all you do, and for keeping us in the loop! It’s a lot but thankful you all are moving forward prayerfully!"],["Other thoughts","So grateful for this church!!!!!"],["Other thoughts","Thank you for taking the time to conduct this survey! It's a great way of making us feel involved in leadership decision-making."],["Other thoughts","I appreciate the care and intentionality Resonate is making to be transparent and involve the body in everything the leadership is doing and working on."],["Other thoughts","Even though we speak like this, dear friends, we are convinced of better things in your case—the things that have to do with salvation. God is not unjust; he will not forget your work and the love you have shown him as you have helped his people and continue to help them. We want each of you to show this same diligence to the very end, so that what you hope for may be fully realized. - Hebrews 6:9-11"],["Other thoughts","I believe in this church. I believe we can find an amazing hire but I also believe God is too big for one hire to mess everything up. I hope this team will pray about every decision and also not put unnecessary pressure on themselves. The Lord has us in this."],["Other thoughts","Thank you to leadership and prayers for wisdom!"],["Other thoughts","I appreciate the amount of liturgy that we use and more wouldn't be bad."],["Other thoughts","Grateful for all you are doing to thoughtfully move forward towards the future of the church."],["Other thoughts","Thank you so much to the leadership for opening up this survey and process to the members. It shows courage and curiosity. Two things a lot of churches could use more of."]],"themes":[{"id":"s1","cat":"S","rank":1,"n":101,"nSurvey":92,"nQual":9,"max":105,"maxQual":9,"name":"Authentic community and Life Groups","summary":"By a wide margin, this is the most-named strength across every input stream. People described Life Groups as the primary reason they stay at Resonate, and the most consistent driver of spiritual formation, retention, and care. The vocabulary is remarkably consistent across sources: community, welcome, real, seen, genuine, family. Many described long histories of weekly meals together, walking through hard seasons, and showing up during personal crises. Multiple long-tenured partners described a stage-of-life cohort that arrived together — buying houses at the same time, having babies in 2016, navigating foster care together — forging deep friendships across years. A respondent in the closing batch named the lingering after service as the visible marker of this depth: people don't leave when service ends because they are genuinely in each other's lives, not just on Sundays.","pull":["You guys are our people.","It's family.","My church is my life group. That's my expression of church community.","We've eaten dinner with the same people for eight years almost every Friday night.","Trauma bond","I haven't found a group that suffers well like Resonate does.","We've walked through some really difficult things together.","We do community really well. That's basically what got us there in the first place."],"more":[71,76,84,86,91,95,96,104,106,107,108,111,113,118,128,129,130,131,133,134,137,140,144,147,148,149,152,153,156,157,159,160,161,165,166,168,169,173,175,178,182,186,188,200,203,204,206,207,214,216,217,229,230,239,241,247,250,252,256,257,259,262,266,268,807,810,811,813,814,816,819,822,823,825,829,831,836,837,840,841,842,843,847,852,853,856,859,860,861,865,869,871,876,880,884,886,891],"moreTotal":97,"interview":true},{"id":"s2","cat":"S","rank":2,"n":81,"nSurvey":73,"nQual":8,"max":105,"maxQual":9,"name":"Quality teaching (Sunday and Wednesday)","summary":"The body identifies Scripture-anchored teaching as Resonate's clearest measurable strength. Chris's exegetical, Bible-focused teaching style was the most commonly named initial draw to the church. Sunday teaching is credited for theological grounding, willingness to address hard topics (Christian nationalism, systemic racism, greed), and treating congregants as capable of depth. Wednesday night classes — often associated with Sarah's teaching — are repeatedly named as the venue where deeper Bible study happens, complementing Sunday in a more active learning environment. The 2-Year Bible reading plan and the Dwell app integration were specifically named as accessible formation tools. One final-batch respondent captured the breadth: Scripture is a central part of almost everything Resonate does, from worship to kids classes to midweek studies to Life Groups, even Outreach Hour. Word Centered value scored 4.67 of 5 — the highest of three values.","pull":["It's open your Bible to...","Intellectual, Bible-focused teaching.","Exegetical, not topical.","Christian nationalism, systemic racism, greed — willing to name those things.","Wednesday nights are awesome — there's something super sweet and familial about gathering around the Word.","The Judges study was so good.","Nerdy classroom stuff and less feel-goody sermons.","We continue to be really exegetical.","I appreciate the academic side of it."],"more":[73,78,80,92,93,99,100,102,103,104,106,108,110,113,116,123,124,127,132,134,139,149,153,154,155,157,160,163,164,199,207,212,216,218,232,248,253,255,265,803,804,808,817,844,848,881,893,894,0,2,3,5,6,8,11,12,18,19,21,22,23,25,26,28,29,31,32,34,35,36,38,39,43,44,48,49,55,56,60,61,63,64,65],"moreTotal":83,"interview":true},{"id":"s3","cat":"S","rank":3,"n":40,"nSurvey":34,"nQual":6,"max":105,"maxQual":9,"name":"Everybody plays / empowered ownership","summary":"Multiple voices used the exact phrase 'everybody plays.' This is described both as a core value and as a lived reality — high volunteer-to-partner ratio, new attenders quickly invited to serve, lay leadership doing significant work, and a sense that the church functions because people show up. One respondent observed that he could not think of any partners who don't serve in some way, and a closing-batch overseas partner echoed this: she can look around on Sunday and tell you the area each individual or family serves in. This same theme also appears as a weakness ('not everybody plays anymore'), reflecting a tension between stated value and current execution.","pull":["Everybody plays.","Empowered ownership.","If you're a partner at Resonate, you're hosting people on Sunday, you're not just coming.","I was relatively new and they asked me to welcome people. I thought that was so cool.","We don't have a hired bishop coming in. It's by the people, for the people, with the help of God.","Volunteer-to-partner ratio is incredible."],"more":[108,2,5,14,15,20,24,29,30,39,43,46,52,55,60,63,66],"moreTotal":17,"interview":true},{"id":"s4","cat":"S","rank":4,"n":37,"nSurvey":32,"nQual":5,"max":105,"maxQual":9,"name":"Authentic, not over-produced culture","summary":"People describe Resonate as organic, grassroots, and authentic — not a 'Sunday show,' not a megachurch, not flashy. Several contrast it explicitly with previous experiences in larger or more produced churches. Survey respondents named the same idea using words like genuine, real, down-to-earth, unpretentious, and humble. One respondent put it directly: she came from a megachurch where she often felt like a number, and Resonate's small, intentional community was the opposite.","pull":["Cog in a wheel of a massive machine","Down-to-earth and authentic.","Small, not pompous, grassroots.","You wouldn't go to Resonate for fun or for status. You go because you want to follow Jesus.","Not a Sunday show.","Not bringing a clown every Sunday."],"more":[70,76,81,85,88,91,92,95,110,126,127,133,140,151,153,164,171,208,210,214,223,232,264,807,821,822,830,845,847,849,852,857,858,869,871,872,882,894],"moreTotal":38,"interview":true},{"id":"s5","cat":"S","rank":5,"n":36,"nSurvey":29,"nQual":7,"max":105,"maxQual":9,"name":"Center-set theology and room for nuance","summary":"People named Resonate's willingness to hold gray areas, allow disagreement on secondary and tertiary issues, and welcome people working through doubt or questions. The phrase 'center-set church' was used by multiple participants. Several specifically named that Resonate gave them room to work through doubt without feeling shame — a formation catalyst, not just a cultural value. One long-tenured partner described their core convictions having 'grown up alongside Resonate' through 15 years of permission to question.","pull":["Center-set church — Jesus at the center.","Room for nuance.","It's okay to ask questions and have doubts.","Permission to work through things instead of feeling shame.","I had so much baggage and I didn't want to feel like an imposter or unwelcomed as I worked through it.","There are some certain truths we hold, but we want to talk about the gray area and provide room for respectful, compassionate disagreement.","I grew up in a legalistic black-and-white culture. Recognizing that we can question things that may have been more rigid in the past."],"more":[74,87,105,114,116,127,132,165,214,217,817,844,848,26],"moreTotal":14,"interview":true},{"id":"s6","cat":"S","rank":6,"n":32,"nSurvey":27,"nQual":5,"max":105,"maxQual":9,"name":"Belvedere Park engagement under Noah's leadership","summary":"Noah's leadership in Belvedere Park engagement is repeatedly named as a bright spot — an area where the church is now seeing fruit after years of struggle. Outreach Hour was specifically named as a sign of community trust, having grown from around a dozen participants to 40-50. Serve Sunday and the Outreach Hour Bible study were named together as practical expressions of the church's heart for the neighborhood. Closing-batch respondents continued the pattern: one named the Monday outreach lunch as the moment that captures why she stays, another named connecting with the community around us as a clear strength, and a likely future elder named outreach as one of the church's clearest current strengths.","pull":["Outreach Hour has grown from a dozen participants to 40-50.","Locals come back for Sunday services.","It also shows that the authentic community in our neighborhood is starting to trust us, because we're investing in them.","Noah has done such a great job — we needed somebody to really own it and run with it.","I'd call it a strength now after years of struggle.","What we do right now is really beautiful."],"more":[71,77,86,87,94,102,108,112,115,124,130,135,138,149,151,179,184,219,231,240,852,5,7,31,40,49],"moreTotal":26,"interview":true},{"id":"s7","cat":"S","rank":7,"n":25,"nSurvey":20,"nQual":5,"max":105,"maxQual":9,"name":"Care during personal crisis","summary":"People consistently cite the body's responsiveness to tangible needs — meal trains, prayer, showing up in hospital rooms, accommodating disability, walking through grief. Multiple sources shared specific stories of the church showing up during medical, family, or foster-care crises, often with care that lasted months or years. This is named as a defining cultural marker, not just a personal experience.","pull":["Trauma bond.","We had people we knew, people we didn't know, praying, coming to visit, bringing food.","We had people from the church living with us for seven and a half years.","People came out of the woodwork to support us, to care for us.","We got meals weekly for like two years from people while we were waiting.","Watching people suffer well.","I can look over the people that I'm there with and I know some of the hard parts of their story… and they are standing here singing with their hands raised high."],"more":[75,88,149,166,169,186,200,203,230,237,241,243,247,255,819,49],"moreTotal":16,"interview":true},{"id":"s8","cat":"S","rank":8,"n":24,"nSurvey":18,"nQual":6,"max":105,"maxQual":9,"name":"Healthy church culture","summary":"People describe Resonate's culture as relational, safe-to-fail, gracious, non-critical, and stable. Multiple staff came from prior unhealthy churches and identified Resonate as a healing place. The body responded to recent crisis — the lead pastor's departure — with maturity rather than chaos. Survey respondents repeatedly named the transparency, humility, and steadiness shown by elders and the leadership team in the wake of the transition. For many, this season has been credibility-building rather than credibility-eroding.","pull":["Healthiest church culture I've been in.","Safe to fail.","It's okay to fail as long as we're trying to move forward.","Gracious, non-critical, trusting.","I expected the partner Slack to blow up. Instead Hallie texted 'we need to fast and pray.'","Praise God for mature people.","More healthiness than negativeness."],"more":[85,86,92,110,126,164,200,220,225,228,232,264,845,862,26,56],"moreTotal":16,"interview":true},{"id":"s9","cat":"S","rank":9,"n":24,"nSurvey":20,"nQual":4,"max":105,"maxQual":9,"name":"Children's and youth ministry","summary":"Several voices named children's ministry as a genuine strength, with specific reference to the elementary Bible study under Noah, the whole-Bible kids curriculum, and the experience of children growing up in a consistent peer group at the church. Youth ministry is also named as treating teens with maturity and creating space for teens to bring their friends. One final-batch respondent named Wednesday nights specifically as the venue where every member of his family wants to be — from adults in Bible study, to older kids at Shout, to a five-year-old in childcare who calls it 'night church.'","pull":["These are people that NAME in particular has grown up with, like, since he was a baby, literally.","With the addition of Noah (Shout) and the elementary Bible study and the focus on that and how many kids there are at Resonate, that whole thing just is really amazing.","Whole-Bible kids curriculum, including hard books like Philemon.","Youth ministry treats teens with maturity, and teens bring friends."],"more":[87,124,130,139,177,183,188,216,253,824,125,55],"moreTotal":12,"interview":true},{"id":"s10","cat":"S","rank":10,"n":17,"nSurvey":12,"nQual":5,"max":105,"maxQual":9,"name":"Sarah's leadership and teaching","summary":"Sarah's role as a teaching woman in leadership is named repeatedly as both a draw and a formative presence — particularly for women coming from churches where women's teaching was shallow, absent, or limited. Her training of women through the Charles Simeon Trust, mission trips with Women in the Window, and her example of charismatic prayer and faith are specifically named.","pull":["Just having somebody who is a woman who is solid — solid in her theology, solid in her biblical knowledge, solid in her gifting to disciple and develop leaders has just been invaluable.","Charles Simeon Trust training was huge for a lot of the women.","Mission trips with Sarah — that's how a lot of spirituality has multiplied within individuals.","Modeling from Sarah and women leaders.","She's solid in her theology.","Sarah's note earlier this week about praying for her eyes to see the opportunity. It's like, yeah, duh. Why don't we do that more often?"],"more":[156,180,185,195,197,202],"moreTotal":6,"interview":true},{"id":"s11","cat":"S","rank":11,"n":17,"nSurvey":16,"nQual":1,"max":105,"maxQual":9,"name":"Global missions support","summary":"Survey respondents who name this theme cite Resonate's support for global missionaries, the visibility of missionary partnerships, and the ordinariness of the people sent. The smaller-than-expected count suggests this is a real strength among those who notice it but is not as broadly visible to the body as other strengths in this list.","pull":[],"more":[71,86,94,112,115,122,145,151,157,235,13],"moreTotal":11,"interview":false},{"id":"s12","cat":"S","rank":12,"n":11,"nSurvey":11,"nQual":0,"max":105,"maxQual":9,"name":"Foster, adoption, and disability inclusion","summary":"The body's commitment to foster care, adoption, and welcoming children with disabilities is named as a distinguishing mark of Resonate. Several respondents say it shaped their decision to stay, and others say it modeled what it looks like for the body to live out the gospel in concrete ways. This theme did not surface in the qualitative conversations but appears strongly in the survey — likely because the qualitative discussions focused on structural questions while survey respondents had space to name distinctive cultural markers.","pull":[],"more":[75,91,109,113,121,127,130,138,140,169,852],"moreTotal":11,"interview":false},{"id":"w1","cat":"W","rank":1,"n":49,"nSurvey":43,"nQual":6,"max":105,"maxQual":9,"name":"Life Groups need restructuring — vision, multiplication, geography, exclusivity, and accessibility","summary":"Life Groups are simultaneously the most-named strength and the most-named weakness. Sources who critique Life Groups largely affirm them in principle. The critique is structural, and it shows up across multiple connected dimensions.\n\nVision and purpose ambiguity. Multiple sources named that the body lacks shared clarity on what a Life Group is for. Is it discipleship? A peer-led friend group? A neighborhood mission unit? A long-term covenant friendship?\n\nMultiplication has stopped. Several voices named that the original expectation of group multiplication has quietly faded. Most groups are now closed.\n\nGeography drift. Groups described as 'location specific' have become organized by life stage and friendship rather than physical proximity.\n\nExclusivity at scale. Long-running groups feel closed to new attenders. Multiple sources named people unable to join because every group nearby is full. People gravitate toward their life groups in ways that can make new and timid people feel unwelcome.\n\nAccessibility for new attenders. Newer attendees describe long onboarding curves before relationships form, and the jump from new attender to Life Group as too big.\n\nContent and leader pipeline. A handful of sources critiqued variation in curriculum quality and called for structured training and accountability for Life Group leaders.","pull":["There's no desire to multiply new life groups. The goal of life group, most people think, is to be with the same people for eight years.","Most of our groups are closed. We're never going to reach more people that way.","Was multiplication still the goal? We haven't heard that messaging in years.","Sarah's group may never multiply.","I don't expect all of our groups to multiply, but, hey, there's people on the outside of that.","When everyone in a life group starts having kids, and more people move in... when you get so big it's hard to have those intimate relationships with people, when there's like 30 people showing up.","Sometimes there's a culture of collecting people, but like collecting fun, cool people.","The bigger the church gets, the easier it is to hide in the group.","On Sundays, the primary emphasis seems to be you're drawn to talk to the people that you are in life group with."],"more":[270,273,274,280,281,291,294,299,300,301,305,316,331,338,348,351,352,354,355,363,374,375,393,394,398,404,405,408,17],"moreTotal":29,"interview":true},{"id":"w2","cat":"W","rank":2,"n":36,"nSurvey":30,"nQual":6,"max":105,"maxQual":9,"name":"Lack of formal discipleship and leadership development","summary":"Multiple voices identified that there is no formal discipleship structure, no broader pipeline for raising up leaders, and that part of the issue is people don't share a common definition of what discipleship means. Some imagine a mentor-mentee model. Some consider Life Group to be discipleship. Some say they want a discipling relationship but don't know how to ask or where to go. The discipleship gap and the leadership development gap are closely linked. Survey respondents repeatedly proposed cross-generational mentorship structures. A closing-batch respondent named the women's version directly: she would join a women's discipleship sign-up in a heartbeat. Another named the equipping side: the church doesn't spend enough time equipping the body to live sent. Reinforced by closed-ended data: 17% of survey respondents want a discipling relationship and don't have one; leadership development is the #1 strategic priority at 50%.","pull":["Discipleship is something we could grow in.","I don't feel like I have someone at Resonate who's discipling me, if I were to answer that formally.","If I had to wave a magic wand: hand someone the Transformative Friendships book, do that for 35 weeks, come back and talk.","We say this — disciple-making disciples, equipping — that's language we say every week. But if we don't know what that means...","We need to define discipleship more clearly.","I wonder if that was not Chris's strength — developing leaders.","We need to be building up more men, but certainly more women, to know and embrace their gifting as leaders.","We can't just rely on a team of three elders to disciple."],"more":[273,288,308,312,315,322,348,364,390,405,51],"moreTotal":11,"interview":true},{"id":"w3","cat":"W","rank":3,"n":34,"nSurvey":29,"nQual":5,"max":105,"maxQual":9,"name":"Diversity gaps (ethnic, age, family stage, socioeconomic)","summary":"The staff focus group was nearly unanimous that Resonate is mono-ethnic, mono-family-stage, and lacks generational, socioeconomic, and racial diversity in both body and leadership. Survey respondents reinforced this from many angles: the body is largely young, white, middle-class, married-with-kids; older partners and singles describe feeling on the margins; respondents from mixed families and Black respondents specifically named the gap between Resonate's diversity and the surrounding neighborhood. The aspiration to 'look like our neighborhood' was named as 11 years old without significant movement. One long-tenured partner specifically named the lack of older families with college and high-school-aged kids as a felt absence.","pull":["We've said for the 11 years I've been here that we'd like to look more like Atlanta. We haven't taken a lot of steps in that direction.","We don't have people of color in leadership or as elders.","Hard for 20-something singles to be a part of this church. Hard for elderly people to be a part of this church.","Our partner meetings, the room suddenly is very white.","We are very, like, mono-ethnic and also mono-family-stage.","Socioeconomic status is probably a greater barrier for our church than any kind of racial differences.","We need some older men in the eldership. We need older people on the LT for that generational steadiness and wisdom and experience. We have never had that.","They had older families... people who were a couple of steps ahead of us. That feels like it's missing at Resonate."],"more":[287,296,306,333,342,344,372,377,402,408],"moreTotal":10,"interview":true},{"id":"w4","cat":"W","rank":4,"n":23,"nSurvey":19,"nQual":4,"max":105,"maxQual":9,"name":"'Fuzzy' disagreement culture and unclear positions on women and LGBTQ","summary":"The same gray-area culture named as a strength is also named as a limit — making it hard to challenge sin, push for growth, or expect change. Multiple sources described feeling unable to challenge friends or be challenged in their life groups. New believers are particularly under-served by a '10 options' posture. Survey respondents extended this concern in two specific directions: theological clarity on women in leadership and LGBTQ+ inclusion. Some want Resonate to move toward egalitarianism and a more affirming posture; others want a clearer complementarian line. The shared concern across both views is that the current ambiguity feels unsustainable. A closing-batch respondent who otherwise loves the center-set model named the cost: the church doesn't talk enough about sin, repentance, and the body's deep need for the gospel.","pull":["I can't tell you anything.","Walking on eggshells.","We don't really focus on holiness — what does it actually mean to do the things the Bible says to do?","Every strength has a weakness. We're mature, so we're not flexible. We think we already know everything","Areas where we say one thing and do another thing.","If you're new in your faith, you don't need 10 options. You don't want to know what the 10 options are.","We accept people where they are, but there should be an encouragement to move away from things we shouldn't be doing."],"more":[279,282,286,288,346,347,353,358,364,377,380,399,400],"moreTotal":13,"interview":true},{"id":"w5","cat":"W","rank":5,"n":20,"nSurvey":18,"nQual":2,"max":105,"maxQual":9,"name":"Volunteer participation declining ('not everybody plays anymore')","summary":"The same 'everybody plays' value named as a strength is also named as a current weakness. Multiple voices in the partner focus group noted that the everybody-plays culture is straining as the church grows. Volunteer participation has declined. The expectation isn't communicated clearly. Pathways to sign up are unclear. A few people carry too much burden. The people who name this most clearly are themselves the regular volunteers. A respondent named the pattern from the inside: people frequently call out from their commitments, which overburdens others.","pull":[],"more":[360,402,4,7,11,12,13,46,52,69],"moreTotal":10,"interview":false},{"id":"w6","cat":"W","rank":6,"n":20,"nSurvey":14,"nQual":6,"max":105,"maxQual":9,"name":"Broken eldership pipeline and process","summary":"This is the most frequently named weakness across the qualitative sources, though it surfaces less often in the survey because most respondents don't have visibility into the eldership process. The process is unclear, takes too long (2-3 years for development plus an asked 5-7 year commitment), feels like a burden rather than a joy, and no one has clearly owned the pipeline. Multiple sources noted that men have raised hands to learn more (nine at the recent retreat) — it is not a candidate problem.","pull":["There isn't a clear process for someone going from 'I'm interested' to 'yes, you are a candidate.'","All I have heard in the last few years is how terrible it is to be an elder.","Way too long of a process.","Nine guys signed up at the retreat.","Doesn't feel very stable. Feels a little shifty right now.","I declined eldership two years ago because I didn't get a lot of clarity from Chris around what that expectation was.","We've lost more elders than we've kept."],"more":[298,300,308,343,359,360,385,402],"moreTotal":8,"interview":true},{"id":"w7","cat":"W","rank":7,"n":19,"nSurvey":15,"nQual":4,"max":105,"maxQual":9,"name":"Application gap — equipping people to live out what they learn","summary":"Multiple qualitative voices identified a gap between what is taught from the pulpit and what people know how to do with it in their lives. Resonate is described as strong on equipping the mind, weaker on equipping people to apply teaching, lead life groups, multiply, engage non-Christians, or live out the 'everybody sent' framework. Survey respondents reinforced this: one named the use of prayer rooms during or after service as a concrete way to add personal challenge to apply the Word; another said the body doesn't spend enough time equipping people to live sent; another said she's less sure on how everyone lives out the rich teaching they receive. The closed-ended data reinforces the broader gap: only 8% of respondents actively seek opportunities to share their faith despite 77% reporting comfort with it.","pull":["All of this knowledge and mental ascension. Like, yes, I assent to that with my brain, but how do you live it out in your heart?","We do really good on everybody plays, but the everybody sent is not as good.","We're really good at equipping the mind, but not at equipping leaders to lead life groups, multiply, engage outside the church.","The thing I think we're weakest at is everybody's sent. We don't talk enough about how to be sent.","I'm always looking for that kind of content. And it feels like there's just not a lot of it.","We talk a lot about abiding. Okay, right, so I should abide. What does it look like? What are people doing that looks like abiding?"],"more":[295,297,322,392,405,3,6,8,9,22,34,35,38,42,44,47,60],"moreTotal":17,"interview":true},{"id":"w8","cat":"W","rank":8,"n":18,"nSurvey":16,"nQual":2,"max":105,"maxQual":9,"name":"Building and space limitations","summary":"Several sources named building and space limitations: kids' classrooms are getting full, the courtyard is underused and underdeveloped, the sanctuary has noise issues during worship, and the building is increasingly straining to hold the body that is in it. The courtyard specifically came up as a present limitation and a future opportunity. A small number of survey respondents specifically called for a capital campaign to expand. A closing-batch respondent flagged a specific physical concern that bridges into the Threats section: the wall behind the church has failed in several places.","pull":[],"more":[345,372,479,505,549,563,567,606,671],"moreTotal":9,"interview":false},{"id":"w9","cat":"W","rank":9,"n":14,"nSurvey":10,"nQual":4,"max":105,"maxQual":9,"name":"Lack of structure / governance / systems","summary":"Multiple voices, particularly former elders, named a long-running pattern of inadequate structure — no clear budget process initially, no document tracking, no clarified decision rights, no consistent vision-setting process. One ex-elder described being 'shocked, shocked' at how little structure existed. The lack of structure was frequently linked to Chris's posture toward institutional discipline. Survey respondents named the same pattern from a different angle: initiatives lack clear and consistent communication about the what, why, and when, and follow-through to completion is inconsistent. A respondent who is also an elder candidate observed it from inside leadership: while the church gives an impression of excellence, the longer you're around, you start to notice the church is just figuring things out as it goes.","pull":["I was shocked at how little structure there was. Like nothing.","Wild West after that.","We're still trying to get the document tracking system set up.","Still operates somewhat like the house church it began as in Chris's [Leah's] parents' basement.","There are things at Resonate that don't scale well","It is hard to find partner communication if you are not on Slack","Where do partner documents actually live?","Constant pushback against structure."],"more":[298,312,325,326,327,360,531,582,593,660,726,755,770,59],"moreTotal":14,"interview":true},{"id":"w10","cat":"W","rank":10,"n":13,"nSurvey":10,"nQual":3,"max":105,"maxQual":9,"name":"Growth is mostly church transfers, not new believers","summary":"Several voices observed that the church grows through people transferring in (often from megachurches or rigid prior backgrounds) rather than through evangelism and conversion. One participant could only name a couple of people he knew who came to faith through the church in recent memory. The long baptism liturgy was cited as evidence that adult baptisms are infrequent.","pull":["Most of our people are church transfers.","I think most of our church growth is interrelated with babies and church transfers.","We've seen the numbers of like, what, a few people choose adult baptism.","I'd like to see more people coming to faith because of our ministry and not just coming to our church already a Christian.","We're not really an evangelist church."],"more":[334,365,427,442,446,463,559,625,733],"moreTotal":9,"interview":true},{"id":"w11","cat":"W","rank":11,"n":12,"nSurvey":12,"nQual":0,"max":105,"maxQual":9,"name":"Visitor and new attender experience","summary":"Newer attendees and those who hesitate to take social initiative describe the onboarding experience as harder than it could be. Several respondents specifically named the first 5-7 visits as feeling lonely, difficult to navigate, and dependent on personal invitation. The jump from new attender to Life Group is named as too big — there are not enough on-ramps between visiting on a Sunday and committing to a small group. Others noted that visitors are not always actively welcomed by anyone other than staff. This theme connects directly to the W1 Life Groups weakness on accessibility but is its own concern: it asks whether a first-time visitor can find a way in at all.","pull":[],"more":[294,306,354,363],"moreTotal":4,"interview":false},{"id":"w12","cat":"W","rank":12,"n":8,"nSurvey":5,"nQual":3,"max":105,"maxQual":9,"name":"Saying we want to engage Belvedere Park while unwilling to change for it","summary":"Multiple staff voices named that the church says it wants to be rooted in the neighborhood but has not been willing to change in the ways that real engagement would require — cost barriers, comfort barriers, structural changes, asking different things of long-tenured partners. This is closely related to the diversity gaps weakness but is distinct: it names the willingness-to-sacrifice question rather than the demographic gap itself.","pull":["We say we want to engage in Belvedere, but we don't want change.","If we're going to be here, we better be here.","People don't realize what it would take to actually be rooted in this community.","We're asking people to assimilate, and it's just harder for them to do, and why would they want to?"],"more":[287,40,68],"moreTotal":3,"interview":true},{"id":"o1","cat":"O","rank":1,"n":79,"nSurvey":73,"nQual":6,"max":105,"maxQual":9,"name":"Find the right next lead pastor","summary":"Specific desired qualities surfaced from multiple voices: vision-setting, passion for discipleship and leadership development, multi-ethnic church experience, pastoral warmth, comfort with structure, accessibility as a teacher, heart for new believers, and a long track record of proven character. Closing-batch respondents added several specific notes that align tightly: a pastor who teaches well but is truly a shepherd and a person of integrity; someone mature in faith whose life gives evidence of the Holy Spirit at work; someone comfortable with grey areas but confidently holding essentials, who can listen to different opinions with gentleness and humble patience; an effective, humble, and relational leader who can guide and equip the staff. One respondent reframed the task as finding a leader to lead the way to where Resonate wants to go, not replacing what was lost. Another asked the search committee to find someone who won't come in trying to change a lot, but who will see the good God is doing in Resonate and be excited to join it.","pull":["A pastor with a zeal for missional work and the Antioch model.","Someone with multi-ethnic church leadership — not someone trying to turn us multi-ethnic, but who has been a leader in one.","Strong on discipleship and leadership development.","Visionary, not just a Chris replacement.","Open to a leader whose passion shapes the culture.","Comfortable with structure.","Pastorally warm — not just on stage.","I want someone who was part of an elder-led, disciple-making church and loves leadership development.","A clear search committee process."],"more":[376,480,512,533,561,575,708,709,711,712,713,714,715,716,718,719,721,722,723,725,726,727,728,730,732,733,734,735,736,737,738,739,740,741,742,744,745,747,748,749,751,752,753,754,755,756,757,758,759,760,761,762,763,764,766,767,768,769,770,771,772,774,778,779,780,781,782,783,784,785,787,788,789,791,793,794,796,798,799,800],"moreTotal":80,"interview":true},{"id":"o2","cat":"O","rank":2,"n":58,"nSurvey":53,"nQual":5,"max":105,"maxQual":9,"name":"Deepen Belvedere Park engagement (programs, partnerships, space)","summary":"Deepening engagement with Belvedere Park emerged as the single most-named opportunity. Specific ideas span three connected dimensions. First, programs and outreach — building on Outreach Hour, classes the building could host during the week (ESL, financial literacy, after-school, AA, Celebrate Recovery), and direct ministry to those experiencing homelessness or housing instability (a day shelter partnership, transitional housing, the motel behind the church being purchased and used for sobriety support). One respondent imagined a recurring fair where the homeless can come for haircuts, clothes for job interviews, and financial education. Second, partnerships with other local churches and organizations. A closing-batch respondent named continuing partnerships with organizations around Resonate, including hosting a Perspectives course. Third, developing the building and outdoor space — the courtyard, a kitchen for meals, a community garden, expanded kids' space. One closing-batch respondent imagined outreach so active that the church buys another building in the strip to minister to the community. Closed-ended data: 26% picked deepening Belvedere Park roots as a top-2 strategic priority.","pull":["Investing more deeply in local mission through monthly courtyard events, partnering with other churches, and authentic community presence.","How do we really be good neighbors? Physically where we are.","We have this exterior space, this courtyard that has so much potential.","Outreach Hour and Sunday morning may need to look different — and that's okay.","Partnering with Pastor Will more often to do monthly courtyard events versus just every now and then.","Early Life Groups were more externally focused than today."],"more":[415,417,420,423,424,425,427,428,430,434,438,439,440,443,447,448,454,455,459,460,462,468,474,479,480,482,485,486,489,492,497,502,503,505,508,517,522,523,526,527,532,534,543,547,549,550,552,558,559,563,565,567,568,575,593,598,600,602,603,606,607,608,609],"moreTotal":63,"interview":true},{"id":"o3","cat":"O","rank":3,"n":40,"nSurvey":34,"nQual":6,"max":105,"maxQual":9,"name":"Build formal discipleship and equip people to disciple","summary":"Multiple voices identified discipleship as both a weakness and an opportunity. Several proposed clarifying the definition first, then building equipping pathways. Specific suggestions included a sermon series on discipleship, a Gospel-Centered Life curriculum option for life groups, equipping people to disciple in their existing relationships, and just talking about it more. Several voices specifically warned against adding programs as the primary fix, and instead called for equipping people in existing relationships. The 'everybody sent' piece of the church's framework was named as the weakest part — not because people don't want to be sent, but because they don't know how. Closed-ended data: 27% picked equipping more people to make disciples as a top-2 priority.","pull":["Define discipleship more clearly.","One-on-one discipleship as alternative to group-only model","Maybe a sermon series on discipleship.","A new Life Group curriculum option — Gospel-Centered Life as Gospel 101.","Hand someone the Transformative Friendships book, do that for 35 weeks, come back and talk.","We need to be raising up more people to disciple and to be explicitly training people to do that.","Maybe it's just talking about it more, you know, to start. What does it look like?","Discipleship is in our mission but many of us don't know what that looks like. How how to do it.","Programs kill churches.","We don't talk enough about how to be sent.","We're really good at equipping the mind, but not at equipping leaders to lead life groups, multiply, engage outside the church.","The thing I think we're weakest at is everybody's sent. We don't talk enough about how to be sent.","Practicing writing out your story or telling it in chunks."],"more":[364,390,405,412,427,434,444,462,474,479,485,497,498,518,543,544,554,560,561,572,573,577,593,607,732,733,739,754,764],"moreTotal":29,"interview":true},{"id":"o4","cat":"O","rank":4,"n":26,"nSurvey":25,"nQual":1,"max":105,"maxQual":9,"name":"Strengthen youth and kids ministry","summary":"Resonate's strong young-family demographic creates a leading-indicator opportunity: many of those families now have older kids whose needs the church is not yet structured to serve. Sources specifically name a strong youth program, better middle school transition support, and discipleship pathways that grow with kids as they grow up. One survey respondent described an existing model worth studying — Grace Fellowship Snellville's youth discipleship structure where college students disciple high schoolers, high schoolers disciple middle schoolers, and middle schoolers disciple elementary schoolers.","pull":[],"more":[369,374,390,425,429,444,464,468,470,493,500,501,504,509,518,523,527,572,574,577,583,585,604,607],"moreTotal":24,"interview":false},{"id":"o5","cat":"O","rank":5,"n":25,"nSurvey":22,"nQual":3,"max":105,"maxQual":9,"name":"Plant or send churches","summary":"A clear subset of sources articulated church planting as part of the 10-year vision — multiple plants across Atlanta, with neighborhoods like Clarkston, Stone Mountain, Tucker, and other parts of Atlanta named explicitly. The qualitative framing was specifically about sending — not satellite churches but praying about who Resonate could send to plant their own community. Several sources tie this to a multiplication model for Life Groups. Closing-batch respondents added two specific framings: one suggested encouraging Life Groups to consider what it would look like to join with a few other groups in church planting, and another argued for getting Life Groups more actively missional, preparing them to plant, share, and live life with others to spread the gospel intentionally.","pull":["Praying to see who we could send out to plant a church somewhere else that is their own church community.","There's something beautiful about that I'd like to see.","We could be a sending church.","We need to figure out ways to multiply."],"more":[415,416,417,422,425,427,439,459,462,466,470,475,488,491,493,496,502,520,522,537,543,572,595,782],"moreTotal":24,"interview":true},{"id":"o6","cat":"O","rank":6,"n":16,"nSurvey":12,"nQual":4,"max":105,"maxQual":9,"name":"Build leadership pipeline including women and people of color","summary":"Multiple voices linked the leadership pipeline gap to the diversity gap, naming that any future pipeline would need to specifically raise up women and people of color, not just men. This is the single most-named strategic priority in the closed-ended data. A respondent argued the case directly: if eldership remains male-only, the church needs a comparable developmental pathway for women, because the current alternatives are less visible and less defined. Closing-batch respondents reinforced both dimensions: one wanted a women's discipleship sign-up structure she could join in a heartbeat; another hoped to keep raising up godly men and women who can disciple others; a third dreamed of communities that reflect a number of ages, backgrounds, and cultures. Closed-ended data: 50% picked leadership development as a top-2 priority — the runaway #1.","pull":["We need to be building up more men, but certainly more women.","I'd like to see more diverse elders and staff so that helps people feel like they can be a part of our church.","How will women be a part of leadership beyond the current structure?","Need more women raised as leaders and disciplers."],"more":[380,402,427,480,489,543,768],"moreTotal":7,"interview":true},{"id":"o7","cat":"O","rank":7,"n":16,"nSurvey":11,"nQual":5,"max":105,"maxQual":9,"name":"Fix the eldership pipeline / redesign the elder model","summary":"Beyond identifying the eldership pipeline as a weakness, multiple voices specifically described this as an immediate, fixable opportunity that does not require waiting for a new lead pastor. Two former elders independently sketched a redesign with two distinct tracks: shepherding elders with limited commitment focused on prayer and pastoral care, and executive elders or ministers with broader leadership function.","pull":["Elder governed and leadership led.","Shepherding elders with limited commitments around leading, but bigger commitments around praying, pastoral care, being good examples, service to the church.","Executive elders, or whatever else we call them — that do more of the things that I've done in the past.","Not everyone can do that. PERSON did not want to do what we were doing. He wanted to pastor people.","The eldership thing is not helping. It's hurting. We don't know what to do, but we have to reset it.","We're working on it now."],"more":[385,402,427,486,543,560,564,770],"moreTotal":8,"interview":true},{"id":"o8","cat":"O","rank":8,"n":16,"nSurvey":13,"nQual":3,"max":105,"maxQual":9,"name":"Become a 'people of prayer' / Spirit-empowered emphasis","summary":"Several voices named opportunity for Resonate to grow into a deeper culture of prayer — corporate prayer, prayer for baptisms, prayer for family salvation, prayer for the church's mission. Survey respondents named the same impulse from a slightly different angle: more space for the Holy Spirit's work, charismatic-friendly worship, prophecy, healing, a more emotionally expressive culture alongside the existing intellectual depth. There were signs of momentum in qualitative — a prayer group at the men's retreat and women beginning to gather to pray. Closing-batch respondents extended this in two specific directions: one imagined the courtyard transformed into a sustainable garden and prayer/contemplation space; another asked for prophesy and words for people, and space and mentorship for art and dancing for the Lord.","pull":["I had this vision of Resonate... that we be more people of prayer, but pray for baptisms.","All of our children of the people who attend church here are sort of covered in the grace of Jesus.","There's been this leaning in to the mystery of having a relationship with God.","Lectio Divina, meditation, mindfulness, breath prayers — these aren't just academic, they're heart things.","Inviting the Holy Spirit into all aspects — worship, prayer, daily encouragement.","Press into a little bit of the supernatural and the power of the Holy Spirit.","Turning that back room into a prayer room.","There's a little bit of a movement, some traction"],"more":[371,381,418,449,451,455,489,521,529,554,578,579,596,607,714,756,759,800],"moreTotal":18,"interview":true},{"id":"o9","cat":"O","rank":9,"n":14,"nSurvey":11,"nQual":3,"max":105,"maxQual":9,"name":"Inter-church partnerships and network membership","summary":"Strategic partnership emerged as a top opportunity in the survey, broader than any single program. Survey respondents repeatedly named specific organizations and models worth partnering with. The qualitative streams reinforced this with explicit naming of Ecclesia as a potential network home, and Pastor Will at the Gathering as an existing local partnership worth deepening. Several voices specifically called for Resonate to join a denomination or network for accountability, leadership development resources, and shared identity. One respondent specifically named One Race as a model where pastors of churches gather around racial justice.","pull":["Partnering with other local churches and sharing resources.","Maybe Ecclesia can help us on the structure side.","One Race is one example where pastors of churches get together and come around racial justice.","We've felt siloed because we're non-denominational."],"more":[420,479,548,553,558,560,573,596],"moreTotal":8,"interview":true},{"id":"o10","cat":"O","rank":10,"n":12,"nSurvey":9,"nQual":3,"max":105,"maxQual":9,"name":"Reach unreached and scale evangelism","summary":"Several survey respondents propose specific scaling of evangelism efforts — classes and workshops on sharing the gospel, Alpha groups for skeptics, regular evangelism prompts in Life Groups. One respondent gave a particularly clear strategic frame: Resonate is a church of former fundamentalists glad to be free of legalism, but the body has not yet found its voice for reaching either progressives in Decatur or Black nominal Christians. The qualitative 'mature to multiplying' reframe captures the same idea.","pull":[],"more":[365,432,442,446,559,575,733],"moreTotal":7,"interview":false},{"id":"o11","cat":"O","rank":11,"n":12,"nSurvey":9,"nQual":3,"max":105,"maxQual":9,"name":"Build structural / governance clarity","summary":"Multiple voices, particularly the two former elders, called for clearer governance structure — codified decision-making rights, clearer org chart, distinction between operational and theological decisions, and a vision-setting discipline that has consistent follow-through. A respondent who is also an elder candidate added a structural proposal: additional staff leadership roles, including an executive pastor and full-time roles for outreach and youth discipleship, alongside a larger and more diverse team of leaders. A closing-batch respondent named the staff dimension specifically: hoping to see stability among leadership and growth in the church staff.","pull":["Codify what's already happening: elder governed, leadership led.","We need a consistent process for setting vision and checking ourselves against that vision as we go.","Pastoral team / minister role as parallel to elder.","Decision-making rights need codification.","Generational diversity needed in eldership and LT."],"more":[364,402,582,588,726,770,791],"moreTotal":7,"interview":true},{"id":"o12","cat":"O","rank":12,"n":10,"nSurvey":7,"nQual":3,"max":105,"maxQual":9,"name":"Multiply Life Groups (and clarify expectation)","summary":"Multiple voices named the need to return to a multiplication expectation for Life Groups, with a goal of one to two new groups per year. Several voices distinguished this from 'all groups must multiply' — some long-tenured groups may not, but others need to.","pull":["If I have one or two every year that multiply, that's a lot of space opening up.","Was multiplication still the goal?","Multiplying believers, multiplying life groups, and then multiplying a new church.","We need to figure out ways."],"more":[427,474,522,525,542,543,733],"moreTotal":7,"interview":true},{"id":"o13","cat":"O","rank":13,"n":6,"nSurvey":6,"nQual":0,"max":105,"maxQual":9,"name":"Tension: stay small versus grow outward","summary":"Six survey respondents explicitly want Resonate to deepen rather than expand, to remain mid-sized and relational, and to avoid the move toward megachurch metrics. This view is small but persistent and represents real concern that growth itself could erode the qualities that define the body. The planning committee should surface this view honestly as it discerns the 10-year vision — not because the data points toward staying small, but because honest discernment requires hearing both directions.","pull":[],"more":[414,439,452,481,495,502,503],"moreTotal":7,"interview":false},{"id":"t1","cat":"T","rank":1,"n":26,"nSurvey":26,"nQual":0,"max":96,"maxQual":0,"name":"Economic pressure and giving capacity","summary":"Respondents express concern about inflation, job loss, possible AI displacement, the slashing of helping programs and federal funding, and the elimination of nonprofit tax exemptions. The cumulative concern is whether partner giving capacity holds, whether the church's core financial base remains stable, and how the church responds if economic strain becomes pastoral need. A final-batch respondent named a specific scenario: continued political and economic factors could affect giving down the road, and even rising fuel prices could change how far people are willing to travel to be at Resonate.","pull":[],"more":[613,615,620,624,631,635,645,649,652,653,668,672,678,683,684,689,700,704,705,647],"moreTotal":20,"interview":false},{"id":"t2","cat":"T","rank":2,"n":18,"nSurvey":18,"nQual":0,"max":96,"maxQual":0,"name":"Political and cultural polarization","summary":"Respondents raising this concern split: some worry that Christian nationalism or partisan capture will mislabel Resonate's Jesus-centered message as 'woke' and erode credibility. Others worry the church will uncritically import progressive ideology and lose its scriptural anchor. The shared instinct across both views is that the church should identify with Scripture rather than with either political side.","pull":[],"more":[677,616,618,626,628,632,633,636,661,666,669,686,688,698,695],"moreTotal":15,"interview":false},{"id":"t3","cat":"T","rank":3,"n":16,"nSurvey":16,"nQual":0,"max":96,"maxQual":0,"name":"Belvedere Plaza decline and gentrification","summary":"The empty Kroger, evening safety concerns, and broader gentrification of the Belvedere Park area are named repeatedly. The threat has two dimensions: the immediate concern about whether the building stays usable at night, and the longer-term concern that the neighborhood Resonate is committed to may be priced out before the church builds genuine bridges to it. A final-batch respondent posed it as a question: what if the rest of the stores in Belvedere Plaza eventually closed too, and the area became an abandoned shopping center?","pull":[],"more":[635,623,634,640,644,648,650,658,665,671,685,690,696,703,706,676,681],"moreTotal":17,"interview":false},{"id":"t4","cat":"T","rank":4,"n":16,"nSurvey":16,"nQual":0,"max":96,"maxQual":0,"name":"The pastor search itself as a risk","summary":"Several respondents name the pastor search as the single largest looming threat — choosing wrong, choosing someone whose culture doesn't fit, losing partners over the choice. Three sub-concerns recur: an outsider who doesn't understand Resonate's culture taking over, a 'Chris 2.0' versus an over-correction in the opposite direction, and the risk that whoever is hired ends up shifting the church's DNA.","pull":[],"more":[619,659,612,622,629,630,641,646,667,692,693,694,699,702,654],"moreTotal":15,"interview":false},{"id":"t5","cat":"T","rank":5,"n":7,"nSurvey":7,"nQual":0,"max":96,"maxQual":0,"name":"Atlanta's transience","summary":"Atlanta is a transient city. Respondents describe key partners and leaders moving away, college students leaving after graduation, and the difficulty of building durable community in a population that turns over. A final-batch respondent named it plainly as a hindrance to the kind of long-term relationships Resonate is built around.","pull":[],"more":[663,662,621,679,614,637,670],"moreTotal":7,"interview":false},{"id":"t6","cat":"T","rank":6,"n":6,"nSurvey":6,"nQual":0,"max":96,"maxQual":0,"name":"Apathy and cultural drift away from local church","summary":"Several respondents named the broader cultural drift away from local church involvement as a threat. As the world becomes less religious and more polarized, the question is whether Resonate will continue to be a place where people show up at all.","pull":[],"more":[691,701,625,656,697,660],"moreTotal":6,"interview":false}]};